      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0002" n="art0002">
        <head>FOOD THAT DOES.</head>
        <p>THE FOOD THAT DOES.</p>
        <p>Scientifically-thought out Food Bererage which has Saved Thousands from Disease and Danger.</p>
        <p>Thousands of men end women, after reading the particulars published respecting the merits of VI-Cocoa have bees induced to become regular drinkers of this popular food beverage. But many have said to them- selves, " I really must use Vi-Cocoa," and have tailed to do so. Why ? There is no more agreeable and inexpensive cocoa on the market. A packet or a lid. or Is. bd tin is value indeed, for Vi-Cocoa does all -that it is claimed to do and there ia 113 so-called , concentrated or foreign cocoa in the world to equal it. It is liigily nutritious, imparts stamina to the system, is refreshing to the weary brain-worker, ar.d recuperates the body after physical fatigue. Do sot ask your grocer for cocoa. Ask for</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0003" n="art0003">
        <head>Wocoa</head>
        <p>Wocoa</p>
        <p>—lt makes all the difference. Every grocer sells Vi-Cocoa in GI. packets and 9d And Is. thl. tins.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0004" n="art0004">
        <head>FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</head>
        <p>FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</p>
        <p>By Philip Snowden, M P. SOME THOUGHTS ON LABOUR TROUBLES.</p>
        <p>There are a good many things in connec- tion with the recent and present strikes which afford material for very serious and interesting reflection. This epidemic of disputes did not begin with the Seamen's strike last June. Some of the features which have characterised these troubles were present in certain strikes and lock-outs which had previously taken place. For two or three years there has been great unrest in the Welsh coalfield which has expressed itself most prominently in divisions among the leaders and attacks upon the Executive by the men. There were similar troubles in Durham and Northumberland, where the men threw over the agreements which had been made by the officials on their behalf. The strike of the North-Eastern railway men, and the dour resistance of the Boilermakers of the terms their leaders desired them to accept gave enormous encouragement to the rebellion against the old method of peaceful negotiations. YOUNG MEN IN A HURRY.</p>
        <p>The younger men in the trade unions are now taking a much more prominent part in the work of the organisation than was formerly the case. From one point of view this is most gratifying, but it has its disad- vantages. In a democratically governed organisation, where a decision is arrived at by the votes of the members, the vote of the youngest and most inexperienced carries as much weight as that of the oldest and wisest. It is gratifying that young men should begin at an early age to take a share in directing their associations, but it is necessary that they should realise that some experience and knowledge is necessary to direct these affairs with wisdom and success, and that the latter are not always found to be the accompani- ment of youth and enthusiasm. Young men as a rule are naturally impulsive, hopeful and revolutionary. They have not lived sufficiently long to have their hopes and ambitions subdued by the disappointments which always come soon or late to those who think that the walls of the city of privelege will fall if the trumpet blast be sufficiently loud. AN ATTRACTIVE ROAD.</p>
        <p>The way to the millenium through revolu- tion is an attractive one. It promises excite- ment and incident; and it is so much easier to get to the goal by one glorious and dramatic stroke than by the hard, long, and tedious road of slow and gradual reform. And it must be admitted that revolutions have sometimes succeeded, but that has only been in cases where the people had been prepared by long agitation or development for the change, the revolution in that case being no more than the act of force to give birth to the new condition which was ready to come forth. The recent strikes are no doubt due to some extent to a belief that revolutionary methods must be adopted to get better conditions for the workers, using the -word revolutionary to describe methods which, though neither criminal nor destruc- tive, are not political of conciliatory. The success of recent strikes has been extraordinary. The strikers have had no means behind them. They have had no strong union to support them. They have, in most cases, belonged to the class of workers who live from hand to mouth, and whose poverty is so extreme that the loss of a day's wage smds them to the pawnshop or brings them to starvation. Yet in spite of these disadvantages they have won con- cessions far g:eater than those for which large and wealthy trade unions have waged long and costly strikes without success. It is interesting to try to find out why the men have been so remarkably successful. Pro- bably there are many reasons, but the main one is that the conditions were unusually ' favourable for success. The season of the year was to the advantage of the men from all points of view, and equally to the dis- advantage of the employers. The state of trade is very good, and it is not only good but very profitable. The employers have given way because it paid them better to do so than to continue the fight. They have given way because they could well afford to do so.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0005" n="art0005">
        <head>SOME APPALLING FACTS.</head>
        <p>SOME APPALLING FACTS.</p>
        <p>mosi</p>
        <p>The strikers have brought to the know- ledge of the public some very appallinr facts about the wages for which able-bodied, full- grown men work in occupations of a most arduous nature, and on these wages have to exist in our large towns and cities. The strike of labourers in Manchester has been for a wage of 20s. a week ! It is unthink- able that men with themselves and wives and families to maintain should have to work in foundries and other places where the work is hard and exhausting for such a wage as that. Nay, the position has been worse than that. The pound a week is the ideal of a living wage to which these men have been casting their eyes and for which they have come out on strike. Their wages hitherto have been 18s. Eighteen shillings a week for more than fifty hours of the hardest physical labour, and on this to keep a home in a town like Manchester. Why, when one learns of facts like these, the wonder is not that the men have come out on strike for something more, but that it should ever have been possible to get any self-respecting men to submit to work for such miserable remuneration. If an industry cannot afford to pay its workmen better wages than these, then it ought not to be allowed to be carried on. But the readiness with which the employers have conceded advances shows that the industry can afford better wages; and better wages can be got when the men muster up sufficient courage.</p>
        <p>_....l_____</p>
        <p>to demand them and make a united stand to get them. AN INTOLERABLE STRUGGLE. Although the workers who have been striking may not be ocinscious of it to any great extent, the present discontent is partly due to the unsatisfied desire, to enjoy a higher standard of living. The felt wants of the working classes have been increasing greatly in recent years, and the means to satisfy these wants have not been forth- coming. There has been a rise in the standard of housing, and working men are now paying much more in rent than formerly : in most cases it may be true because they desire more and better accommodation than formerly satisfied them. There is more desire to see the country, and something is wanted for holiday making. Children are more expensive to rear because parents have a fuller sense of their duties and responsi- bilities in regard to their children. In a hundred ways the desires of the workers have been increasing, but there has been no increase in their wages to meet these new wants. The struggle to Hie has thus become most intense, and these strikes are outbreaks of natural and justifiable discontent on the part of workers to whom the struggle for a living has become intolerable. And yet after all how modest their demands are; bow little will satisfy their discontent. Their ideal is a pound a week ! But if their immediate demands are satisfied perhaps new desires will assert themselves, which cannot be satisfied on a pound a week. STANDING TOGETHER.</p>
        <p>The way in which the allied &amp;ranches of labour have stood together in these strikes has been most gratifying, and in fact very unusual. It has been a reproach against the skilled workmen in the past that they have been too aristocratic to make common cause with the labourer. But there is a growing sense of solidarity among all classes of work- men, and in the growth of this spirit lies the hope of labour. Never before has the advan- tage of the sympathetic support of allied workmen been so clearly demonstrated. The different classes of workmen in the transport trades in London, for instance, would not have gained the substantial advances they have got if they had not all stood out till the last trade had been satisfied. The Manifesto issued by the men's leaders is justified in claiming that the few days' experience of the strike has brought home to people in a very " striking " way the fact that labour and the working man is the basis of our social organisation. From this sense of solidarity much may come. It can exert itself with greater effect in other ways than in strikes. The two things which are needed are, first, the just and intelligent desire among the workers for better conditions; and second, the knowledge that it is only by all classes of workers making common cause that success can be secured. Given that knowledge, and all that the working classes may in reason and justice demand they can obtain without the necessity of strikes.</p>
      </div>
