      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <head>MUMUNGTOR BOARD OF</head>
        <p>MUMUNGTOR BOARD OF</p>
        <p>GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>A Deputation Question. The fortnightly mooting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held at the Workhouse on Monday,Mr G Mather presiding. There were also present :—Miss Broadbent, Mrs Simpson, Rev A 11l Mitchell, Messrs E I. Bent, A J Jolley, W Gill, A Brooks, W Houghton, W Brooks, W Furness, J A Hughes. J A Rushworth, M W Seton, W Flanagan, A Bottomley (clerk), It W Peacock (assistant clerk), J F.Awbery (master).</p>
        <p>THE MASTER'S LEAVE. The House Committee recommended that Mr. Todd, , assistant master, and Miss Bigginauperintendent nurse, 'be appointed to act as master and matron during the temporary absence of Mr. and Mrs. Awbery on annual leave. The Rev. A. M. MITCHELL said that having regard to the disturbed state of these ports and the almost certain increase of the number of inmates, it appeared rather undesirable for the master be absent from hie post at the present time. It was not stated when the mister proposed to take his holiday. He (Mr. Mitchell) had no lack of confidence in Mr. Todd but it was not fair to place an assistant master in charge under such condi- tions. Mr. Todd did not receive any honorarium for the extra service. It would be much better if the master and metros were granted three weeks' leave of absence, the dates to he specified. All the officers inside and outside the House ought to be subject to the same rules and regulation. The Board should know definitely when holidays were to be taken, Mr. Mitchell moved an amendment on the point, but there was no seconder and the minutes were approved</p>
        <p>STRIKE EFFECTS. Coning to the strikes in Liverpool and other places end the consequent dislocation of traffic moused thereby, the Master of the Warrington Workhouse was authorized by the House Committee, if necessity arose, to obtain supplies of food stuffs for the Workhouse. The Guardians also authorised the superintendent of he Cottage Homes to purchase food stuffs as he thought tit.</p>
        <p>TIIE TARIFF PUNIC. At a meeting of the Cottage Flumes C immittee, it was recommended that the best thanks of the Guardians be conveyed to the Executive Committee of the Women's Tariff Reform League for their kindness in taking the children over eight years of age for an outing to Frodaham. na. TAYLOR'S SALARY. At a meeting of the Finance Committee, the members approved the redommendation of the House Visiting Committee to incroase the salary of Dr. Thomas Taylor, resident medical officer, to El OD per annum. A DEPUTATION. Mr. JOLLEY moved that the Chairman, Vice-Chairman, Mr. Flanagan and the Clerk be appointed a deputation to attend the North-Western Poor Law Conference to be held in Chester on September 20th and 30th.</p>
        <p>oaio Mr. SCION seconded. The Rev. A. M. MITCHELL thought it absurd to send four members on each a deputation. The Chairmanned the Clerk would be suf2:ieut. What purpose would it serve to send four? Cheater was nit far sway and why should not the ladies have the opportunity of attending? Mr. RUSHWORTH : They can, if they like. The Rev. A. M. MITCHELL said it would be possible to attend Oa conference and return home each night. He supposed, however, that the four gentlemen selected would spent the nightie Chen's,. He hoped they would enjoy themselves. ((Aught •.) Taey ought not to ' receive more than 124. Bd. per day, including third- class railway fare. It would be much better if the deputation were considered a day deputation, end the members returned horns on Friday night and travelled beck again on Sate day. Mr. BENT (vice-chairmen) said be would be pleased to give way if one of the ladies wished to attend. He did not think Mr. Mitchell ought to state thlt the daputa- tion would stay in Cheater all night. It was not his (Mr. Beat's) intention to do so. An amendment by the Rev. A. Mi. MITCHELL was not seconded and the original proposition wan :approved. BEV. A. M. MITCHELL AND VACCINATION. The CLERK informed the Board that be had prepared certain returns on vacci oration, at the revest of the Local Government Bear... The Rev. A. M. nitwit:N.,. tasked for the details to be placed before the Board. The CLERK said he would submit them if the Board desired. Mr. A. BROOKS said Mr. Mitchell could see the returns at the clerk's dace. The inforrentios would probably not interest the general body of members. The Rev. A. M. MITCHELL said the members ought to take an interest in such an important question. The CHAIRNIAN said the members of thnt Board were full-grown men and women, outlawed with a certain amount of wisdom and commonsense. He did not think for a moment they would take any dietetic's from one member in !aspect to what they teat or ought not to do. Mr A BROOKS said that if the Clerk found that other members, besides Mr. Mitchell, were asking for k information on the suhject be could supply the report , to the Board. .</p>
        <p>rne CLIRRK : I &amp;All be pleased tn supply it. M. EIOUGHTON : Toe Clerk is always courteous and kind to any member of the Board. The Rev. A. M. Mt rcitatt. : I think ho ought to be in a positioo to answer coestiona put to him with regird to vaccination la the Coion. The matter then dropped.</p>
        <p>RE WARKABLE DE 1. TH BY LIGHTNINU. During a heavy thunderstorm at Burslern a ten-year- old boy named Reginald Wright was killed by lightning. The child was ciossiog a piece of waste land, dragging behind him, by means of a length of wire, a small tin box. The lightning was apparently attracted by the metal, and it struck the lad on the right siie of the body, killing him almost instantly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH. Garden Society's Summer Show.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH. Garden Society's Summer Show.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath may fairly be described as a garden village, the inhabitants vieing w th each other to lay out their gardens to the best ad, antaga. This friendly rivalry is admirably stimulated by the Stockton Heath Garden Society, which held its tenth annual summe4 show amid the pleasant surroundings of Wilderspool Park on Saturday. The day was one of perfect summer radiance—if anything, the sun's rays were too strong and the ext.ibition attracted many people from Warrington, as well as the residents of the village. The long spell of drought and sunshine has bad a more or less digastruus affect on flower shows in all parts of the country, and the St ckt m Heath Society suffered by it, the entries falling below last year's. On the score of quality, however, there was litt!e cause for complaint. The exhibits were tastefully staged in two large marquees and presented a bright picture of colour and beauty. The varied and delicate hues of the sweet peas, of which there was an exceptionally fine collection, were the first to attract attention. The handsome silver bowl presented by Lady Greenhill for competition in this class was carried off by Mr. F. Broady, for the second year in succession. His exhibit was a splendid example of c,reful cultivation and artistic arrange- ment. The special Coronation prize for sweet peas was won by Mr. Baker. There was poor competition in the rose classes, but the flowers shown reached a very creditable standard. The silver rose bowl pre- sented by Messrs. Eustance and Co, Ltd., was won for the second time in succession by Mr. J. Blinston. Miss Hallowa was again awarded the epergne offered for the most effective table decorations. The carnations were exceptionally good in quality but numerically the entries were disappointing. The cut flowers were notable for the richness and variety of the blooms .„</p>
        <p>, introduced, but in some instances competitors spoiled the effect of their effo•te by attempting to compress too many specimens into on, bunch. The junior section of the society is meeting with er.ciuraging success, a good display of wild flowers testifying to the keen interest which has been aroused. With regard to the vegetable classes, onions were above the avr rage and beetroots provided a really excellent competitim. Potat es were also good. Several features in the non-competitive part of the show attracted much interest. Alderman Bolton sent a beautiful array of orchids, accompanied by a series of water colour paintings of these wonderful flowers. A famtus 1.500 orchid grown by the alderman was depicted in that way. Messrs. Ker and Sons, of Aigburth, showed choice herbaceous p'ants and Mr. G. Tandy, of Latchford, sent some liliums which</p>
        <p>_ . 4 1 • vio , ---, -- • , , 4 ••• • t 1 , ** l'!-''..• gr • ) 'N tg ... / -;0 • , ,- - ' . t 411,1 f ' 'l. --, • , ( , - 7 ' • li .. , _ -' _ :,„ 'f - "'• c - _ - - i</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>MRS. LEGH</head>
        <p>MRS. LEGH</p>
        <p>were greatly admitted. Mr. G. A. Dunlop, assistant curator at the Warrington Museum, bad arranged an instructive collection of over 100 named varieties of the wild flowers of the district. Great interest was aroused by Mr. Ward's (Lower Walton) exhibit of the busy bee at work. Mr. Ryan, a member of the society, bad some lovely roses on view. During the afternoon the society's orchestra, con- ducted by Mr. W. E. Ray, submitted a pleasing pro- gramme of selections, and in the evening they played for dancing. A smart display of drill, exercises, Ac., was given by a contingent of Boy Scouts, the troops represented being the let Warrington, in charge of Mr. J. Walsh ; 4th Warrihgton. Mr. R. Garner ; 7th Warrington, Mr. C. Bancroft. The 7th Warrington showed their skill at signalling, and the Ist Warring- ton took part in physical drill and sword exercises. There was a refreshment tent in charge of Mrs. Henry Grecnall and other ladies. A. novel feature was a guessing competition, competitors having to guess the number of peas in a bottle. The first prize wa won by Miss Thora McDermid, who gave as her estimate 1,008, the number actually in the bettle being 1,064. ‘The useful work which the society is doing was best evidenced in the excellent competition in the garden classes. In their report the ju 'gee stated that in the class for the best kept and best stocked cottage garden all the exhibits were highly creditable, especially the first prize garden t Mr. Kippax's) which was a splendid specimen, 11 awers ant vegetable being exc-Ilent. The c!asses foe the neatest and best arranged fib ver garden and small front gardens were , also very good. A special p ice was given ia a case , in which a particularly bright display of fi were bad been grown under unfavourable conditions. Referring to the class for the neatest and best stocked backyard garden the judges said he Stockton Heath Garden Ssciety were doings good work by encouraging the cultivatioa of backyard gardens and good results were shown by the pleasing effects obtained by the com- petitors. In the class for the ne ttest, cleanest and best stocked backyard greenhouse the exhibitors hid shown a gaol deal of skill in the cultivation of green- house plants, and the class was well contested. All the other classes were very gaol, but some of the gardens were Buffet iag severely horn the long drought and were not looking at their best. All the com- petitors in the class for the best display of creepers on hose were worthy of high praise. In many cases the creeper covered almcst all the brickwork of the house, adding greatly to its appearance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>OFFICIALS.</head>
        <p>THE OFFICIALS.</p>
        <p>The officials were —President, Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart, C.V.O. ; vice-president. Revs James C ailing and H V Pigot, Metiers H R Armitage, T Barer, J H Clarke, Leonard G Davies, Vere B Davies, E 3 Hall, H J Jenkins, Roger C Parr,A J Willett, II I Wynne Jones ; cornmittae, Mr NV H Woodcock, chairman ; Mr J H Fletcher, vice-chairman, and Messrs J B Bax'er, J Bell, D S Birrell, J W Bouchier, E Bradshaw, G A Danlop, T E Hen, R Gorton, M W Grant, C W Hallows. .T Holt, H B Houghton, 0 Hudson, F A Jenkins, J MeDertnid, J Owen, T H Plevin, F A Rathbone, W Skelton, C G Stone, C Wilcocksmi attd J R Wood; hon treasurer, Mr D E Watson; acting lion, secretary, Mr E G White. The judges were : Outdoor exhibits, Massrs. P Wilkinson and R Poulton ; exhibits at the s'iow, Messrs. St mey (Widnes), Bennett (LymW, Bramwell (Altrincham) and Finch (Liverpool). THE OPENING CERENIONY. There was a good attendance at the opening ceremony which was performed by Mrs. Legh, of High Legh Hall, who was accompanied by Colonel Legh. Sir Gilbert °retinal% Burt., presided and amongst others present were —Lfudy Greenall, Mrs Lyon (Appleton Hall), Colonel and Mrs Coaches Lyan, Lsdy and Miss Colquhoun of Colquhonn, Mr Ireland-HI ackburne, MiSR SISSOIIS. %11. Alan Sykes, M.P., Ray .1 Coll inn, Rev. H. V Pigot, Ms Flumry Greenall, Mranl Mrs R I Wynne. Jones, Mr 11 Beresford Melville, Couinpllor W Il d lenkins, Mr and Mrs T Baxter, Mr and Mrs. P Rylands, Mr A Lister Have, Mr W 11 Woodcock (chairman of committee), Mr J H Fletcher (vice- chairman), Mr E 0 Whits (secretary pro tern,. Sir Gilbert Graenall, after congratulating the society upon having such a fine day for its show, said that owing to the very dry season the show was not quite so large an usual ; they did not expect it. The entries, however, were oft very good quality, and those who bed seen them assured him that they came well up to the togh standard that they produced in Stockton Heath. The show was not the only work that the society did ; it had many other branches. One of the best features was the attention given to outside gardens. This year, he was glad to tell them, there was • larger entry in that section than ever before, and the fudges said they had neater known the gardens to be so good. (Applause.) Owing to adverse weather and other causes the funds of the society had not been in the most flourishing condition. The very hest thanks of all interested in the socisty should he given to the members of the committee for the way they had put their shoulders to the wheel to try to poll the society round, so that it might be a financial success. They were all proud of the show and it attrazted many people to the village. Toey were pleased tilt Mra</p>
        <p>Legh had taken the trouble to come over from High l,egh to open that exhibition and they all h'ghly appreciated her interest in thesociety. (Applause). Mrs. Legh, who had a hearty reception, said it gave her great pleasure to meet them on such an interesting occasion and the object for which the prizes were to be given had her warmest sympathy. Flowers gave the greatest of pleasure and interest and had a soothing, elevating and civilizing effect on us. An invalid said to her the other day I would rather be a flower than anything else, because they give so much pleasure." There the soothing effect must have coma in. Then she must tell them a little story about a geranium. A young couple married. The wife knew nothing shout cooking or house work and as she was rather may, she just took it rosy and did not trouble to learn. (Laughter.) The husband came of rather a drinking family and finding his wife untidy and his home dirty he came to the conclusion that the public house was more attractive and often spent his evenings there. One day a neighbour came in to the wife, told her she had been sent for to nurse a sick daughter, and asked her to water and take care of her geranium. It was a beauty. The wife put it in the window admiringly and then noticed how dirty the window curtains motel tealie it,. The spirit moved her to wash them. Then toe pones of glass looked dirty ; she washed them. Than the floor drew her attention. When her husband came in the difference in his wife and home was so apparent and no attractive that he remained at home that evening, and not being sunk deep into the abyss of drunkenness, he gave it up. People possessing gardens could hive all sorts of nice things, but gardens did not come within the reach of all. Every room, however, could haves window box which would afford pleasure both to passers by and to those who watched and teodei the flowers. (Applause.) A little while ago she heard someone nay" The rose is a very con- tented flower." Sir a entirely disagreed with that, because the rose, perhaps more than other flowers, became sickly and died if it were not watered and tended properly. Mrs. Legh concluded by speaking very highly of the appearance of the village and said she always noticed the gardens and window boxes when she passed through. (Applause.) A handsome bouquet, composed of flowers grown by at embers tf tha society, WAS presented 0 Mrs. Legh by Miss carrell. Mr. R. I. Wynne Jones, in proposing a vote of thanks to Mrs. Legh, emphasised what she had said about the importance of carefully tending rose.. Half an hour male a great difference ton flower in the atmosphere of a hot tent and a rose which won a prize at the opening of that show might be absolutely worthless now. Mr. W. H. Woodcock seconded, and the vote was heartily passed. The Itev.J. Colli as propored a vote of thanks to Mrs. ' Henry Greenall and the lady judges and helpers. Mr. T. Baxter seconded, and said a flower show with- t out ladies would be every dull affair. (Laughter and applause). ; The vote was cordially agreed to, and Mrs. Henry Greenish responded. Councillor W. H. J. Jenkins proposed a similar ; compliment to Sir Gilbert Greenish for ',residing. Ile said be rejoiced in the good work which Sir (Silbert was • always doing, inleupport (1 agriculture land of every good canes in the loctaLty.</p>
      </div>
