      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>TERRITORIALS RETURN. An Excellent Fortnight's Training.</head>
        <p>TERRITORIALS RETURN. An Excellent Fortnight's Training.</p>
        <p>Lnoking bronzed and the pictures of robust health, the Warriogton Territorials returned home on Sunday after a fortnight's training under canvas in the pi.turesque rural dish jot of Farleton, Westmorland. The men were due to reach Bank Quay Station about noon but owing to the large amount of traffic con- sequent upon the breaking up of the brigade camp, their j ourney was greatly delayed and it was two o'clock before the first tr..in arrived, the second follow- ing after a short interval. Toe first contingent were under the direction of Colonel J. D. Fairclough (com- manding) and the Adjutant, and the second under the care of Major Appleton. Headed by the regimental and bugle bands, the men marched by way of Wilson- Patten-street, round the Cannons at Arpley, Bridge- street and Sankey-street to the lawn in front of the Town Hall. They were then dismissed by companies to the Drill Hall, shortly afterwards returning home. A small number of men, under the direction of Lieutenant and Quartermastet Ingram, were left behind to strike the camp. In many respects, says a military c Trespondent,the training has been a memorable one. Although the divisirm has been weakenel to the extent of some 4,00) troupe (members of the units selected to go on mmteuvres w.th the Regular Army next month), this did not in any way interfere with the effoi ts made to increase the efficiency of the remainder. General Bethune had drawn out in advance a well-reflected scheme, and the weather was such that the operations were only once, and then not seriously, interfered with by rain. All ranks are delighted with the training. The work was hard, but not quite so hard as in the Lune Valley a year ago, and always there was ample time allowed for recreation. Oa Friday night last the operations entailed a bivouac in the open, but the weather conditions were so delightful that the men thoroughly enj .yed the experience, and would have been quite willing to have it repeated. The health of the camp was excellent throughout. A few trivial accidents there were, of course—these nary be looked upon as inev'table where 10,000 men, drawn chiefly from large centres of popul4tion, go under canvas together—but, thanks to the splendid arrangements and wise precautions made by the medical and sanitary staffs, serious cases of sickness were entirely unknown. The result is that the men have benefited materially ii health during the training, as well as in a knowledge of their military drake. Reveil'e s envied early on Sunday morni.g. and soon the lines of every un't presentei scenes of ' exceptional anim.e on. Tents ha.' t be struck aid stores returned, and then each unit in turn set aft to the allotted entraining station, Ifilnthorpe, Burton, and Holme, or Oxenhohne, as the case might be. Faces and necks of the troops were burnt to a deep bro. ze, and the men marched with a jaunty, easy stride from the hips, a stride which told to the least observant eye the (ale of the route marching and hill- climbing which had formed such a feature of the fortnights operations.</p>
        <p>Athletic Since it</p>
        <p>At the annual athletic *parts of the Salford PL.lice and Fire Brigtde Athletic Society, at Weaste, on Saturday, L. C. D ,inteth, of Warrington AR. (13yds.) took first prize in the quarter-mile bicycle handicap °pm race. mmirminme- 211111MINIMIIIIP</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>LIBERALS AND THE CLAIM TO VOTE.</head>
        <p>LIBERALS AND THE CLAIM TO VOTE.</p>
        <p>Last Day To-Day.</p>
        <p>Tc-day is the last day upon which the man who is entitled to Le placed upon the register of voters, lot happens not to be there, can make his claim. This year is all the more important, 80 far as the date is concerned, because 20th August—the last day for claiming the vote—falls arm a Sunday. Liberals should at once ascertain whether they are on the list ; and, if not, should authorise the nearest Liberal agent to at once put forward a form for them. It must be done by to day at the latest. 00118810 n from the register means that one's vote is lost for a year dating from Ist November so far as municipal elections are concerned ; and t.r a siarlar period, dating from Ist January, in regard to Pa, liamentaty elections. It is true that no general election is contemplated in the New Year, but there is always a chance of a by-election. Therefore, all Liberals should inspect the lists which are affixed to town balls, places of worship. and other public places, and ascertain whetheritheir names are on,</p>
        <p>THE LATCH-KEY VOTER. There are special reasons this year why Liberals should look after their votes. One is that the 'latch- key" voter is dead. For five years the "latch-key " voter has worried political agents in general, and the law courts in particular. _This year he is no more.</p>
        <p>for a recent decirsion of the Court iii-Ap-p-eid Ka-a—M;11; it clear that unless a man ii, ratei to the pot law he is not entit'ed to an occupier's vote, which means a vote for all elections.</p>
        <p>On the existing register are tens of thousands of people who come under the " latch-key " qualification. But the decision of the Courts places them in the category of the "lodger." But whereas it is the duty ' of the overseers or town clerk to place every occupier on the list who is entitled to be there, it is not their duty to put lodge's on. The law is that the lodgers must claim for themselves. Even though a lodger was on the old list, he has still to claim personally through a new form to be on the new list. POINTS OF IMPORTANCE.</p>
        <p>The date for Claiming as " old 1 ,dgers " expired Cu 25th July, but there is time for Liberal lodgers who did not do so to claim as " new lodgers. " These " old lodgers " end the " latch-key voters are in exactly the same case. If they are not on the overseers' list they must claim for themselves. It is nobody's responsibility beyond their own. There are other points of importance in s,curing the franchise. Here area few of a general kind. If rccupancy of a house has been continuous from 15th July last year to 15th July this year. If removals mare taken place in the same Parlia- mentary borough between 15th July last year and 15th July this year. If lodgers have changul their rooms in the same house, and their qualification is still £lO a year minimum for their room or rooms unfurnished, notwithstanding that their landlord or landlady may have changed. .</p>
        <p>All these are entitled to a vote. Again, the necessity is fcr Liberals to examine the lists, and, if their qualifications are as above and they find their names omitted, they should at once send in a claim. REMOVALS.</p>
        <p>The case of the removals is most important. There are,for instance,seven divisions in the Tower Hamlets. Yet an occupier living in any one of these for a month or so each, is entitled as a " successive" voter to claim for his last rB3llO I ce. Again, St Pancras and Islington each have lour Parliamentary divisions, but removal from cne division to ancther in the same Parliamentary borough does not dis- francLiße; although removal from Islington into St. Pancras would void the vote. It is quite stupid —but still it is the law.</p>
        <p>Tens of thousands of people in London and the provinces pay their rates in their rents. But although they have paid their rent regularly, their names may not appear on the Overseers' List owing to the fact that their landlords have not paid the rates for their houses. Still, the occupier can get his name inserted by paying the rates himself and deducting the amount from the rent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>WARRINGTON COUNTY MAT.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY MAT.</p>
        <p>On Thursday btfore his H.onotH, judge Stanger, EC</p>
        <p>ORME AND SON V. W. 111('NEit SLOANE.—Mr Worthington of Manchester appeared for the plaintiffs and Mr. Browne for the defendant—Mr. Worthington stated that plaintiffa were billiard MIAs makers and the defendant was recently in their employ and the action van brought to recover £37 ss. a railway contract ticket between Manchester and Liverpool or Eli value and a tickat /or the Royal Exchange, Manchester. or in the alternative 4 guineas. The defendants solicitors bad written admitting £37 51. Mr. Collins, one of the directors of the firm said they enraged a good number of travellers. The defendant was riven • contract ticket ame years ago to travel from Warrington to Manchester and back on the Company's business. Later it was extended from Manchester to Liverpool. It was given under the same conditions that they gave tickets to other travellers. The firm also paid for a ticket for the Royal Exchange under the same conditions. The firm would be able ti get the railwiy ticket transferral or get • rebate.—ln answer to Mr. Browne witness said the derendfmt asked for an increase of salary and the firm said they would pow his fare between Manchester and Warrington.—Mr. Browns real a letter fron the defendant, who was unable to be present, stxting that he thought the firm should be generoua to him, as he had been in their service 16 years, and had received orders amounting to £12,000, resulting In • profit to the firm of E 7,5,4. Mr. Browne added that the tickets were given to the defend tut personally, and no arrangement was made in the event of his leaving the service of the- firm for the tickets to be returned. They were giver. for 12 monthc—ilis Honour said he thought the tickets were given to defendant for the puTose stated by Mr. C rills and he would give lodgment for the plaintiffs for the return claimed, messed at £l2. The £37 ss. was admitted.</p>
        <p>HEREDITARY ENEMIES OF NONCONFORMITY THE UNREPENTANT LORDS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gerard N. Ford, of Manchester, President of the. English Congregational Union, in speaking at Bolton- le-Sands, nes:. Lancaster. on Saturday, referred to the Parliament Bill, and said at last the hereditary enemies. of Nonconformity had hem made to bit,, the dust. Nonconformists I ad sis !Tared many things at the hands of the House of Lords, and hope deferred bad mad* the heart sick because of them. The Lords had thwarted them at eaery turn, and reform had had to be wrung from them, not given by them. They had been utterly unrepentant to the very last, and he did not think anyone, even a 1 3ry could blame Nonconformists if they threw up their hats and rejoicid tha at last. England was really free. (Applause,.</p>
        <p>The London and North-Western Railway C ',spar have given notice to senders of lamb commodities and to Irish radarny companies that delivery of consign- meats cannot be undertaken for Liverpool. Birkenhead, Oldlasm, Sheffield. Chaste-, Warrington, Stockport Bristol and London for foreign shipments. Eacorlioow to Manchester and places served by train services through Manchester are also nupended.</p>
      </div>
