      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>CRICKET, BOWLING, AND</head>
        <p>CRICKET, BOWLING, AND</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Cricket Notes.</p>
        <p>The match originally set apart for the benefit of Festal], the Warringten professional, wz.s rendered an impossibility by the heavy rain which was experienced last Saturday week. The club therefore decided that the professional should have the proceeds of the next home fixture—a match with the Northern Zingaii which came off under delightful conditions at Arpley on Saturday. The suitors brought a strong side, but Warrington were without W. Richardson, 11. Hands and J. W. Pearson, who were away on holiday. The result of the match was eminently pleasing to the home supporters, fr although confronted with a formidable task, the Warringtonians achieved success by a substantial majority. The visitors had first turn at the wicket; but bad it not been for A U Turner and A C Saville they wou!d have made an iwiifferent chow. The bawliog of Pestell and Petal idge gave trouble to most of the batsmen, but A II Turner was in great form, hitting out very p`uckily and carrying out his bat with 40 to his credit. Saville proved a vigorous and sound partner and had madJ 24 when he was smaitly caught by Callon.</p>
        <p>Warrington began their innings with Pestell and G. Wocdccck, but the start was not very hopeful, both being back in the pavilion with only five tuns on the board. The feature of the game was a magnifi- cent stand by S Partridge and 11 Speakman The visitors curiously under-estimated Speakman's payers of hard-hitting, and brought the field C:0E0 in They had a great surprise, being frequently dispatched to the boundtry, and b tth he and Partridge defied changes of bowling and sent up the score at a merty pace. Pattridge in particular displayed wonderful skill and confidence, and runs came at the rate of two a minute. Speakman was caught by Saville if Dickenson when he had ktucketl up 52. his innings including eleven 4's. Partridge's dismissal was brought about in similar fashion by Livesey, when he hal placed 56 to his cr.dit. 13. Siicock contributed a good nct ant innings uf 27. When time was called the Warrington total had reached 181 for six wickets.</p>
        <p>Warrington Conservatives are easily maintaining their leadership of the Wallington and District League. Sankey Wire Mills were their opponents on Saturday nr.d had to admit defeat to the extent of $O runs. J. Constable and J. Duckworth were in brilliant form, making 29 each, and Sergeant-Major Soatt also batted smartly for 23. On the Sankey side W. Hunter (13) was the top scorer.</p>
        <p>Whitecross piled on a majnity of 40 at the expense of Latchford Wesleyan. Hutchinson was in great form with the bat and made 41, Barrow also con- tributing a notable innings of 21.</p>
        <p>Wilderepnol Drewei y who were down to play et Orford only appeared with five men. It was decided however to clay the match. Orford baited first and compiled .14. tut the Wilderspool men had to be content with !cur.</p>
        <p>The following is the League Table up-to-date : Pld. Won Lost Drn. Pls. W'g't'n Comer. l3 ... 12 ... 1 ... 0 ... 21 Sankey Whitecroas Bee Sankey Wire Mills 15 ... 6 ... 7 ... 2 ... 14 Walkers Brewery Latchford We. Orford _ 13</p>
        <p>Rylands Bros Wildars'pl Brewery 12 ... 2 ... 10 ... S ... 4 There was a keen encounter between Warrington St. Mary's and the Oaklands, the Saints winning by 4 runs. The losers were weak in the fielding depart- ment, as is evidenced by the fact that the top scruer for the Saints was Mr. " Extras " with 23. Littler knocked up 15 for the Oaklands.</p>
        <p>Another win was placed to the credit of Latchford Baptist first eleven last Saturday, when Marston were the visitors. The home team bitting first knocked together 114 runs to which Manton replied with oily 48. The batting honours for Latchford were shared by Cliffe and Maybury with scores of 49 and 38 respectively. The latter was also in rare form with the ball, taking 6 wickets for 20 runs. For the visitors Littler batted well for his score of 19.</p>
        <p>To-day the first team entertain Runcorn M.F.C. The meeting generally provides a very close finish, and this game should be no exception to the rule, -seeing that both sides are doing exceedingly well at present. The second eleven by COIICIIIBIOII3 at llunc-xn. First team : W G Hayes (captain), E F Cl.ffe, James Cox, Joseph Cox, A E Walker, A Lee, II liaybury, A Albinson, G H Heald, J B Wilkinson and J Hunt.</p>
        <p>Walton Juniors were victorious over Bold-street Juniors by a maj,rrity of nine runs in a low scoring match. U. Blum- did good work for the winners, *coring 18 by sound cricket and capturing four wickets for ten runs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>Bowling Nan. WORKS BOWLING ASSOCIATION FINAL.</head>
        <p>Bowling Nan. WORKS BOWLING ASSOCIATION FINAL.</p>
        <p>oill'oll4TlON.9 NO. 2. TR11.311.11. krmitage and Rigby, when they met Corporation o. 2 in the final for the " Finlayson " Cup last Saturday, as I auticipated, fought their Waterloo, though I did not think they would go down by so many as 26 p iota, despite the fact that they had 40 points to Wain before they could get to the stratum where the Corporation commenced. Mountains are not molten even to an experienced team—a (earn with an enviable record—and un the whole of the match the 40 was only reduced by 14 chalks. Perhaps it was that the losing team were too nervous for the great occasion or perhaps they were too dispirited ; but whatever be "glided thsmt'us Carporation set to work with grim deter minatioo,and fully justified the inclusion of young blood as the sporting phrase has it. As the various sets of men enteted the splendid green at the Horse and Jockey Inn it was apparent that taken individually Armitage's men had handled bowls for a much longer period than the Corporation men. But this did not deter the Corporation lads, who were fortified by the consciousness that their handicap was 70 and their opponents was only 30. However, it was only during the last two ganws that the Cosporation could say with any degree of confidence" %hatever happens, wheth ier tbe hail, rain, or sunshine, we have won a silver cup and gold medals are much better than silver medals, though Fume of us would be content with those. But it is time I got to the games the mselve s.</p>
        <p>0. A. KNOWLES V. P. LAWLESS.</p>
        <p>This was the first game concluded and boded ill for the Corporation, Knowles going down badly by points to Lawless. Lawless was in mucb better form than his chsllenger who at all periods of the game was aeveral chalks behind.</p>
        <p>W. ERIDGH V. A. S. HANDS.</p>
        <p>It is always encouraging to one's side when the Captain wins his ribbon, but Hands of the Corporation did not I think discourage his followers when he succumbed to W Bridge to the narrow margin of one point. Hands started well, the game was a grim business for him, though he continually displayed his geniality as well he might when be stood at 12 at the 10th end while his opponent was soliloquising at 3. One would have thought that for Hands all was serene, but Bridge began to ploy" teachers' and was able to peel at 14, only a moment later to oust the Corporation captain from his leadership.</p>
        <p>V. SW AN V. A. ECKERSLEY. Eckersley of Armitage's played a capital game and woo handsomely by 15 points to 10. He got going well first and stood at 7 when Swan had to be content with 3. But he was not content very long. He made a fine break of 6 which placed him at 9. By the aid of two singles F.ckersley was able to peel at that figure and continuing his creditable exhibition he ran home victor by 15-10.</p>
        <p>T. DAWSON V. W. ROSTIRN.</p>
        <p>Damson (Corporation) only just defeated Rosters by 1 point. They peeled at 4 end 8 when Dawson w ith a sort of inspiration, ran to 14 while Rostern only added another chalk. However, the former's spirit was contagions and Rostern emulating him peeled at 14, though Dawson by. fine wood ran home viator.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>S. VIRTU AND S. LE}..</head>
        <p>S. VIRTU AND S. LE}..</p>
        <p>These two men were equal at 4 and 7, and all that reeds to be veld is that Lee made a floe break of 8 which gsve him victory.</p>
        <p>J. LEITCH V. R. GREEN.</p>
        <p>J. Leitch, otie--caitbeiou—nge-s-t-Oithe Corporation players, had the satisfaction of beating Armitage's captain. Ile played exceedingly well, a fact which was appreciated all round. At the 7th end be was leading but the captain was not at all dismayed and playing with keen judgment he wan only 1 point behind ashen ladtab bad reached 11. However, the latter again aztaadsd the dividing line and won by 3 points. Well dons Leitch !</p>
        <p>P. SHAW V J. TOTTPIR. Potter had practically all his owr .ray with Shaw, who only obtained 8 chalks.</p>
        <p>S. RILEY V H. DAGNALL.</p>
        <p>This was a gam. of fluctuation. Degasll went along capitally and was 6 ahead when he stood at 8, bet Riley playing ateadily omits:del him at 13 and wan by a couple.</p>
        <p>G. F. RICE V. C. NORTH.</p>
        <p>Arter being terribly behind young Rco had the satisfaction of obtaining victory by one point. His opponent was double when he had only five to his credit, hut retrieving himself he peeled at 13, and after North bad got a single he ran home with the aid of a splendidly placed couple.</p>
        <p>P. CLARKE Y. H. HoLERO-K.</p>
        <p>These two were admirably matched, considered both from the points of view of age and experience at the game. They were equal at 4 ant then Holbrook obtained 3 s'ngles, but Clarke male a break of 7 before he could move aglin. However, Holbrook managed to get farther points at a greater rate than Clarke and reached home with two points in hand.</p>
        <p>W. BERESI,oRD V. R. SAVORY.</p>
        <p>There were many touchers throughout this battle. It yes a neck and nick race, only one or TP73 points being the difference all the way. Ar oitage's man planed a fine chalk at the finish, which made him game-15-1 I.</p>
        <p>W. SCMNER V. FIRM</p>
        <p>Tie last gam; was full 01 itit's.;;S;. J. Firth, of Armitager, went off in dead earnest, and the figures at the 5.. h end were 6-3 in his favour. Then Sumner made an enviable break of 7, but even such a good position is not safe, and Firth graduilly crept up until ha was within a couple of the coveted number. Sumner was then only short of one chalk, but Firth ousted him by. really fine couple of wovh. Sumner's play towards the close or the gave wm a bit eccentric, for he was almdst alternately making touchers or wides.</p>
        <p>A. HANDS, CAPTAIN OF THE WIN: CHOSFIELD'S INTEH-DEPARTIIENTAL I COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>This interesting competition promoted by the Recreation Club has proved in every way a g eater success than was anti,ipatsd. Tao enthusiasm shown at the commencement was maintained right to the end and the Committee are t be complimented a/awl the excellent arrangemests which left no room for the slightest h:tch. No fewer than 49 teams of six men per team ente el and considering that this was the initial competition, the total was highly satis- factory.</p>
        <p>For the final stages ou S.torday the green was in perfect condition and with " old sol" in his best humour a splendid afternoon's sport was anticiptted. Nor was tbis expectation belied, for each player put forth all he c...uld to gin the smelt st advantage over his opponent.</p>
        <p>The Finalists were the Tin Canist.r Department and Me. John Taylor's No. I team. The former had a handicap of 22 and the latter one of 15 83 that without a wood being played the Tin Canister team were seven points ahead.</p>
        <p>Mr. Taylor's team, however, proved fairly equal to this task and the Csnister men had to bow the knee to the tune of 12 points The match, however, was not won in a canter by any mean', the iisue being in doubt right up 1) the last game, and the large attendance that had aseerubled were provided nith (toms really clever performances which would have done justice to more experienced "hands.'</p>
        <p>THE GAMES. 11. WELLS V. T. I.ITIIERL IND.</p>
        <p>By far the best match of the afternoon was that between fl. Wells, (Tin Canisttrs), and T. Litherland. , The play of both is envied in many quarters and on Saturday afternoon both men kept up their reputa- tions. The placing of some of the woods at times was almost marvellous. Litherland started off with one to bin credit after the first end,only to be overtaken ,by Wells after the second. He again got ahead but he was closely followed by Wells, the players peeling at the I.lth and 12th ends. This was the last time, however, for there wan no holding Litherland, who ran to game 15-10, attar a mt.st enthusiastic encounter.</p>
        <p>T. DODSON V. IV. JENKS.</p>
        <p>Another goad game was that between T. Datum, Tin Canister, and W. Jenks. The former was the first to make any strides but after peeling at 4 he gradu- ally fell away to leave Dobson the victor by 15-7. It was not such a run-away victory as the score indicates, however, only inches separating the woods on several occasions, and Dobson is to he congratulated upon potting up such a splendid fight against such an experienced bowler as Jenks is known to be.</p>
        <p>P. ELWELL V. J WILCOX</p>
        <p>A tight encounter was that between D. Elwell, Tin Canisters, and J. Wilcox, although the former Flayed a masterly game and his win was very popular. Wilcox was the fir,t to scull with a single, but Elwell did not allow him to add to his account after that until the sixth end. After peeling at 7, Elwell obtained a couple at the following end and maintained that lead right to the finish when the card read 15-13 in his favour.</p>
        <p>W. DIRCIIALI. V. J. ASIIBOURSER.</p>
        <p>Ashbcurner of Taylor's, was one of the two to obtain the coveted number for his side, running home rather easily by 15-7. The second end was the only stage at which they peeled and from then Asbbourner bad matters all his own way. Try as he would Birchall coul3 not overhaul him although he played some very good woods, but he had to reckon with a man who can handle a pair of bowls in no uncertain manner. Anyway, be put up a good fight and may yet be able to reverse his defeat some time in the near future.</p>
        <p>J. OLEAVE V. 11. 'ATTU:WOOD.</p>
        <p>The remaining player to record the full number of points for Taylor's team was H. Littlewood who defeated J. 01* awe, 15-8. (heave started off in a most determined fashion but his antagonist did not allow him to get far before he put a check to his career. After peeling at the 3:d and 6th coda, Little- %nod only allowed his npponent to obtain a single in six more ends, aLd it was too late for Gleam e to redeem himself.</p>
        <p>A. GLEAVE V. . EriowN.</p>
        <p>A. Gl.ave of the Tin Canister's started cif in no half-hearted fashion by winning the firt two ends each time with two home," and the third end with a single. Brown then managed to obtain a single but he never thomughly recovered his form and Cleave ran out winner by 15 points to 8.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND D:STRICT BOWLING ASSOCIATION. INDIVIDUAL MERIT COMPETITION. FIRS'. ROUND.</p>
        <p>The weather last Saturday was ideal for bowling, when the find round was continued. The surroundings at the King and Queen green are exquisite. Twenty- eight gsmee were brought to an irsse and there still remain thirteen games to complete the first round. These are down to be completed to-day barseen the hours of 1 30,,and 3 o'clock. No grace will hr given after 3 o'clock.</p>
        <p>The green had plenty of music in it. At periods it took competitors all their time to keep the woods on the green. Of COUT4P, once they got " the feet" they were all right. Some of the results wme surprising.</p>
        <p>The most intereetine game during the afternoon was between Secretary J. W. Davies and Alf. Ford. It was a ding-dong game and I thought Davies would just get borne when leading nicely is the teens, but Ford got nibbling at singles and just scraped home by a majority of 3. A very good game indeed. John Holbrook of Dittos did excellently in defeating W. E. Middleton, a member of the green, by 21 5. Bob AntroVas churned some of his old form against S. ti :eau. His game simply mystiflA hie companions aud the spectators as well. Antrobus opened the game with a single and then Green made a tine break of eight Ant-a:Juts tinging a tricky mark, scared twenty points' off the real. It must not be said that Green bawled badly. Had he possessed a stronger pair of woods, I doubt whether Antrobus would have had stab an IMUS,IIi run of points. W. Andrews slid whet ha liked with George Iligham though the latter had been hurrying to the green. I sa.d at once he was not tit to play under such conditions. Asdrews won spite easily 21-6.</p>
        <p>Harry C iok and W. Cuter were • good match. Carter possessed the better knowledge of the green. Cook gave me the impression that he would win at the outset hut when the critical tims cams he lost, Carter winning 21-15. Fred Dile surprised everyone by his exhibition against Jack Monits. I thought it a really good thing for Monks, who, by the way, cat give points to some of our very beat biwlers in Warrington. Well, Dale revelled in the q tick ruining and finding his length time after time. no never give Monks a chance of making a good fight, Dale finishing 21-11. Albert Gleave's game against Bert liogsn was a preliminary game in a sense on a cross mark which wanted catching. tie did what he liked winning quits comfortably 21-I.</p>
        <p>I was mi eh impressed with E. 13 onlyrn's game against Jack SWI ,too sod if he verb ces the form displayed kat S tat thy iie will want some sbahing. Sganton was burl I,sten. by 21-18. T. N. Fieldin woe on the j it! with IL M. Tilling. Ile was Always comforted° from Ihe o itset and nit being troubled, won quite easily 21-7.</p>
        <p>P. Ott) L., 8. 11, rNINI TEklf, RECEIVES TEIE CUP.</p>
        <p>LANCASTITHE AMATEUR ASSOCTATION. C.I.M.&amp;TERY 31[33 THE CRO3FLELD CUP.</p>
        <p>You 9111 dor Mess rec Meat that last Saturday predicted tint .1 e Warrington Ceine..ery would defeat Sainton Literals arid no win ths Crorfleld cup offered in connect i n with the Lonosahire County Competition. I was m'attken; cooks ire! gas i 3 apt to deceive To go down by 9 points in a gems in which the winning team Obtained 219 points la perhaps not usoreditable, bit am sure the Oen, star; men did not give of their best. They knew thiiy hid a better reputation than their opponents —nod they ralied on th it awl it failed them. Sorely they omitted to recognise that in a ficial each team is generally determioel to win and that nay sl ickoes 3 on their part would 19 readily counter-hammed by the more strenuous efforts on the part of the opposing team. Fancy only winning games oat of 12! I cannot any any more, lam too &amp;stop iinted and almost ashamed Ito Oink that they übooli fail to play their trump card.</p>
        <p>. ... T.„....,-.,...?: , _ ..._.... , . , , ...,. • - Ak. . 4410 t • e 1/4 to,' Nit ' 1 4 f ...,- , . 1 f 4. 4,..,:..., ~ „ 4 i 1 . .. . ,</p>
      </div>
