      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>BOWLING. WORKS BOWLING ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>BOWLING. WORKS BOWLING ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>CORPORATION NO. 2 WIN TIIE "FiNLAYSON" CUP.</p>
        <p>Last Saturday the final of ths Warrington Works' Bowling Association attracted a large crowd at the horse and Jockey fun. The competing teams—Armitage and Rigby and Cmporation No. 2-I:ed numerous supporters who readily appreciated the efforts of their respective "MAK." After R capital light the match resulted in a win for the Corporation by 26 mints, though it must be remembered that they had on advantage over Armitage's of 40 ['lints at the start. The garnes are graphically desatibed in another column. Scores :</p>
        <p>CORPORATION. ARMITAGE AND RIGBY. Handicap (70) Handicap (30) G A Knowles ... 7 P Lamb,. ... 15 AK Hands (c,ptain) ... 11 W Bridge ... 15 W Swan ... 10 A Eckersley 15 T Damon „. 15 W R05tran......14 S Leo ... ... 15 S Firth ... ... 7 Laitch ... 15 BGreen... .. 12</p>
        <p>ii;;;;" 1:1 15 H Dagoall... ... 13 S Riley... .. 15 G F Rice ... ... 15 C North ... 14 P Clarke ... 13 H Holbrook ... 15 W Bereeford ... 14 It Savory 15 W 5umeer......14 J Firth ... 15</p>
        <p>223 Corporation won by 2C, points.</p>
        <p>At the conclusion of the snatch the presentation of the cup and medals by Mr: W P Hylands took place. Mr S It Armitage presided over the gathering. and amongst those present were Sir Gilbert Greenish, Bart. C.V.0., Mr W P Bylaw's, Mr Thomas Locker, Mr W Webster, Mr John Hankinson, Councillor Ashcroft, Mr W Patrick (hon. eecretary) and Mr John Willett (chair- man of the committee). Mr. WILLETT said it was a source of pleasure to all of them to sse such a large attendance, bat he hoped a fortnight hence, when a match would be played with the Caledonians for the benefit. of the Nurses Institu- tion, that there would be a still larger attendance. He had not a single disrespectful word to say against the Infirmary, which was a grand institution, but be was afraid the Nurses' Home was somewhat neglected, and this year members of the Works Bawling Association had made up their minds to play a match tor the benefit of the inetitution he had named. (Applause.) Mr. W. PA:RICK WM pleased the match bad gone off with such a swing and he thanked the captains and secretaries of the various teams for the manner in which they had assisted him in the various duties he had had to perform during the season. They had been of an arduous character, although the assistance he had received had made them a pleasure. He hoped that by the forthcoming match announced they world ha able to band over Efill to the Nurses' Institution. (Applause.) Mr. hinny remarked they had had a very pleasant afternoon, and the games had been played in a truly English sportsmanlike way. Although his teem (Armitage's) had been beaten they had not been unworthily beaten when they considered the number of paints they had to fight against. (Hear, hear.) He congratulated his men upon the fine way in which they had stood up to the game. He also heartily congratu- lated the wieners. (Applause). . . . . . .</p>
        <p>„.- Mrs. Hylnnis then presentel the cup to the captain of the winning team, A. E. Hands. Mr. HANDS, in reply, remarked that Britishera took their sports sadly, and be thought it was true. When he came to the green that afteraoon, instead of expect- ing a pleasant game, his beat WAS in his boots— (laughter)—when he remembered that the Corporation team had to fight against Alossrs. Armitage and itlgby's but they bad Came out victorious, which was a matter of satiefac ion to the team. (Applause). Mrs Rylands then handed gold medals to the winners and silver medals to the runners up. Si( GILBERT GREENALL, Bart., proposed a vote of thanks to Mrs Rylands and Mr. Armitage. With r-gard to the latter he remarked that alth raga his team had been defeated they had certainly not been disgraced, as they had played a most sportsmanlike game and had taken their defeat like sportsmen. Ha hoped they would live long, and have better lack ant time. (Applause). Mr. WEB,TF.a seconded, and said it most be very gratifying to the Committee of the Association to see such a fine gathering. He had always thought that a game of bowls w,irr a game that anyone could take part In and play in a gentlemanly and friendly manner. (Hear, hear). The motion was carried with enthusiasm.</p>
        <p>Mr W PETER RYLANEIS, replying On behalf of Mrs Ry lands said they had both watched with great interest for many years the progress of that association. He had the pleasure of being at the annual meeting last year, and every Saturday he read the newspaper reports with interest to see how the Warrington bowlers were getting on, particularly the various teams from his own works. (Applause ) It was a matter of regret that Rylands Bros. were not represented on that occasi.m. (Laughter nod appl lose ) If they had bent he was sure they would have put spa good show. He dared say they would see them there another year. He also wished to express his gratitude to the official of the association for the tremendous amount of work they had done in bringing this tournament to such a successful issue. Fifty teams entered, and it was certainly a great honour for the teams who had reached the final. (Applause.) A omapetition of this character, extending over so many Suturd us, had invalved a large amount of work upon Mr. Willett and Mr. Patrick, and he asked the audience to give them three hearty cheers, which were heartily given.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>" CO NSERVAIIVE " CHALLENGE CUP.</head>
        <p>" CO NSERVAIIVE " CHALLENGE CUP.</p>
