      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>CROSFIELDS' RECREATION CLOD Inter-Departmental —Competition Final.</head>
        <p>CROSFIELDS' RECREATION CLOD Inter-Departmental —Competition Final.</p>
        <p>Saturday witnessed the closing stages of the Inter- departmental Competition in connection with the above club, when the final was decided between teams representing the Tin Canister Department and Mr. John Taylor's No. 1. This is the first time a competi- tion of this kind has been held. and it speaks well for the club that it has proved each a success, as was manifest by the large gathering which assembled on the recreation ground. Mr. J. J. Croafield and several departmental heads were present. The comp3tition started with the magnificent entry of 49 teams with six men to a team, representing no fewer than 291 members, so that the committee are to be congratu- lated upon the step ttkert. Some excellent boaling was witnessed in the final. The Tin Canister Depart- ment were conceded seven points, and even then it was evident that they would have to play for all they were worth to gain the upper hand. In this respect they were unsuccessful, for when time was called it was found that Mr. Taylor's team were victori.Jus by the margin of 12 points. Scores :</p>
        <p>TIN T.:AMSTER:DEPT. MR. JOHN TAYLORS 50. 1. Handicap 22 Han heap 15 II Welk (capt) A °leave l5 S Brown T Dobson 7 W Jenks J Gleave D Elwell , l5 J Wilcox W Birchall</p>
        <p>84 Majority for Mr. John Taylor's, 12.</p>
        <p>CP.O STIE LD S WINNISG Mr. J. J. Cresfield in ti</p>
        <p>After the match the prizes were distributed by Mr. J. J. Crostleld.</p>
        <p>The- §-e.Ti;l7:-rany (Mr. Jennings) in presenting his report stated that the Committee started the season with a heavy programme before them, it having been decided to hold three mains to run an inter-depart- mental competition, to have friendly matches with Messrs. Gossage, Rylanis Bros., Conservative Club and Liberal Club, and to enter a team in the Warrington Works Association. Two of the mains had been held and had proved successful, having an entry Or 110, and prizes being offered to the value of £9. With regard to the Inter-departmental Competition, if t%e same spirit and enthusiasm prevailed next season as this, Captain G. Crosfiel4 had kindly con- sented to present a Challenge Cup. (Cheers.) In the hiendly matches the club had not been beaten on the the aggregate number of points while oa two rccasions they had been victorious away from home. In the Works Association, however, they were not as successful as in the previous year, having to succumb to Lockers by the small margin of 13 points after I conceding to them a start of 70 points. He thanked the Committee for the energy displayed as also the rank and file for the excellent order maintained. Applause) Mr. J. J. CROSFTELD said that although he did not actually follow bawling he had been greatly interested that afternoon. He pithered that that was the first I year such a competition had been held and he was pleased to hear that it had proved such a pronounced success. He had been struck with the good feeling that existed between the opposing teams and hoped that it would always continue, as it proved what true sportsmen they all were. He would like to show hie interest in the competition by promising next year to present a prize (Elear, bear) He zmogratulated both teams upon the excellent performances given. (Applause.) Mr. J. Roeie proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Crosfield which was seconded by Mr. A. JoNI:9 and carried unanimously. The prizes took the form of gold medals f w the winners and silver medals with gold centres for the runners up these having been provided by the Reel eation Club.</p>
