        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SAMEDAY, AL:GUST 19, 1911</p>
        <p>THE STRIKE. " Even then, had the corn panies met the representatives of the men, the present position would have been saved. The companies arrogantly refused this, and by so doing denied, the railway workers of this country what has been conceded to every other class of worker." The above quotation frog the statement of the leaders of the four railwaymen's unions issued just after midnight on Thursday,sums up in a few words the root cause of the desper- ate situation which prevails to-day. Are the men entitled to recognition through their unions? There can only be one answer from all who have given sympathetic study and consideration to the rights of Labour, and there is little room for any answer not in the affirmative from those who wish merely to decide fairly between man and man. There are doubtless sound reasons for adversely criticising the action of the strikers in coming out so precipitately, in giving such short notice, and in refusing the proffered " Royal Commission" point blank before its composition and powers were understood. But over and above these considerations we do not believe the public will acquit the railway directors on their side of a stubborn obstinacy which I persists in maintaining obsolete industrial methods, and is certainly the fundamental cause of the whole trouble. Why should the principle of direct negotiation between representatives of employers and employed, which is good enough for the great manufacturing industries, good enough for mineowners and shipowners, good enough, in a word for every other con- siderable undertaking, not be good enough for a railway company? It means, of course, recognition of the unions. Why not, then, recognise the unions, like everyone else? It is argued that the unions do not represent a sufficient proportion of the men. Until their recognition has bt„, secured, their membership will naturally be limited, But they do already represent the great bulk of the effective, permanent staff, and the time cannot be indefinitely delayed when they will represent the whole of it. And after all is said that can be said about tactics there is the broad outstanding fact, which dwarfs all others, that there is a very large number of railway employees who are not getting a wage sufficient to keep themselves and their families in comfort or decency. How can a man keep a family on seventeen to eighteen shillings a week? Let those who blame the men answer this question if they can. The continuance of such conditions of labour is a social and economic wrong which has been aggravated in recent years by a reduction in the pur- chasing power of even this meagre we. 'At the same time the earnings of Capital have been growing faster than ever in the history of this country. It may be true that the railwEty companies have not shared, as have other industrial concerns, in the increase. This, however, is a question solely of management and rating, and if it comes to be a question with the public who use the railways for the conveyance of them- selves and their goods, " Are you prepared to pay slightly higher rates if there is no other way?" the answer would without doubt, be "we will pay such rates as will enable the companies to give a living wage to all their employees." The railways not only repre- sent a vast industry, but they closely affect the everyday existence of the whole corn- munity and the relationship between them- selves and their employees is a question which cannot in the least be considered merely a matter for the individuals concemed,whether titled directors or employees. The concern, the inconvenience, nay the suffering, are equally the public's and the worker's, and there can be no sanction or approval of any arrangement which does not better the lot of the employees on strike, ensure them the recognition which is their due, and guarantee the nation against a recurrence of such a disruption of industry as prevails to-day.</p>
        <p>On Thursday, August FREE CHOICE OF 10th, the House of MEMBERS. Commons passed a resolution in favour ot the payment of a salary of £490 to Members of Parliament not in receipt of a Ministerial salary. On Monday the House passed the necessary vote in Supply to carry that resolution into effect. The business of the House of Commons in these days, when there is a Government in power which really believes that the duty of Parliament is to legislate for the needs of the country, is so great and so engrossing that the choice of constituencies would be limited to representatives who are in possession of inlependent means unless there was some provision for the support of members who give practically their whole time to their Parliamentary duties. The Osborne judg- ment has prevented Trade Unions from making such provision for labour's choice of members who are really competent to repre- sent labour. There is much to be said from the labour point of view alohe for the proposal that the state, and not labour levies, should give this freedom of choice to labour. But there is still more to be said, from the point of view of the whole community, for a system which would give every constituency a free choice of representatives, unhampered' by the financial restrictions which would make the House of Commons a plutocratic preserve.</p>
        <p>Into the larger problems A DANGEROUS raised by the social GOSPEL. phenomenon of almost</p>
        <p>universal unrest it is neither possible nor de Arable to enter in these notes. It must be sufficient to point out that when the stalwarts of the Tory cause have been advocating resistance pressed to the very extreme of physical violence against any measure of Home Rule that may be passed into law, and when they have also been justifying and exemplifying in their own persons total disregard of the order and decencies of Parliamentary life, it is not astonishing to find that the mob of a city like Liverpool, where Mr. F. E. Smith, for example, has not scrupled to call in at a by-election the dangerous aid of racial and religious animosity, is showing itself ready to follow the example of the mob of" gentle- men" who break with ease the law and order governing debate in the House of C.3mmons, and turn the gospel of resist- ance, preached by the so-called Unionists, to purposes of which the preachers never dreamt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>LNOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>LNOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>Warrington markets have made a profit of £2,951 ss. 2d. during the past year. • 0 4 . .</p>
        <p>The Borough treasurer's abstract of accounts, just issued, show that the excess of assets over liabilities on the whole of the Corporation's rates and undertakings is £571,630 2s. 3d.</p>
        <p>5,375,572 passengers ;ere carried by the Warrington Corporation trams during the past year. The net profit on the system was £2,344 of which £344 went to the reserve fund and £2,000 to the relief of rates. • • __ • _</p>
        <p>The abstract of thel;orough accounts gives an insight into the cost of the Warrington Corporation Bill. The items recorded am:— Parliamentary agents' charges (on account) £1,533 ; counsels' fees, £347 10s. ; expenses Sm. of witnesses, £449 14s. ; maps and mounting, £7 58. 10d. * * *</p>
        <p>The King will stay in Scotland, according to present arrangements, for between six and seven weeks. His Majesty is expected to arrive in Scotland from Yorkshire on Tuesday next, and he will take up his residence at Balmoral Castle about Wednes- day, the 30th, and is to stay there until the first week in October. 1* * *</p>
        <p>The Warrington Agricultural Society had the remarkable experience of a delightful summer day for its annual show on Thurs- day, but there was the railway trouble to contend with, and although this had no appreciable effect on the number of exhibits. it appears to have frightened many would-be visitors from a distance from attempting the journey. The persons paying for admission totalled about 4,200, which is a decrease on last year, when the traditional wet weather cast a very real damper on the proceedings. The average ratepayer has probably little idea of the cost of maintaining kw and order in the borough. During the past year the expenditure on the local police force (including wages, uniform, accoutrements, &amp;c.), was £7,084 13s. 2d. but in addition, there are other items for the conveyance and maintenance of prisoners and upkeep of buildings, bringing the total to £7,901 Os. 2d. The Government contribute £3,542 6s. 7d. towards the pay and clothing of the force, and altogether the income reaches £4,379 10s. id.•</p>
        <p>There is much intei:est for English people at the moment—though, perhaps, no particular comfort—in the announcement that the rainfall of a village among the hills of Assam, during the ten weeks from May Ist to July 9th this year, was 250 inches. The village is Cherrapunji, the rainiest spot in Asia, and presumably in the world. Ha annual rainfall is something over 450 inches —say, fifteen times as much as London. Cherrapunji stands on a plateau, over- looking the plain of Syl het, and it is 4,455 feet above sea-level. _ . . . . . . .</p>
        <p>Lord Wolmer's activities in the House of Commons may recall early memories of his father to those who watched the debates in the Commons nearly twenty years ago. The first experience of the present Earl of Selborne was not a happy one. He had used language about Irish matters on a provincial platform, and these were made the basis of a "scene "in which the young member cut but a poor figure. Few who witnessed that occasion would have been willing to predict that the day would dawn when the then Lord Wolmer would be a risen hope of the unyielding" Die-Hards.</p>
        <p>• Nor in the days when he was sowing his Parliamentary wild-oats could anyone have foreseen that the day would come when the Earl of Selborne would be a strenuous defender of the rights of the Upper House. In conjunction with two other heirs of peerages he penned a lament against the cruel fate which was to condemn him to an I assembly which was not the less a cage "because its bars are gilded." Lord Selborne indeed carried his protest against translation to the point of trying to sit in the Commons after he had acceded to the peerage—a protest that ended in a not altogether dignified exit. 4. •</p>
        <p>The attack on the Government and on the principle of payment of members was led by Viscount Wolmer, the son of Lord Se'borne. The peculiar claim of this young man to champion the principle of services freely rendered to the State without pay or profit was brought out in an interesting speech later on by Mr. Philip Snowden, who has been making same investigations into figures, and who finds that in the course of 33 years 24 relatives of Lord Selborne have taken as salaries or pensions some £316.000. Mr. Snowden has also looked up the record of some more aristocratic families, and he finds that 532 of such families have found 13,888 offices for 7,911 relatives, and they have taken altogether out of the taxes some £108,000,000. Well might Mr. Snowden suggest that it was time the democracy had a turn. .</p>
        <p>• " There are numbers a foolish people who are inclined to blame the Government or Mr. Lloyd George's Limehouse speech for the industrial war, while others, equally foolish, see in it an argument for Tariff Reform. The miseries of people who complain that they are unable to get sufficient food under present conditions are not to be assuaged by a policy designed to make all food dearer. Nor will the pretence that Tariff Reform is a relief from the present difficulties of the workers appeal to people who have the evidence of the Continent before them. High duties did not prevent an almost general railway strike in France. Nor have they given the workers either of Germany or of France conditions which will excite any envy</p>
        <p>in this country. The people of England have had painful evidence during the last few days that barriers to the free movement of produce result in an immediate increase in the cost of everything they buy.</p>
        <p>- Compulsory arbitration may be possible in England when the working classes feel that they can rely absolutely on the impar- tiality of the tribunal that is set up.—Mr. Perry Alden, M.P. at Tottenham. There is nothing so overrated as a sense of humour—especially in a husband.—Miss Marion Ashworth in the " Smart Set." Teachers should train the minds of children by exercising them upon subjects that can be made use of in life.—Mr. H. T. Cawley, M.P., at Stand. If nature's methods had free play there is no doubt that she would in some centuries evolve a superman.—Sir James Barr at Dublin. _ .</p>
        <p>There is nothing in the history of this country of which we can all be more proud than the origin, growth, and success of our great friendly societies.—Lord St. Aldwyn at Oxford.</p>
        <p>. i Religion s a source of power to all our great social reforms.—Mr. J. T. White at Barnstaple.</p>
        <p>LOCAL PARS. S Nrs3</p>
        <p>Royal Court Theatre. Next week the boards at the Royal Curt Theatre will be occupied by Mr. and Mrs. Chas. Draycott ía that popular drama "The heart bowel down."</p>
        <p>WRING ISO MACHINES, best value obtainable. Cash pries, 996.—J S. GARLAND, Ironmonger Bridge et., Warrington.—Advt. Palace and Hippodrome. Michael Richardini's troupe of sensational gymnasts bead what promises to be an excellent bill for next week. C,orman's Juveniles (12 in number) will present their pretty and refined vocal scena, and the rest of the turns will be of exceptional interest.</p>
        <p>The Late Mr. R. A. Naylor. Miss Naylor, The Towers, Beeston, has sent us a copy of a pleasing souvenir of her brother, the late Mr. R. A. Naylor. The souvenir comprises an -excellent portrait of the deceased gentleman, and some typical verses by him on '• Fliendship," the whole being tastefully printed 03 silk, and enclosed ;tetween art boards bearing the Naylor monogram. FOLEY CHINA TEA SERVICES, 4a pieces 12.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge st, Warrington.—Advt. Workers __ Educational Association and . _</p>
        <p>University Education. The Provisional Committme of this scheme reso'ved on Thursday to form a class immediately on special terms, advantageous to the students, for an experimental course on Industrial History. Names of intending students, male or female, should be sent in at once to Mr HA Webb, 116, Padgate-lane, joint -secretary pro tern, of the Workers' Educational Association. An open meeting will be held Tuesday, fSeptember sth, 8 p.m, at the Academy-street Adult -school, to make final arrangements.</p>
        <p>A few soiled BABY CARRIAGES to be sold obese.— Apply J. B. GARLL.ND, 51, Bridge et., W gtoo.-AdTt. Royal Cs-Operative Collecting Society. . .</p>
        <p>gr. George Wooldri Ige, the rical superintendent of the above society entertained his agents and friends to a tea, supper sod sociil on Mood iy nig'it in cones- &lt;pence of his resigning his position owing to ill-health. II After tsa a capita musical programme was sub- I utitted by Mr. J. Dickenson and included a recitation by Mr. RiMardson, tenor songs by Mr. J. Clayton, Bong by Mr. S. L. Arthur, humorous and concertina items by Mr. Sara Aukland. M. J Bellamy was Vie scampi:ties. Mr. J. Dickens It (inspictor to the society) spoke in the highest terms of the good work perforated by Mr. Wooldriige as also did several agents who all expressei deep regret at losing their late master. Mr. Wooldridge's successor will be Mr. J. Bellamy. MOTOR CARS FOR HIRE, long or short distances.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. .Union of Clerks. The monthly meeting of the Warrington Branch of the National Union of Clerks wis held on Monday August 14th, at the Labour Exchange. The chief business was the consideration of the minutes of the Executive Council meeting held at Birmingham, 15:h July. Present : Messrs G E O'Dell (chairman), W .7 Armstrong, 1' E Barker, Miss M Brodie, Messrs F Hughes, W J Read, F Wroe, 1, Whitehead, the general sec-e'ary, and the assistant general secretary The Branch Secretary reported that the branch was making satisfactory progress, having members in Colborne and Northrrich. lie also informed them that the General Secretary had sent circulars to all Municipal Clerks of Warrington, but up to the present he had had no response, and he hoped that before very long he would be able to enrol them, in order to enable us to try and get the adoption of our minimum wage for Corporation clerks.</p>
        <p>CLEARANCE SALE OF CYCLES, all prices reducscl.—J S . GARLAND, 51, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
