      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>SAD BATHING FATALITY</head>
        <p>SAD BATHING FATALITY</p>
        <p>Two Lads Drowned at Warrington.</p>
        <p>Two inquests were held by Coroner Brighouse at the Felice Station yesterday afternoon on the bodies of Barry Laden) (14) and Daniel Callaghan (12), both of whom were drowned while bathing to the Mersey near Messrs. Monks Hall and Co,'. wharf on Monday lea. Annie Luken, wife of Frederick Lad rm, Al, Lloyd street, said she last saw her son alive about 213 on Monday last, when he left the house stating that he was going to get some clover for his rabbits. Deceased was a very good swimmer, and the boy Callaghan and he were friends.</p>
        <p>Daniel Callaghan, hell porter at Messrs. Crosfields, living at 152, Wane Ad-street, mid he last saw his son live between noon and one o'c'ock on tfonday. He had beard that his eon was a swimmer but he could not way for certain. Thomas Artbor Kendrick, 29, Mill-lane, g-rid he met -the two deceased and they went for a swim near to Messrs. Hooke works. There was about eight r ; them -altogether. After 'Callaghan had been swimming for a while he seemed to get into difficuhies and cried for help. !Adam went to his assistance end they struggled for a bit and then Callaghan floated away and !Adam seemed to sink where he was.</p>
        <p>Alfred Spencer aid "lohn-.lWilliam Brown spdke to recovering the bodies: Daniel Callaghan said he wished to express his sincere gratitude to Mr. flatton and the men who worked so hard in trying to recover his eon's body. The Coroner remarked that the lads could not be blamed for going bathing in the hot weather. The j try returned a verdict of " Acc.dentally Drowned."</p>
      </div>
