      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>More Debates Wanted.</head>
        <p>More Debates Wanted.</p>
        <p>Sir,—lt was with considerable interest I read Mr. England's letter in last week's" Examiner" and his comments on the debate that took place at Bridge Foot between Mr. H. New, of Warrington, and Mr. Henshall, of Manchester. I was present and fully ag ee with Mr. England that it would be a good thing if another meeting could be arranged betwesn the two gentlemen or any other representative of the Tariff Reform League but I would ga further and suggest a meeting of that character should take place in every ward. lam sue if an opponent could be fout.d t 3 meet Mr. New he would gladly agree to this suggestion. I have not consulted him, hut as a member of the Liberal Party in Warrington, I am sure he and the party are most anxious that the public should have both sides of this important issue placed before them. Trusting means can be found to bring this auggestion to a successful issue.—Yours faithfully, JOSEPH DUTTON. _</p>
        <p>19, Grafton at., Warriogton. August 15th. 1911.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>Dissatisfied !</head>
        <p>Dissatisfied !</p>
        <p>Sir,— The courtry seems to me at the present time to be in a very anomalous condition. We have had a , great agitation going on for some few years against the fl nine of Lords, and the present Governmont boa succeeded in placing the heel of the House of COMMOIM on the neck of the Lords. We find that his Majesty's faithful Commons, w;th the true instinct of the proverb that "charity begins at home," have exeicised their new found libeity to vote thems3lves a sa'ary of considerably more than XI a day. We find in onr large towns strikes and ri3ts and the people dragooned by mlituy end p lice. The peaceful inhabitauts find their supplies of food getting scarcer and dearer. AU this under a great beneficent Lib:ral Government. We seem as if someone had smvn the wind and nos we are reaping the whirlwind. Can you t-11 rue, Mr. E.lit.,,hoa long the storm will last ? Hos soo3 we shall get into calm water? And, h .n- -soon we shall hue a Government wh ae first principle will be the greatest good d the freatest number, peace not strife, happineha and unity sot division? I rem3in yturs ,</p>
        <p>Plll4 SQUOD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>National Service League Critics.</head>
        <p>National Service League Critics.</p>
        <p>Sir.—Tnur paper rarely fails to provide int resting paragrallis aalied with editoi ial humour in the columns headed "Note.; and Noticns." L St Saturday you gave your readers an extract fr mo a monthly called " The Arbitrator," containing a paltry I.lt at Mr. Blatchford. •('n looking through my copy of "I he Arbitrator" I was surprised to see that your eyes bad failed to see an article following the paragraph quoted. This article was in heneral a violent attack on the National Service League and in par icular bitter personal abuse of local ironworkers oht support the league wit'i tongue and pen in the capacity of h n. helpers. I venture to poiut out this attack as one of topical interest, which did not deserve to be missed in your paper. There was little argument and much misrepresenta- tion in the attack, but it is important, for it shows that Warrington is influencing public thought to so large an extent that twice in one issue a Londin monthly sees fit to jibe at certain local citizens. The reason is clear :</p>
        <p>I. The N.S. L. is very pwerful in South Lancashire. 2. Many of cur most energetic helpers are staunch Liberals</p>
        <p>3. Disregarding the prejudiced advice of their paid officialg, many Md., Unionists are publicly .express. Mg their belief in the democratic system advocated by the N.S.L._ . .</p>
        <p>4. The influence of men like M. J. Elwell of the Associated Iron and Steel Mokera of Great Britain is so widespread that organs supported by men with little British blood in their veins are trying to kill National Service by telling lies. This attack is one of the greatest compliments paid to the local working men, who during the last few years have sacrificed much time and energy in Warrington to the National Service League. It signifies that the p iid officials begin to tremble at the advent of a citizen a iny in which every Britisher shall lake his place, regardless of class or wealth and perform common duties and share common privileges. The Hon. Helpers in this town are the pioneers; they are attacked because they are making tO3 rapid headway.—Yours &amp;c., B. S. TOWNROE. The Tariff Reform Picnic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>Lady Greenall and Cannelliar Roberts.</head>
        <p>Lady Greenall and Cannelliar Roberts.</p>
        <p>Sir,—As Mr. Roberts has thought fit to comment on the corresp ndence which has been published, I pro- pose to make a fow comments on my pat. Mr. Roberts refers to a similar riquest made by the late Councillor John Magian. Does be suggest that there is any analogy between a Co onation Treat to upwards of 9,000 children, selec ed impartially from the whole of Warrington, and a picnic given by a town councillor to the children of his own ward t) anticip3te or celebrate his own Oection ?</p>
        <p>On Wednesday, 12;h July, Mr. Roberts sent fc,r Mr. Stansfield and asked him to approach the teachers. I saw Me. Stansfield and he told me he would do to). On Wednesday, 12th July, Mr. Roberts rang me up on the telephone. I was engaged at the time. He then asked my butler if Mr. Stansfield had been to Fee me. My butler came and told me, and I aat a message to Mr. Roberts that if he wanted ts know anything I should be obliged if he would write to ms. On the 14th, Mr. Stansfiel I communicated to me that if I would write Mr. Longshaw the matter would probably be settled. I did Cu, at the same time writing to Mr. Roberts (my letter has already been published). Mr. Longshaw informed me as the result of th.ir meeting that the teachers bad expressed a unanimous wish to do all they possibly could to help me. It was after this resolution they learnt Mr. Roberts was sending out requests for the lists to be sent to him. I WAS certainly not aware of this fact when I wrote Mr. Longshaw and Mr. Longattaw was evidently not aware of it when he called the meeting, , •,,</p>
        <p>Mr. Fulton Smith has neither written nor seen me in connection with the Picnic.</p>
        <p>If anyone attempted to get behind the backs of the Educatiol Committee surely it was Mr. Roberts by writing to the head teachers, after the Law Clerk, the Director of Education and Mr. Roberts as Chairman, had decided that it would be improper to comply with my request. Was this done with the approval of the Law Clerk and Director of Education?</p>
        <p>I It appears from the colrespondence that the leiter ' was not brought before the Education Committee until the 17th July, although I wrote on the oth July I expressly asking for this to be done, and on the sth July Mr Roberts had definitely refused to comply with my request. I should like to know whether there was a meeting of any of the School Management Committee on any date between July Bth and 17th, and if so why was not my request mentioned to them. The resolution as passed by the Education Com- mittee expresses approval of the action of the Chair- man, but it does not show that the committee clearly understand what that action had been.</p>
        <p>It appears to me that Mr. 'likens at first took upon himself to refuse my request without referring it to the Committee, and that afterwards the fear of unpopularity with his fellow-townsmen induced him to try to irregularly comply with a request which he still alleges to have been an improper one. The public must judge whether he has observed " the best traditions which appertain to the holding of public office in this country." As to Mr. Roberts reiterated remark that my request to help the children was an improper one al d ought never to have been made, I will leave the decision to the judgment of the pwents of the children, the children and the public of War- rington, and ask if they wuld have been satisfied if I had calmly accepted Mr. Roberts' prompt refusal. In conclusion I must congratulate Mr. Roberts on the very able manner in which he blows his own trumpet. —Yours faithfully, FRANCES E. GREENILL,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>August 17th, 1911. Walton Hall, Warrington.</head>
        <p>August 17th, 1911. Walton Hall, Warrington.</p>
        <p>Cycling near Lage Annecy (France) recently. a man was attacked by an eagle, receiving a blow on the head which partly stunned him. The realisation of the first portion of lila Birkback Bank properties took place on Thursdey at the Mart, Tokenhouse Yard, London. The Bile realised nearly 00,000.</p>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS. Chambers—Naylor, A fashionable wedding took place at the church of St. Boniface, Finsbury, Cheshire, on Tuesday, when Mr. Arthur Chambers, eldest sin of Mr. G. C. Chambers, Liscird, Cheshire, was married to Miss Elizabeth Anderton Naylor, eldest daughter of Mr. John Naylor (of the firm of Messrs. R. A. lsisylor, Ltd., Warrington), Beeston Towers, Tarporley Cheshire. It was an ideal summer Jay and the old church, flooded with bright sunshine, presented an exceedingly picturesque appearance. The bride and bridegroom are menahers of highly respected families and a large congregation assembled to witness the ceremony. The officiating clergy were the Rev. H. Woodman, Vicar of Banbury, and the Rev C. A. Gr.ffin, Vicar of Dunham-o'-thArld Mr. Jahn Naylor, jun., was at the organ, and played the folowing chobe programme in a very tasteful manner :—" Offertoire (" Lei Are- W ely), " Grand chorus in D Major (0 ailmant), " Bridal Chorus " (Wagner), '' Saint d'Amour " (E/gar) and the " Wedding March " (Mendel ssohn). Tie bride, who was given away by her. father, was charmingly attired in ivory satin, veiled wi.h ninon and lace, trimmed with orange bl Boom. She had a full Court train, also a tulle veil over a coronet of orange blossom, white heather and myrtle. She carried a lovely bouquet of cream roars and white heather, and her r nly ornament was an amethyst and pearl bracelet, the g ft of her mother. The bridesmaids were the Misses E C. and I. M. A. Naylor (sisters '1 the bride), Mies E M. Shaw, Harrow-on the-Hill; Miss F. M. Ainsworth, Mach Wenlock, Shropshire; and Miss Ent ais'e, Calve'ey, Cheshire. They wore very pretty dresses f p ale blue satin, veiled wall ninon, and whirrs crinoline hats trimmed with piok n.sea and f rget-me note. Their bouquets were composed of pink and white sweet peas, and their ornaments were gold a fetylin brooches with whole pearl the gifts the b idegroom. Mr.H.T. Chambers,broiher of the br d g oom, acted as best man The mother of the bride was attmed in porn-coloured s :tin, with rich beaded trimmieg, arid velvet and lace toque to match. The mother of the bridegroom was gowned in grey voile, trimmed sith satin. After the ceremony a reception was held at Beeston Towers. 150 guests being Fres its. Later the newly-married couple left for the Yorkshire coast where the honey- moon is being spent. The b ide travelle lira saxe blue linen dress, ir.th Irish crochet collar a d cuffs, tussore silk coat, and velvet and lace toque to match. The following is a list of presents : Bride's Father, cheque, carved oak sidoboard, kitcheo furniture Bride's Mother, gold bracelet, hensehold linen, eider- down, sofa, rug, Thermos flask, silver ani enamel after- noon teaspoons Miss L. K Naylor, Zeiss Benoslais silver tea servi.e J Naylor, Jon, silver cake basket J A Naylor, case of carvers, chest of drawers, window sent II A Minion Royal Staffordshire dinner service Miss E E Naylor, Royal Stafforistiie URI service Miss I M A Naylor, biscuit, cheese sad butter dish lair and Mrs Chambers, cheque Air and Mrs Fronk Morley (York), silver brand board Mr C E Ainsworth (Much WenlaJk) bread scoop Mrs Ains orth (Much Wenlock) bolster slips and lace Ali a F M Aiuswtrth, silver fruit dish Mr Tarry (London), book Mr and Mrs Ashton. wine glasses Mrs Grimshas (Stilton-on-Mersey), jardinle -re Mr and Mrs More (Dutton), jam pot Mr and Mrs Laurie, two flower vases Miss Laurie, handkerchief sr chat lan Robertson Smith, syphon stand Mr Barbe-, Sutter eish Misses Ainsworth, hand pained tabli centre Mine Doris Aksaorth, tons], handmade lace and initials Mrs Bussock-Slaw afternoon tea clo'.h,lace corners and border Miss EM Shaw, plant stand Mr and Mrs Skinner (A arrive:en), silver teaspoons and tongs Mns Walter Lucas (London), silver serviette rings lair Alfred and Miss Skinner, silver flowar vases Mr and Mrs Suffield (Ilkeby), silver salt cellars Miss Simpson and Miss Bartlett (Alasick), silver salt cellars Mrs Wilson sod Miss Verity (Filey), biscuit barrel Mr and Mrs Jones (Liverpool), cut glass L silver jug Mr and Mrs Henry Crocker (London), travelling clock Miss Entwisle, afternoon tea cloth and out crackers Mr end Mrs Huta-isle. inlaid ro_ewood cloak Mr W Entwisle, toast rack Mrs Smethurst, silver afternoon teaspoons Dr Archer and Mrs Archer, china vase Dr Robertson, old glass vase Mrs Robertson, Aberdeen terrier Misses Robertson, silver mostsrd pot Maids at Beeston Towers, toilet service and set of Bien Mr Griffiths, silver photo frame Lady Tollemache, gilt photo trams Dr Ede Worcester silver ash tray Nurse Gabble, Liverp sot, tray cloth Dr and Mrs Marshall, bearskin rug The Rev and Mrs Woodman, Indian cloth Mr and Mrs Armstrong, plate basket Mies Mortal Reynoldson, (Hull), silver muffineers Miss Vickers, Stourport, Worcester china vase Mrs Morley, York, table cover, fish knives and fork Mr W and IV Motley, fish carvers Mr Mevywcather, book Rev and Mrs Griffin, bra set Mrs and Mr VI iggins, surprise light and :hale Mr Startles, silver auger sifter Mr and Alfa AlcConnel, silver egg stand Mr aid Mrs Gleave, cheque Mr sod Mrs Slaw (Bunbury), bon-ban dishes Miss Lloyd, tray cloth Mrs Keith Douglas, silver hair pin box Miss Coppltsions, Honiton hat pins Miss Heat in, tab'e centre Mr and Mrs Sturgess. (Northwicb), centre pieta Bee J J James, weather glass Mies Martin, pincushion Mr Rearnont, cht rpm Mr Fraser f Eartoum) cheque Mrs Roberts, table and cushion covers Mr and Mrs Maconnell. travelling clo,:k Messrs and Miss Byrd, crumb snoop Mr W B 3 ash (Edinburgh), muffin dish Mr and Mrs Byrd, silver afternoon tea knives M- and Mm Shakeshaft, (Warrington) silver fruit service Mr C L JOlllOl., silver candlesticks Mr and Mrs H 0 Dickson (Balton) silver serviette rings Mr and Mrs Morton. silver and enamel candlesticks Mr end Mrs ElBOll, glass dishes Mrs and Miss Thompson, travelling clock Mr Barber, butter dish Dr and Mrs Sergeant (Preston), knife machine Miss Tullis, Letherhead, Old Dutch spoons Mr and Mrs It Blower (Church Stratton), Sheffield, plate tee caddy Mr and Mrs B W Blower (Shrewsbury), picnic basket Miss Elmay Ellis, tea tray Hatton—Thompson. A wedding of considerable interest in the district ' was solemnized on Tuesday afternom at St. Thomas' , Church, Stockton Heath, when Miss Annie Thompson, of Whitefield-road, Stockton Heath, and only daughter of the late Mr. Thompson, of Orford, and Mr. Frank Hatton, seco il son of Mr. James Hatton, of Hill Cliffs. The Rev. James Collins (vicar) officiated and Mr. Pilling of Golborne, presided at the organ and played Mendelesohn's Wedding March. The bride who was given sway by her brother, Mr. J. 0. Thompson, was attired in a dress of ninon over silk and white picture hat. Her bouquet consiated of white roses, her bridesmaid was Miss Nora Thompson (niece) who wore an Empire dress of mu.lin over oink satin, with a cap to match. She carried a basket of sweet peas. Mr. George Ha•ton (brother of the bridegroom) discharged the duties of best man. The reception was held at the home of the bride's brother " Grosvenor " Whitefield-road, Stockton Heath. Late in the afternoon the happy couple left for Dunoon where the honeymoon is being spent. The bride travelled in a tailor made blue coat and skirt with a hat to match.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>WWI ARE WITNESSES WORTH? _ Coroner Brighouse's Opinion.</head>
        <p>WWI ARE WITNESSES WORTH? _ Coroner Brighouse's Opinion.</p>
        <p>Addressing the witnesses at an inquest held at the Police Station yesterday, the Coroner (Mr. Samuel Brighouse) stated that he was very sorry, but owing to the rules and regulatiois f the cmmty he was not allowed to pay them more than Is. for their attendance He thought it was a scandal that they should be called away from their in..iness for such a sum, and all the coroners in Larcashire agreed that it was unfair, and they were endeavouring to rectify it. A meeting of the coroners was held at Manchester the other day when it was unanimously decided to bring the matter before the County Council and he hoped that they would decide to give a generous allowance to witnesses. They must not blame the coroners however as they felt perfectly ashamed of themselves.</p>
        <p>ALTERATION AT THR FILF.CUTTIRA' AR)l9.—Alder- , man Bolton applied for permission to make alterations at the Filecutter's Arms, lie stated that the altera- tions were for better living accommointioo, and better sanitary conditions. They were not going to increase the drioking accommodation bat the alterations would provide for the family to be entirely separate. There was no objection on the part of the ',sauce, the Chief Constable stating thst the tenants would lasve a far better opportunity of living in decency than they now possessed. The spplication was granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>IRISH AFFAIRS. NOTES FOR IRISH READERS.</head>
        <p>IRISH AFFAIRS. NOTES FOR IRISH READERS.</p>
        <p>ATIGUINI WITH FALSE PROPHETS.</p>
        <p>Some reader of the" Examiner " was good enough to send us the other day a copy of a weekly journal— the ‘' Bolton Chronicle "—in which appeared an article purporting to show how well-grounded were its fears of the Protestant Minority in regard to Home Rule. We desire to pay our acknowledgments to the unknown sender for introducing us to the lut id licture which the article painted of the intolerance which Protestants are alleged to be likely to suffer under Home Rule and its supposed oppressive Catholic majority. It is as true to-day as it was in Grattan's day when the Irish statesman said it was " impossible to argue with a prophet—o le could only refuse to believe him " and this prophecy of religious intoletance order Home Rule, uttered as it is on platforms up and down the country by an army of paid speakers sod canvassers, is of such a vague and netulous character as to make its refutaf on no simple matter. There is nothing to " get your teeth into." But whilst we think the melodramatic terrors nor so highly coloured as to be ridiculed even by Unionists themselves, may well hr left to the contempt which we saw masted out to them only the other day ii the Mi Idletin bye-electioo, we think it might be relevant to submit a few opinions publicly expressed by lead- ing Protestants in various parts of Ireland Lord Spencer, one f the roost single-minde 1 and devoted N'iceroys Ireland has had, speaking at Chester in 1883, after close persanal knowledge of the country in trying times said : " I have bad some experience of Ireland, aid yet I do not know any specific instance where there has been the exercise of reliaious intolerance on the part of the Roman Catholics against their Proteetant fellow-country men." Where bitter religious arimosity had sheen itself, Earl Spencer stated it was `• not in those pr Ninres where tt e Roman Catholics greatly pre- ponderate, but in Ulster where more than ODO half of the population belonged to the Protestant faith." It may be argued that this described the Ireland of 1886 twenty-five years ago—when the possibilities of religious iotuleranee were much less than those of the present day. The statements of the Irish Viceroy of 11880 are more than c affirmed in the public utterances of his pressotsuccesaor in office, the Earl of Aberdeen Lord Abaideen, ex-high c ornmhAtioner of the General Assembly of the Church of Scotland, wired the follow- ing messages to Mr. J. M. Henderson, Al P , during the general electirtn in D.cember, 1010. "You are entirely at liberty to quote m o as declaring to you that, after years of continuous residence in Ireland, watching affairs and meeting people of every class and creed, lam profoundly convinced as to the baseless- ones of alarm about the consequences of Home Rule." "On II .mo lisle for Ireland, I repeat and emphasise the opinion of my former telegrams, espeeiall regard- ing api rehensi on of religious intolerance. Numerous Protestant ministers in Roman Catholic parts of Ireland sopport me in this view." To bring the testimony to a later date still, the dis- tinguished nobleman who to-day leads the Liberal frees in the House of Lords, where he is honoured by both parties for his political amity and restraint, the Marqu;s of Crewe, a former Lord Lieutenant of Ir.-land, wrote as recently as June 12th, 1911, in the collo ving terms : "It is evident that a renewed attempt is to be m ide to alarm public opinion in England, Scotland and Wales by excited forecasts of the dangers and disabilities which Protestants toy expect to undergo un ler a system of Home Rile for Ireland. In 188(1 and 1503 the animosity between the classes, largely agrarian in its origin, was far stronger that it is it present, and the line of cleavage roughly followed that of religious difference. But even in those years, as I well remember, it was evidenced that the possi- bilities of intolerance in a self governed Ireland were deliberateg and geosely exaggerated, with a party motive. Now, when the various chasms know each other better, and there is less occasi n for friction, the attempt to excite religious discord will utterly fail, as I firmly believe. Even in the old days, I often heard these baseless fears ridiculed by sensible Unionists, and I cannot doubt that the number is now far greater of those who are willing toexamine Home Rule on its merits, as a piece of politic d machinery, without endeavouring to raise a cloud of mistrust and prejudices. Each of the testimonies given above represents the convietioh of men who have had close personal knowledge of the country and its conditions. In a later article we propose to cite the statements of representative Irish Protectionists of all parties and all walks of life to refute what is proloably the basest calumny ever perpetrated in the interests of a mentally bankrupt political party.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>LIFE IM THE CHILDREN'S CAMP AT PRESTATYN.</head>
        <p>LIFE IM THE CHILDREN'S CAMP AT PRESTATYN.</p>
        <p>With a family of 120 boys and 8 adult officers, there is much to be done, but in excellent weather, such as we have had during the past week, -.fork is made lighter. Faces and legs exposed to the sun have become tanned. Appetites have grown in proportion to the desire to sleep. It is really an experience to live with a family of this sort for a couple of weeks to be knocked up by a proportion of the youngsters at 5 am., their clear voices ringing out in the quiet morning air; and as the sun shoats up behind the hills you are reminded that another day awaits you, made up of its responsibility, frolic, fun and duty. Era another night's repose, something will be written deeply in the minds of Warrington lads which many a milestone of life wi 1 never erase Breakfast at eight is prepared by a number of helpers, atti then a batch of lads is sent to peel potatoes ; another to wash pots, while the rest busy themselves putting beds and blankets oit to air and brushing and scrubbing huts. The lads takes keen interest in this as a prize is given each day for the best-kept hut. There is always much t he done by th whi give their services to the lads. A bank is kept on the ground and any lad possessing coppers can bank the money with the officer in charge of his particular hut,' and as each post comes in bringing a few stamps or an odd postal order he changes this with the superin- tendent and banks the money. A shop is kept and this is quite a busy affair, chocolato, rock, and post cards being sold. The bank is open for withdrawal purposes once a day when the shopkeeper his a busy time. Another important but Serious side of a day's life in camp is the ambulance department. We are equipped with a medicine chest and as it is common ' for lads to get sunburn, wasp stings, cuts and splinters, the first-alders are kept busy,but it is grand to be able to do this in the nick of time, and to allow the little chaps to enjoy themselves. The other day we had a batch of 25 lads stung with wasps, but they were soon alright after treatment. Change of air and food make other treatment necessary. A whistle is the signal for all things, and the youngsters, though not organised before a frw days ago, respect instruction, on the whole admirably. Games of all sorts are engaged in throughout the day, ant it does ones heart good to see the young holiday makers having the best out of it. A stroll from one side of the camp ground to the other will bring you in contact with a humorous mixture, the "box of his hut" taking spite out of the footba'l, and moat likely you will see a one-legged cripple without his crutch, it having been distarded or smashed, and off he goes and smartly too; the little quiet fellow who ha, never a word to say in Hut PI sits writing a post-cardl to mother and wants to know how baby and all the rest are, and if father is working. A bit further on you see the proud possessor of a halfpenny' and he polishes it in Prestatye sand ' thinking it will buy a biggar stick of rock, but the " worst and best " aide of the trip is to see a poor little chap looking on with longing eyes and wstering teeth, as be sees his hut mites buying in when the shop is open. This is the time for getting the loose halfpennies out of your pockets, for the camp is for. those little fellows who have no c3ppers, and thanks to the good spirited folk who come to see us, we can generally get some for them. A visit to the various huts being made all sorts and sizes of bundles will be fund. The handkerchiefs would require washing, but that matters litt'e. There is a stick of rock for, Sally and Tommy and some shells on a string for baby, that eager little feet have paddled for on a stocking filled av,th queer shaped and many coloured stones. There are the presents for all at home, and the last halfpenny was spent on a p-stcard to sty they were coming. Bat whit a pleasure it is for the little chap who hrus spent a week at the sea- side. When Frilay evening comes, the youngsters will be seen with lir Jiro and happy faces carrying off these simple presen's. Best of all they carry happy memories; whlch the Warrington childrens camp has provided.</p>
        <p>The following are bits front typical post cards sent off by the lads.— I am enjoying myself very much. Mr. is the bank man, and I have put my money in. so it is quite safe. I have got lots of shells and other thing and am having good fun." " Dear M.—l am getting good food and am treated kindly." "Dear A.--I got my stumps changed I wanted to come home yesterday, but now I like it very well." "Dear M.—Want you to send me some money. How is the baby and all the res.. This is all I have to 33y at present, deliver your letter to but 5." J J F.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>CYCLIST GETS £3O DAMAGES. Collision With Waggonette.</head>
        <p>CYCLIST GETS £3O DAMAGES. Collision With Waggonette.</p>
        <p>-- William Arthur flazeldine, aged 20 years, living in Colborne-street, Warrington, cuing by William Haze!dine, in father, of 7, Peel-street, Edge Hill, Liverpool, claimed at the Warrington County Court on Thurs lay, from Harry Wilton, carriage proprietor, of Cross-lane, Earlestown, for £3O for damages for personal injuries suitained through the alleged negligence of the defendant or his servant in driving a waggonette in Wiowick-road, between Winwick end Earlestown, on the Nth April, when the plaintiff was knocked off his bicycle. Mr C McLean (instructed by Messrs G J Lynkey and Son. Liverpool), appeared for the plaintiff, and Mr A Browne represented the defendant.</p>
        <p>The plaintiff in giving evidence said that be, along with Miss Clare of Flag-lane. Warrington, went to Eirlestown on Good Friday evening on their cyclee end It was dark when they returned. When reaching the railway bridge in the road mentioned he saw two waggonettes coming down the bridge. At that point the road was 10 yards wide and he passed by the fl-st waggonette all right, but as he was going to pass the second it swerved as if it were going to page the first waggonate. It was then that he was knocked off Iris bicycle, permanently d &amp;laming bin face, ruining his suit of clothes and smashing his new cycle. Mies Clam said she was going up the bridge in front of the plaintiff and as soon as cbe passed the second waggonette, she now it swerve sod tun into the plaintiff. Harry Francis, Walter Thninni, and John B ill gave evidence on behalf of the defence. Tne latter said he was formerly in the employ of Mr. Walton, and on the day in question be WAS returning from Croft with a waggonette. lie reme nbered seeing plaintiff and Mica I Clare coming up the bridge. As soon as the latter passed bin wagg motto he noticed the pliintiff leave the left side of the road and come over to where his waggonette was standing. Witness shonted to those in the waggonette that the plaintiff wan going to run into them. Ile did not shout to the plaintiff, who ran into the waggonette. Witness jumped off the waggonette after the accident and picked the cyclist up. Thinking that the plaintiff who was on his wrong side was going to ran into him witness pulled up. By Mr. afiaL -an : There was no truth in the state- ment that he swerved.</p>
        <p>John Thorn, waggon maker at the Viadnot Works. Newton-le-Willows, ssid he was on the box of the third waggonette with the last witness. The three waggonettes were proceeding at a walking pace. The driver was on his proper side of the road, hat the plaintiff was not and when witneas saw the plaintiff he teusarked to those in the waggonette that Hazeldine was going to run into them. He had no sooner said that than the plaintiff in trying to get to the right side of the road, ran into the horse and was krocked down. The driver could not have avoided the accident. The jury after bait an hour's absence returned a verdict for the plaintiff with damages amounting to £3O, allocating £2.0 for personal injuries sustained and £lo for damaged bicycle, suit and loss of wages. Mr. Brown said the case might go to another court.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>BOROUGH ACCOUNTS.</head>
        <p>THE BOROUGH ACCOUNTS.</p>
        <p>Auditors' Report.</p>
        <p>The Borough auditors bar presented the following report on their audit of the accounts of the Corporation We beg to report having au‘liMed the Books of the Corpolotion for the year ended 31st March, 1911, excepting the Edacation Committee Accounts,audited by the Local Government Board, and have pleasure in certifying the Treasurer's Abstract of Ac.muuts as prepared therefrom. The melt received has been checked with the counterfoils of the receipts book, the rate books have been proved with the payments into the bank, reconciled therewith, and the balance of cash in band veri lied.</p>
        <p>The Committees concerned have authorized all payments daring the year, these have been vouched and the allocations traced to the accounts.</p>
        <p>The contributions due to the various Einking Funds have been provided out of Revenue. The Local Government Board have sanctioned Loans during the year as follows ; Private Street Works, seven years Erection of Evelyn Street Schools, forty feat Furnishing of Evelyn-street Schools, ten years :£1,077 Water Main, thirty years do. Electricity, seventeen years and the official documents authorising these have been sighted. Caacelled Certificates have been produced. We have inspected the Title Deeds of Properties owned by the Corporaf on as shown in the audited accounts, sighted the Scrip representing Investments, and satisfied ourselves as to the Insurance of existing risks.</p>
        <p>£12,519</p>
        <p>.t 13,833</p>
        <p>The Stocks of 111eriAls on hand have been va'ued and certified by your Officials, and the 13Jok Debts are considered to be good and recoverable. The practice adopted of crewing a Reserve Fund in the Trading Departments is in our opinion highly desirable so as to provide for the obsolescence of AtFels.</p>
        <p>We are pleased to report that the Books are well and efficiently kept and we notice in the Gas Depart- ment many improvements in the form and modelling of the Books •, when these are completed the whole system of the Corporation Accounts will be on a modern basis.</p>
        <p>In conc'usion we have to acknowledge the courtesy and promptness which have characterized the response of the Officials and Staff of the Corporation to our numerous requiremen's. We are, Gentlemen, Yours fa,thfullis BERTRAM F.C.A. 1 Borough THOMAS PEMBERTON, J Auditors. WILLIAM J. WILSON, Mayor's Auditor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>Lymm.</head>
        <p>Lymm.</p>
        <p>liiheugo FROM DROWN' V6.—Ort Monday. whilst Leonard Lookyer, aged four years, sonof Mr. Frederick Lockyer, Star ton, Statham, woe playing near the Bridgewater Canal, a dog he was leading pulled him in, bat fortunately the boy woe rescued by a man named Frank Walsh.</p>
        <p>UNITIO MISSION RAND.—AD open.air nervice an held on Sunday afternoon at the entrance to the Dingle. The addressn were given by Mr. Stephen Ackerley (Lymm) sod Mr. Llewellyn (Warrington). Mr. J. W. Dowownod rendered a solo "Came unto Me" in a most effective manner. Miss Eleanor Goodier presided. In the evening a service was held at the Cross, the address being given by Stephen Acknrley (Lymm) and Mr. Waterworth presided. MOTOR AND CYCLIST COLLIDR-00 SLOW., evening about seven o'clock when a cyclist was rounding the bend near the church he failed to notice a motor which wee coming in the opposite direction. The cyclist being on his wrong aid, tried to get on his right aide and the motor trying to avoid him ran into the lams .me/thing it in four pieces, the motor being greatlr d trnaged as well. The cyclist was pitched clean over tbe motor, being neve-ely hurt, his leg being broken above the knee. Ile was taken to hnaditol by motor car and is now prngrering . .</p>
        <p>UNITED Tais.—Tbe Lymm Primitive Methodists and Congregationalists held their annual trip on Nfoodty hat, when a good number journeyed to Blackpool. The start from Lyrnm was male about 0.30 a.m., Blimnpool being resthed at 9.30 after a weary journey. The day was spent amongst the different attractions which Blackpool is noted for. Toe day was gloriously fins. The re-start ;or Lymm wan begun at 8.35 p.m. and owing t 3 no ramy stoppages on the way, Lyram was not reached until elm sat midnight, everybody arriving safely. We must not forget to congratulate Mr W T Moss sad others ;for their</p>
        <p>egos-Rent itements. FOUND DROWNRD TN.:CEiT AND N./MEM-F.—Mr. R. A. White, deputy coroner, held an inquest at the Police Station, on Monday. coooering the death of Mary Walker, wife of jos Welker, of Brookfield Terrace, Lymm, who was found drowsed in Lymm Darn on Fad .y night.—Jos. Welker, husband of the deceased, said his wife was Ni years of age. He last sits her alive about 9 p.m. on Friday.—Sbe bad returned that day from the Manchester Royal Infirmary where she bad beee under treatment for nervous debility. She seemed very depresaed and Reid she thought she on. worse. About three month. since she said 'be would rather die than undergo the punishment she wee suffer- ing. She had never said ens thing to lead him to believe she might take sway ber life. A few minutes after nine bis daughter told him her mother had gone out and be I went to took for her. Ile saw the body token oet of Lyme Dam about midnight. The bat and stick fousd on tbe side of the Dam belonged to his wife.—Edward Kerfoot, Boothe Hill, and Segment Wasbingtoa spoke to recoveriog the body. A verd.ct of " found drowned" was returned as there was not sufficient evidence to *how bow the body got Into the water.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>STRIKE.</head>
        <p>THE STRIKE.</p>
        <p>HOPES OF A SETTLEMENT. TRANSPORT WORKERS</p>
        <p>SATISFIED.</p>
        <p>At a late hour last night the Conference in London was still sitting. We learn that the transport workers have settled their differences with the ship-Lwnera</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>WARSHIPS AT LIVERPOOL.</head>
        <p>WARSHIPS AT LIVERPOOL.</p>
        <p>2,000 MEN READY TO LAND. Electric Light The cruiser Antrim arrived in the Mersa on Thursday, and two other warships ar expected. The naval men will guard ware houses and docks, and will see that the strikers do not interfere with the running of the ferries.</p>
        <p>The danger of pestilence owing to the non-removal of refuse and the non-cleansing of the streets was commented upon by the chairman of the Health Committee, who hoped the scavengers would return to work. The position in the city grew worse by the partial success attending the strikers' efforts to bring out the workers in the Power Station. _</p>
        <p>The attempt to plunge the city into dark- ness created great alarm, but, fortunately, some of the workers remained loyal, and by completely cutting off the supply to private consumers the lighting of the streets was assured.</p>
        <p>Early in the evening the position was relieved by the Corporation securing sixty additional stokers.</p>
        <p>Every hotel,- -Private house, and police station was lighted by gas or candles. The theatres were closed.</p>
        <p>Many tramway workers came out on strike, and the service was greatly curtailed. The cars, however, were rarely filled, people preferring to walk home rather than risk injury from broken glass.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>MR. H W. MASSINGHAM AND THE SITUATION</head>
        <p>MR. H W. MASSINGHAM AND THE SITUATION</p>
        <p>The Reluctance to deal wish Labour. Mr. W. H. Massingham, the distinguished London journalist, writes as follows to the " Westminster Gazette." Sir, - I hope that members of the Liberal Party at least will not neglect to take a broad and sympathetic view of the crisis in the labour and railway worlds. The following eeem to me o be important and indeed guiding considerations. I. Labour hss beea caaght in the toils of an economic movement which bee been disastrous to it, and its effects have not been conducive to trade union action, nor as yet luta any effective machinery of relief been set up by the State, nor has the rice of prices been countervailed by a relief of indirect taxation.</p>
        <p>1. 'I he general rising of labour in face of the continuous tendency of real wages to decline is, there- fore, the only practical means of resort left to it, and has the specially valuable and important effect of making the community realize that it depends on labour, and that even a momentary withdrawal of this force distracts and confuses the whole nation.</p>
        <p>2. In special reference to the railway world, we are all of us made swine of the existence of them as .conservative and least sympaletic type of capitalist in the country. This element has had the handling of a new and complicated piece of machinery, and in the handling the men, whether right' or wrongly, have came t) think that they have been hardly dealt with, i , either with a direct evasive intention or n the spii it and temper of Mr. Legality. I. The case, therefore, is evidently one forcollogny between the heads of the orgenisthions cf Capital and Labour, direct( d by the one force capable of conduct- ing such intercourse namely, the King's Government 5. We keow that in their attitude both to trade unionism and the conciliation movement the railway directus have thrcughont exhibited the greatest reluctance to deal with the authorised representatives of Labour. They, therefore, reap where they have sown. They hare encouraged disorganisation, and they have got it, with its natural and full effects anal! parties—the Government, the general community, the social services, trade unionism, and capital, which all suffer .together. . . .</p>
        <p>The notion of a strike in sympathy is a token not of workmen's lawlessness or even depravity, but of a close feeling for each other, and a sense of a common lot, which is not possessed by the middle classes, but is nevertheless a great and touching social fact.</p>
        <p>I Let mo finally, remembering the past, beseech Liberal statesmen ti beware of the over-use of force. Tine brings long and deep revenges for such an error.—Ycurs,</p>
        <p>IT. W. MASSINGHAM.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>London, August A STRIKE AT WALTON LAY BY.</head>
        <p>London, August A STRIKE AT WALTON LAY BY.</p>
        <p>Lively Scene. On Tuesday the strike spread t 3 Walton Lay-by, between thirty and forty men who unload vessels leaving work to enforce their demand for "Manchester prices.' Yesterday morning, however, they rcsorned employment, an agreement wi,h the Ship Canal (om- pany having been arrived at. A lively scene occurred on 'Tuesday when an attempt was being made to unload the timber boat Iris. The vessel, which should have discharged her cargo at Liverpool, arrival at Walton on 3tu3day night, and fifty men obtained tallies t 3 carry out the unloading. They were pre- vented from perftrming their work the next day by' body of men who refused to allow tke labourers to enter the ship. The disrantents unharnessed the horses from the vehicles belonging to Messrs. Naylor, to whom a portion of the timber was consigned, and then the crowd threatened to throw the carters int) the canal. The carters retreated with the horses,sf ter which some disturbance took place. A number of police, who wee. sent for, made several arrvsta.</p>
        <p>LOCAL POLICE COURT SEQUELS. A sequel to the strike at Walton Lay-by on Tuesdsy VIVI heard at the Borough Court on Wednesday, when James !I, Boger-street ; John Loyd.., 14, Ellesmere-street ; James White, no sattled abode; Fred Archer, ludginghouss, 31, it,,. end Crown-street; Jamas i i His, no settled aoode, James Nlitchell, lodging-boast, 14, Birbauld-street, all unemployed with the exception of Loyden, were charged before Mr. C. White and other magistrates with having been drunk :d disorderly in A rpley.rotel, while Mitchell was also chsrgel with assaulting P.C. HewitL The Coird Constable said the previous day he received • notifica- tion that a strike had taken place at Walton Lay-by. A number of officers were despatched to the scene and it was ascertained that the prisoners were preventing the other men from unloading a hoot by using violent threats. A later report was received that • man named Whitcherby had been robbed of 41. I. It appeared that the complainant, who had been chinking, was lying near the scene of the disturbance and offered the prisoners Is. to boy some beer, of which they all par- took, and subsequently they robbed him. When arrested tbe prisoner Higham had Is. in his bad. P.C. Hewitt was endeavouring to get the money from bins when the pris 'Der Mitchell assaulted him and knocked bins to the ground. The Chief Constable In asking for a remand stated that about 70 or SO hooligans had that morning been to the lay-by and placed it in a state of turmoil, until the arrival of the police. If the prisoners were liberated they would probably be a source of rest annoyance to the inhabitants. The case was remanded for • week.</p>
        <p>- A further cure win beard on Tharaday, before Ifr. W. Roberts (in the chsiri, Mcsitrs. A. .1. Jo:ley, Edwin Woods, W. T. Williams and [Jr. Nulen, when Thorne, Monks, 21, Ellesmerustr•et, was charged with being drunk sod disorderly in Iluseometreet the previons day.—The Chief Coestable said the prisoner was one of the leaders of the gang of Si that visited the lay-by and caused such • deal of trouble. Prisoner was one of those customers who, if he was in • crowd, would cause no end of trouble. lie asked that the prisoser might be severely dealt with. as his behaviour was very bad, and his record worse—The Bench senteoced the prisoner to • month's hard labour without. the option of a use.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>RELIGIOUS e- SOCIAL</head>
        <p>RELIGIOUS e- SOCIAL</p>
        <p>Presentation to the Ray. T. A J. Ridpath. rhe Rey. A. J. Ridpath preached his farewell sermoon at the Warrington Parish Church and St. Georges Mission on Sunday, prior to leaving Warring- ton to take charge of the parish of St- Whelnetham, near Bury St. Edmunds, Suffolk, to the living of which be has been presented by Colonel Idockton. The rev. gentleamo beg been senior curate at the Parish Caurch, with special charge of St. George's Mission, for the past seven years and We departure will be regretted by the people amongst whom he has diligently laboured. The much-appreciated extension of the Mission, at a cost of £1,090, in a monument to his exertions. On Sunday morning Mr Ridpath occupied the pulpit at the Parish Church aid spoke of the privilege which it had been to him to declare no often the message of the gospel in that church. At the evening service at St. George's Mission, Mr. Ridpath preached from the words," We know that we have passed from death unto li(e,because we love the brethren. He that loveth not his brother abideth in death." I. John iii. 14. 113 laid he was thankful to God for enabling him to realize the fellowship of the Gospel in their little community at St. George's. He could not forget that their cam- malty had been helped censtantly during the time he had been in charge of the mission by the generous I assietence of wealthy friends. He would only refer to two, but not by name. One had the courage to per- form the colossal task of becoming the Lady Bouatiful of a town of over 70,000 inhabitants. They knew her very well and how greatly they Wk VS *misted in their funds by her. They also needled another lady woo visited that place week by week t 3 assist Mrs. Willis at the mothers' meetinge, and sale s alto took a great and personal interest in all that elonged to their welfare. And that led him to his next point, and that was that it would be well for England he was not speaking only of Warrington—if those who gave mGoetary help would also give their personal interest. They knew what angry pessions had been aroused during the past :sleek. To what was it all due. It was due in no small measure to the congress- tion of parties in their own great towns. All the wealthy people seemed to live outside the towns in pleasant places. He did not know of any process that could prevent that, but he knew of one force that would alleviate the evil, and that was the force of love, which brought the lady to whom he had referred every week into \Varrington to help at the mothers' meetings here. Love could bring people together, and he shou'd he grateful if any words ot his could induce some young man who was well off to work as a layman in that parish, and give his time, his thought and his parse to further the work of aia,l in the poorer parts of War- rington. Inc respect and Wren in which Mr. Ridpath is held by the people of St. George's found tangible expression on Monday evening when the rev, gentleman was pre- tented with a pocket wallet and a cheque. The Rector Rev. Canon Willis` is away on holidays but a warmly appreciative letter was received from him. The Rev. T. A. J. RIDPATH in returning thanks, said he WAS sorry to leave the pe3ple of Warriugtoo, but be teas looking forward to having a church, a house and gardens of hie own. Referring to the Rector, he said he did not think any clergy in England could have pleasanter relations with the incumbent than they had with the Rector. (Applause.) They had been most pleasiog throughout. Sir. Ridpath also referred to the help given him by Mr. Sumner and the sidesmen. The Sunday school teachers also had been s great help to him. He twin thanked them for the gift, which, he said, would be a symbol of their affection to him. Applause.) The Rev. A. J. Brett and Rev. T. Vickers testified to Mr.Ridpath's good work and wished him" God-speed." Mr. Ridpatb's successor will he Mr. Buckmaster, son of the Rev. Charles J. Backmaster, vicar of Hind:ey, Wigan. Mr. Buckmaster has been educated at Rossall and Keble College, Oxford, and Cuddesden Theological College. He is to be ordained a deacon by the Bishop of Liverpool at the end of September, and will come to Warriogt.on immediately after his ordination. He will have charge of St George's Mission. General Booth's Motor Tour. General Booth has decided to undertake another motor-car campaign. It will extend over 26 days, beginning on Sunday, August 20th, at Barry Dock, and ending with a public reception in London on the night of September 14th. Among the places to be visited after the departure from Barry Dock are : August 21st, Shepstow and Gloucester; 22nd, Evesham and Leamington ; 23rd, Nuneaton and Swadlincote ; 24th, Leek and Crewe; 25th, Northwich and Warrington; Sunday, 27th, Leigh (Lancashire); 28th, Altrincham and Ashton-under-Lyne ; 20th, Bury and Nelson; 30th, Ski; ton vol Heighley ; 31st, Harrogate and</p>
        <p>WAR RINGTON</p>
        <p>Nortbellerton ; September ht, Sbildon and West Stanley ; Sunday 3rd, Stockton-on-Tees ; 4th, Seabam Harbour and West Hertlepml ; sth, G lisb3rough and Whitby ; 6th, Melton and York ; 70, Cottingham and Goole; Bth, Normanton and Brighouse ; Sunday 10th, Rotherham ; Mb, Melton Mowbray and Kettering ; 13th, St. Neota and Hitchln ; 14th, Wattord, reaching ILondon at night.</p>
        <p>Pe</p>
        <p>r " • .</p>
