      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>OPEN-AIK POLITICS.</head>
        <p>OPEN-AIK POLITICS.</p>
        <p>THE PEOPLE'S VICTORY. Tariff Picnic Criticised.</p>
        <p>Upwards of 1,000 pe vie attended the weekly areting, under the auspices of the Warrington Progressive Labour League, held at Bridge Foot on Monday evening. The speeches were followed with great interest and members of the audience were not slow to punctuate them with rounds of applause. Mr. J. C. H .Hand presided and there were present in the van Messrs. James Kendal, of Manchester, E. Wynne Jones, J. Morton, Harry New, and Walter Cnsworth. The CHAIRMAN said that every true Liberal ought to feel proud and pat himself on the hash because the Veto Bill had passed through the House of Lords. (Cheers.) Now that the " die-hards " had been relegated to that position which they ought to have been placed in years ago the way had been cleared for good scud Liberal legislation, which would be for the good of workingmen and everybody else. (Cheers.) Mr. KENDAL said he did not know whether that large meeting was there to discuss the present trouble in the labour world, but he could say that most of the men preseat</p>
        <p>SINCERELY SYMPATHISED with a large number of those who felt it necessary to interfere with our comfort. He was also quite sure that in the case of those men who had been working hard in the hot weather for 15s. and I7s a week that there was not a right-minded man who did not feel a good deal of sympathy with them and he (the speaker) only rerretted that in the 2ath century the interference with the Com- fort of the public was the only way by which men could get their wrongs redressed. (Hear, hear.) And person- ally he expressed the hope that the day would soon dawn when there would be a better way of obtaining justice on both sides than by means of strikes and lock-outs. (Cheers.) He was glad to think that an effort had been made in that direction by the Liberal Government who had established a Board for giving box makers and other sweated labourers reasonable wages. (Applause.) After complimenting the Liberals on the " very rine van " which they bad jest procured, -Mr. Kendal said that through the passing of the Veto Bill the people of this country had WON A GREAT AND BLOODLESS VICTORY, though it was certainly not unaccompanied by bad language. (Laughter and applause.) Throughout the struggle Mr. Balfour had shown almost a calm indiffer- ence. Even in the midst of the South African war he was still enjoying his golf and in the midst of the recent struggle they bad noticed bin disregard of the seriousness of the great light they had been waging. In connection with that straggle be (the speaker) wanted to congratulate the lisog. (Hear, hear.) It was sonsetbing when Radicals had the proud position of standing up against men who were talking about our' young and inexperienced King," who they said had been intimidated by Messrs. Asquith, Lloyd George and Churchill. By standing on the side of the people the King could not have better inaugurated his reign nor rounded oft the Coronation ceremonies with greater genius and statesmanship amid all the clamour of interested persons. He bad shown himself to Le a constitutional monarch and had ESTABLISHED THE THRONE OP THIS COUNTRY MORE</p>
        <p>FIRMLY than it bad been for generations. He (Mr Kendal) also wanted to pay a tribute to Lord Hugh Cecil—the future leader of the Tory party—and a very flt leader, too. (Laughter). He would not be looked at amongst Radicals—they could buy his sort at any price. (Laughter.) He was glad to think that either the noble lard or Mr. Austen Chamberlain was going to come out at the top in the venture, but whoever succeeded to the leadership of the Tory party they wished them joy in their bargain. It had been established that on the other side there were men wha were prepared, by their actions, not to be true men, never mind gentlemen. Now that the people had won, what were they going to make of it ? Lord Derby was armions to win seats as an end in themselves but the Liberal party wanted to win victories in order that good might be accomplished. (Applause). As a result of the recent struggle they had got enormously increased power and even if the House of Lords did bang up Liberal legiala- Don for three years they could wait patiently, for instead of them having it piece meal they would have a glut of good legislation which would make them wonder whether the millenium had arrived. In his opinion there ought to be in the forefront of the Liberal programme the reform of the House of Lords, for althougo they had tenured the power of crippling the Lords veto they had not yet won the fight. Throughout the speeches in the Hone&lt; of Lords on the Veto Bill there was a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>REV. E. BLANSHARD KEELING,</head>
        <p>THE REV. E. BLANSHARD KEELING,</p>
        <p>-- Late Surerintendent of the W arringtun Wesleyan Circuit who preached his farewell seranna in Bawsey- road Wesleyan Church on Sunday evening last. He is taking up duties at I ford, Land:an. New Pastor far Golborne-street Baptist</p>
        <p>Church. Toe Rev. Herbert Ogden, of Oldham, bre ancepted an invitation to be the pastor of Galborne-atre it B.ptist Church. The rev. gentleman is announced to conduct the Mole Class anniversary services on the 27th inst. Mr. Ogden succeeds the Rev. R. Letueren, wso resigtei he pastorate about 15 months ago. Wycliffe P.S.A. Society. There was a fair attendance on Sun lay, the Secretary riroiwd " .otfhpeosvaeirdregystr4 Harry There eet hNr eeew k se moral and spirituel. We all liked to look upon a strong well built, powerful man, especially when he had his strength well under control, but we loved mole to look upon a good moral min, one who seems to be master of himself sal one who rules well those under him. But a strong spiritually ',Winded man was to be envied, one who by the grace of Gad coald control his will and , temper, what was once his master was now I thoroughly subdued. Mr. New gave some striking [ illustrations of men, who onne %sere ruled by sinful I passions, hut were now able to stand temptation, insults I and injury because they trusted in the Almighty Oae. Mr. H. Olaborne and Mr. W. Beawick, also took put in the meeting. Mr. R. Wallington presided at the organ. St. John's Presbyterian Church. _ . .</p>
        <p>- - - On Sunday evening last the Rev. W. Reid preached at St. John's from it. Matthew x., 41. Christ, said the preacher, concluded his charge to the twelve in the words of the text, when commissioning then, to minister in the interests of His Kingiom. Apart from encouragement the pasfage conveyed a good deal of valuable information as to the working of the Kingdom's laws. The prophet mood at the foot, the righteous man midway. and the child at the summit of the scale. doubtlets for the reason discovered by St. Petit, that prophecios passed as did atandards of morality, while love remained. The rewards, too, were according to law, the prophet receiving an increased prophetic capacity, the righteou man, in obedient life,an exalted conception of righeous nese, while all who possessei the power to sympathise with and receive a certain character, potentially possessed tint tame character themselves, with its inevitable reward. Next Sunday evening the Sacrament of the Lord's Supper will be observed at St. Job's's.</p>
        <p>veiled intimation that they would exercise the powers they still had to the full. It was the desire of the Tories to reverse the Bill as soon as they got a majority in the House of Commone, and if the House of Lords were reformed immediately their objeot would he frustrated. (Applause.) They wonted the House of Lords to be no effective and useful institution and not as at present. (Applause.) After complimenting the Die•hards upon the honest staid which they made the speaker said that another of the first things Liberals should do was to give</p>
        <p>JUSTICE TO IRELAND. (Loud applause) From the language renently used about John Redmond it was rtally wonderful that he had not got a lit of swank on. (Laughter.) The time bad gone by when they were frightened of mentioning Home Rule before a British audience. For the last hundred years Ireland had been unfairly treated and now she should base the chance of working out her own salvation. Liberals wished to remove the reproach from this country that Ireland was about the only part of our Empire which had not self-government. Elsewhere, where they had adopted the policy of trusting the people by allowing them to govern themselves the people had always reepanded rightly to that trust. (Applause.) It was not because of the coalition that the Liberal party were going in for Home Rule, for they had made great ssorillces for it before, especially when they put themselves out of tallice for 2) years because of their loyalty to Ireland. (Hear, bear.) In conclusion Mr. Kendal referred to plural voting and land taxation, stating that the former was indefeasible arid that ere ' long there would be ample evidence that land taxation was eminently justiflable. (Applause.) Is the course of his address Mr. HARRY New said unhesitatingly that a more mean and despicable trick had never been enacted in political life than the</p>
        <p>TRIP WHICH TOOK PLACE to Frodsham on the previous Thursday. (Applause.) All credit to any lady or gentleman who had the care of the children at heart, bat for nearly three months boxes, without any I libels attached, had been put into people's faces without anyone knowing it was apolitical d.idge until the last four or live weeks, when they came out in their true colours. (Mr. Dose : That is a lie.) ,It may be; you are a good judge of lies, retorted Mr. ' New. Continuing, he mid he wanted to condemn the political picnic, because it put the children and their i parents n a very queer and unsatisfactory position. P'or chi Idren,whether of Free Traders or Tariff Reformers, to be labelled like convict 93, with a Tariff Reform label was a system that was obnoxious. (Applause.) , The whole affair pat the poor man in a very peculiar position, for though be might ba a Free Trader he would have to let his children go on the political picnic because such an event did not very often occur. If the Liberal party were to stain their hands as the Tories had done he might tell them that he wouli wash his hands of the local party, as many others would be prepared to do. (Applause.) What they were fighting for was purity in public life and the betterment of the people at large. (Applause.) Mr E Wvssis•Joses said that</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>MR. IItROLD SMITIT</head>
        <p>MR. IItROLD SMITIT</p>
        <p>had insulted Mr. Pierca Williams and every other fair- minded man by not replying to the letter which Mr. Williams wrote to the Press a few weeks ago. Neither Mr. Smith nor any other Tariff Reformer coon answer the letter—in fact, the whole Tariff case was beaten to the world. (Applause) At this point a man in the crowd alleged that the wages in protected countries are higher than they are here and in reply Mr. Jones threw out a challenge. He asked any Tariff Reformer to tell him a single country in Eurcpe where tke cost of living was less, where the hours of work were shorter, where the wages were higher than in Free Trade Britain. (The INTERRUPTER : In Canada.) Mr. Jones : I said in Europe. You are weak in your geography. That only shows you " the incurable sloppiness of the Tariff Reformer. (Laughter and applause.) Mr. Jones also dealt with the question of Home Rule at some length and he stated that no party which assumed the Government of this nation could ignore the Irish question which had got to be settled once and for all. (Loud applause . At the conclusion of the meeting three hearty cheers were given for the Liberal Government.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>LAND BROUGHT FORTH FROGS.</head>
        <p>THE LAND BROUGHT FORTH FROGS.</p>
        <p>By collating the information given by vevious corres- pondents in our columns, it would be easy to make an instructive essay on natural history in a droughty season. The heat has produced 'rime unexpected phenomena, perhaps the most remarkable being the vast multiplication in the number of frogs, animals which it was generally thought prospered most in moist and [humid seasons. It seems, however, that the frog I flourishes equally well in dry weather, and, as he has few enemies, he is in danger of becoming a nuisance.— " Country Life."</p>
      </div>
