      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>Examiner Saturday August 19, 1911</head>
        <p>The Examiner Saturday August 19, 1911</p>
        <p>Earl of Haddington, farms lu.! acres and milk 40 beasts.</p>
        <p>THE JUMPING CONTEST.</p>
        <p>Tina:ens., interest centred in this contest which pro- vided some excellent sport The tree-bordered enclosure commanding a pretty prospect of Cheshire country, was crowded with an audience which followed the achievements of the competitors with enthusiastic interest. The winner was Messrs 1' and H Ward's "Fisherman" which has jumped consistently all through the season and was in the money at the great Yorkshire and Royal Lancashire show. The horse was ridden by , • boy of fourteen, who bandied him beautifully. Result: Ist prize £2O, Messrs T and H Ward, Great Ayton, Yorks; 2nd (215) sad third (210) prizes,divided between T C Whittingham, Barton-on-Trent, and F V Grange, Alvaston, Nentwich ; 4th prize (n), Wellburn Broo., Frystoo, Ferrybridge.</p>
        <p>TRADESMEN'S EXHIBITS. As mull this were numerous special stands and exhibits arranged by tradesmen and agriculturists from a wide area. Messrs. Darton'a, Ltd., seed growers and merchants, Warrington, were again to the fore with a One collection of Leeds and farm products, special attention being drawn to their nem brand of wheat " Victor," which in the public trials of the Somerset County Council produced the heaviest yield of grain out of seven leading varieties (English and French). Mr. F. C. Dutton, Warrington, had a good show of agricultural machinery, and Mr. J. Hiumworth, Warrington, exhibited cutlery and tools.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>WARRINGTON SHOW.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON SHOW.</p>
        <p>BRILLIANT DAY AND A FINE EXHIBITION AT WALTON.</p>
        <p>The tradition which invariably associates more or ) less dismal weather with the Warrington Agricultural Society's show was happily broken on Thursday when perfect summer conditions favoured the twenty-second annual exhibition. From early morning until the close of the day the sun shone radiantly and the sky was almost cloudless. The result was that thousands of townspeople and visitors from a distance took the opportunity of spending a few hours amid the beautiful surroundings of Walton Palk, once more placed at the Society's disposal by its Presi- dent (Sir Gilbert Grecian, Bart.), who takes the keenest possible interest in the show. Despite tie long drought, the park was looking at its best and the premature autumn tints in the foliage gave a finishing t uch to a picture of great rural charm. Owing to the trouble in the railway world there had been grave fears that exhibits would be prevented from reaching the show-ground, but fortunately no such disaster befel the Society, all the horses and cattle being on the ecene in good time. 'I he irregularity of the train service, however, delayed the arrival of the judges and it was well in the alter- noon before the judging of the shires, usually accom- plished before lunch, was completed. The show itself was well in keeping with the society's higfi'rerintation and when it is borne in mind that in at of the classes are confined to the district the satisfactory condition of agriculture in the immediate neighbourhood of Warrington is at once apparent. The ent,ies were somewhat disappointing, showing a decline of 62 as compared with last year. Warrington show is noted for its heavy horses and ODCO more the shires were the strongest section, for numerically there was an advance of 16 while the quatity was probably superior to anything previously seen at Walton. Slight increases were recorded in the pig and cattle classes, but the hunters, hackneys, harness and turn- out classes, as well as the butter and cheese, were all below last year's figures. All day interested crowds lined the rings where the judging took place, hut the climax of interest came mith the jumping contest which, to the average visitor who does not lay claim to expert knowledge, was by far the moat popular event of the day. The grand stand and enclosure, thronged with a brightly dressed multitude, presented an animated and picturesque spectacle. The contest provided excellent sport and as the horses negotiated the various obstacles of gates, walls nod d.tches, loud applause greeted any particularly smart achievement. The Walton Flower Show, which has for several years been held in conjunction with the agricultural show, again attracted much admiration,' the exhibits being well worthy of this pretty garden vidage and affording eloquent proof that the show has fully justified ite existence. The musical programme is always a much-appreciated feature of the Warrington show and on this occasion it was provided by the famous band of the Cheshire (Earl of Chester's) Yeomanry, under the direction of Mr. Fred L. Buckley. The per- formances of this talented body of instrumentalists afforded the utmost satisfaction to visitors, for the programme was arranged with commendable regard for all tastes and the interpretation of each piece was adequate irk the fullest sense of the word. The selec- tions included the overture " William Tell " which was magnificently given, a selection from " Tannhauser," several light operatic items and solos on the cornet, piccolo, tubular bells and xylophone. Although the number of visitors to the shew ground in the merning did not appear to be up to the average the afternoon made ample amends, well filled vehicles ply ing between Warrington and Walton at very fre- quent intervals, whilst a large number of townspeople walked or cycled to the showground. FEATURES OF THE SHOW.</p>
        <p>As already indicated, the shire horse classes were the outstanding features of the show. Mr. James Gould, the well known exhibitor, again had the satis- faction of practioolly sweeping the board, his record for the day being seven firsts, two seconds, two thirds, a championship and four specials. He carried off the district champion cup,presented by Sir Gilbert G reenal I, with "Titchmarsh Baroness" a beautiful bay which Mr. Gould bought so recently as March last. She was exhibited for the first time at the Royal Lancashire show at Bury, taking first in the novice and second in the open class. In addition to being first in her class at Warrington, " Titchmarsh Baroness" secured the Shire Horse Society's gold medal, the cup presented by Mr. John Cozen, and Messrs. Day and Son's special prize. In the class for more without foal, Mr. Gould was first with" Knoulton Countess" and second with "Home Lacy Starlight." The first winner has not been shown very much, but she was first in the novice classat Warrington last year and floit in the brood mares with foil at foot at the Altrincham Show. Mr. Gould considers her to be one of the best mires he his ever had. "home Lacy Starlight" has been shown at every Warrington Show for the past five years, and on two occasions she won the gold medal and champion cup. Altogether the class for barren mares was the best ever seen at Walton. Mr. Alfred Broome won the cup presented by Sir Gilbert Grevnall for competition among his tenantry, with the nice sic-year-old bay 'Windmill Duohess:" :Mr. Gould's Lymm Starlight," which took first place in: the class for novice brood mares, has not been shown for three years. As a foal she won the cup at Walton and she also had a similar honour at Altrincham. Ass yearling she was third at the London show. Mr. Lewis Dodds' " Rustic Forest King " whioh took first prize and cup for the best foal on the ground, is a big, upstanding bay, with plenty of scale, good feet and joints, and an abundance of long fine feather. He looks like making a floe stallion. There was keen competition in the class for two-year-old gelding Cr filly, and Mr. Gould's " Lvmm Quality " and Mr. Dodd's " Rustic Will Rose " were so closely matched that Mr. Bainbridge was called in as referee. He decided in favour of " Lymm Quality " which has a floe record of show successes, having twice taken cups at Warring- ton, and two firsts and a gold medal at Altrincham last year. " Rustic Wild Rose " was second to the winner lest year, and has done a great deal of winning at</p>
        <p>local shows. The hunter classes at Walton are always good, but the exhibits on this occasion were considered t 3 63 the finest class seen together at any show during the present season. Sir Gilbert Greenall rode every horse in each class and after long discussion with his colleague arrived at awards which seemed to give general satisfaction. Mr J II Stokes'a chesnut gelding "Shrewsbury," a wonderfully good-mannered horse which looked like carrying his weight very easily, beat Mr. William Gale's bay gelding which went, if any- thing, a trifle high in front and did not seem to give the riders, easy a mount as the winner. An on-unmet bay gelding of Mr Gale's, a horse with wonderful back and quarters, won from Mr Stokes's " Guinea" which hod not quite no much substance, the second being closely followed by Mr John Drage's " Driver," which has a lovely, easy action. Sir Gilbert Greenall's influence in the Belvoir district was doubtless responsible for getting together such a remarkable entry, and it will probably be a lung time before two such fine classee are seen again in this district. The hackneys were also a fine lot, the Hackney Horse Society's medal going to Mr. A. J. Willett's " Fire Bell," which won in the class for pony brood mares, her foal being second in his class. Mr. Willett's exhibits beat all the big mares in the show, going with tremendous fire and displeying splendid quality all round, la the harness section, Mr. Howard Frank won the under-fourteen hands class with his brown gelding " Apollo "which has a beautiful level action and go&amp; with great power oft his hocks. He was closely followed by two products of the Walton stud, "Tissington Merri- lees " and " Tissington Meddlesome." The over-fifteen hands class were the strongest harness class in the slacw. The chestnut gelding " Walk Over," which has only been in this country for three weeks, before which he was working regularly in the streets of Berlin, won for bin new owner, Mr. S. L. Heppe!, of Berthas whose horsea are under the care of the well known handler, Mr. Bert Mills. The tradesmen's turnouts, although not no good numerically as lost year, were wonderfully good in quality. and spoke volumes for the careful treatment which the horses receive at the hands of their owners. Warrington has indeed just cause to be proud of its own specimens of horseflesh, and the spick-and.span appear- ance of the turnouts was generally commented upon. One of the jadges of the cattle classes unhesitatingly expressed the opinion that a finer lot of animals h&amp; never been seen at Walton show. Mr. S. S. Raincill carried off the cup presented by the vice-president with a wonderful type of diary cow, fit to win in any company. Mr. S. W. Goald's calf which was awarded the cup for the best heifer, is a very nice specimen, and great things are expected of it. Mr. Gould's winner in the aged bull class was also a very noteworthy animal, with deep heavy flesh and good movement. Had these been e championship for bulls, this animal would undoubtedly hove won it.. The Warrington Show never attracts a large entry in the butter and cheese CIDDS&amp;S, as the farmers in this district do not seem to devote much attention to those branches of the industry. However, the quality this year was exceedingly creditable. There was a par- ticularly fine open class; of cheeses. The first prise went to Mr George Platt, of Tarporley, with a white cheese, and he also took the special for the best c]eese in the show. Mr Platt would have been awarded the second prize for a coloured lot in the same class, but, having taken a special, be was not eligible for it. Mr Platt is a well known winner and took first and second prises at the Southport show. Ha has also gained the unique distinction of winning outright the champion award at the Wirral and Birkenhead thew. He is • tenant of the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>OFFICIALS.</head>
        <p>THE OFFICIALS.</p>
        <p>The society is exceedingly fortunate in its officials, who devote themselves whole-heartedly to the advance- ment of the show. Mr James Bainbridge is especially deserving of thanks for the capable and energetic may in which he discharged the secretarial duties. The following is a full list of the officials :—President, Sir Gilbert Greenall, Burt.; vice-president, Mr Alan J Sykes, M.P. ; chairman, Mr Joshua Ball ; vice-chair- man, Mr Henry Mawdsley ; hon. treasarer, Me Alfred Broome, Preston Brook; secretary, Mr James Bain- bridge, Walton House, near Warrington. JUDGES :—Hunters, Sir Gilbert Greenall, Birtakir E , Cotton, Penley Hall, Ellesmere ; hackneys and harness, Mr vó Smethurst, High Bank, Walshaw, Bury, Lanes, Mr C E Greenall, The Manor,Cariton Scroop,o rantham; shires, Mr Thomas Ewart,Dunamore Stud Farm, Rugby, Mr S G Grimes, Search ff a Grange, Bolsover, Chester- field ; tradesmen's or tenant faroers' turnouts, Mr T Eaton Jones, M.R.C.V.S., Municipal Buildings, Dale, street, Liverpool, Mr C E Greenall,The Manor, Carlton Scroop, Grantham ; jumpers, Mr T Eaton Junes an] Mr C E Greenall ; cattle, Mr W I Bennett, Clifton Horse, Poolton-le-Fylde, Mr Win. Bateman, Beanmont Grange, Halton, Lancaster; pigs, Mr Richmond Day- bell, Grange Far m,Worsley,oear klauchester; cheese and butter, Mr E R Thcmpson, Messes W Brooks and Co., I, Elliott-street, Liverpool ; consulting veterinary surgeons, Mr H H Ferguson and Mr S J Hewitt; referee, Mr W Bainbridge. STEWARDS :—Hooters and jumpers, Messrs Joseph Palmer, J W Boumphrey, Edward Marsh, T M West, V V Davies and Geoffrey Owen ; hackneys, harness, tradesmen's and tenant farmers, turnout., Messrs T G Williams, Jas V Moore, C B Ikavies, Cha3 Blackshaw ; shires and draught geldings, Messrs Wm Richaidson, jun, John Whitlow, Jos T B tater, Edward Davies, 11 E Gibbon ; cattle, Messrs H T Ilulme, Jos Percival, 3Vm Norcott, H Brocklehurst ; pigs, butter and cheese, Messrs F A Milling,John Cooper, Bayley Isaac; medals and champion prizes, Mr James Tinsley; show yard, Mc J E Seeker.</p>
      </div>
