      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>FLOWER SHOW.</head>
        <p>THE FLOWER SHOW.</p>
        <p>It was a happy innovation when, five years ago, the Walton flower show was established as a purely local adjunct of the Warrington agricultural show. It Ina stimulated the villagers, already proud of their trim and well-stocked ga:deus, to keener though friendly rivalry, and the outcome has been a steady improve- ment in the quality of the exhibits from year to year. Horticulture has reached a high level in Walton and, notwithstanding the long spell of dry weather, some of the gardens are at present perfect pictures of beauty. The show on Thursday attracted 219 entries and the marquee in which the exhibits were tastefully arranged was inspected with much interest by all the visitors to the park. The groups of green- house and stove plants bad been most artistically selected and celled forth general admiration. In the flower sections asters were prrticularly good and the collections of cut blooms were also highly creditable. The fruit classes were not very well filled, but some Splendid apples were shown. The r finials of the show ' were :—President, Lady Gieenall ; judges, Mr S Sprackling and Mr T Herbert, of Tarporley ; steward, Mr J G Hatton ;searetary, Mr. James Bainbridge. i The following s the prize lint:— PRIZE COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>DISTRICT.-For the neatest and best stocked cottage flower garden : I, William Berry ; 2, F Kipper ;3, F Hatablett. For neatest and best Itocked cottage kitchen garden: I, George Green ;2, F Kipper ;3, H.rry Bell. For neatest and best stocked cottage flower garden (estate workmen, : 1, John Kipp: ;2, James Kipper ; 3, Peter Cl(ffe, For the neatest and best stocked cottage kitchen garden estate workmen) : I, John Kippur ; 2, James Kipper; 3, James Cliffe. Champion prize (open for the neatest and best cottage Bower and kitchen gardens: John Rippe:, Hatton. °REV:ROUSE PLANTS.-000 single Foch.ia : I, F Kipper; 2, P Cliffs ; 3, J Clark One double Fuchria: I, F Kipper ; 2, J Cliff.; 3, P Cliffs. One Begonia : I, F Kipper ; 2,J Cliff.; 3, P Three Pleats in or out of flower, any variety : I, P Clare; 2, F Kipper; 3, J Cliffs. WINDOW Pia:yrs.-One begonia: 1, John Kipper ; 2,J Ratter; 3, F Hamblen. One double fuchsia : I end 2, J Ratter. One single fuchsia : I, J Butter; 2, Hawkins ; 3, John Kipper. Geranium : I, F Hamblett ; 2, J Rutter. Pot of musk 1, J Rutter. Ivy Geranium : 1, F Hamblett ; 2, J Rutter ; 3, 8 Hawkins. Three plants in or out of flower, any, variety 1, J Rutter, 2, P Hamblett. Cyr Fix/Nu-rm.-Six double dahlias (three varieties): 1, F Kipper ; 2, J Ratter. Sir single dahlias (three varieties) 1, F Kipper ; 2, P Cliffs ;3, J Ratter Sir Cactus dahlias (three varieties) : 1, F Kipper ; 2 T Whalley ;3, J Rutter. Six pompon dahlias (three varieties) : I,T Whalley; 2, F Kipper ;3,J Rutter. Sir esters (three varieties): 1, W Berry ; 2, F Kipper ; ,3, F Hattiblett ;hc, P ailfe. Six marigolds (French) :</p>
        <p>I, F Kippax ; 2, W Berry; 3,5 Hawkins. Six marigolds (African) : 1, J Cliff. ;2, P Cliff. ;3, IN Berry. Six carnations (three varieties) : 1,1 Cliffs ;2, .1 'Ratter. Six pansies (three varieties) : 1, P Cliff.; 2, J Cliff.; 3, T Whalley. Six violas (9 varieties) : 1, F kappa:, ; 2, P Cliff.; 3, J Cliffs. Six spikes tan week stocks (three varieties) : 1, iF Kippax; 2, F Hamblett. Six bunches sweet pens (three varieties) : 1, W Yarwood ; 2, W Berry; 3, If Kippax. Six roses (three varieties) : 1, P Cliff. ;2, J Cliff,. Collection of cut blooms :1, John Kippur ; 2, F Kippax; 3, P Cliffe. VEGETABLES (Diatrico.-Three varieties of potatoe (1 of each): I, W Berry; 2, T Whalley. One cabbage I (red) : I. G Green. One cabbage (white) : 1, G threes.; 2, 8 Hawkins and F Kippax. Six spring onions (outside sown) : I, F Kiprax ; 2, G Green ; 3, W Perry ; hc, S Hawkins. Twelve bulbs shallot,: I, G Green ; 2, H Bill ; 3, S 11..wkini ; It c. F Kippax. Six leeks : 1,0 Green; 2 F Kippax ; 3, II Bell. One dish peas (12 pods) 1, VI7 Berry ;2, F Kippax. One dish broad beans (6 pods) : I, F Kippax ; 2, 11 Bell. One dish French tesns (12 pods) : 1, W Berry; 2, S Hawkins ; 3, F Kippsx. One dish scarlet beans (12 pods) : I, W Yarwood ; 2, W Berry; 3, H Bell ; h e. G Green Two sticks celery (white) : 1. F Kippax ; 2,11? Haroblett. Two sticks celery (red) : 1, 11 Kippax; 2. F Hamblett ; 3, H Bell. One vegetable marrow (white;: No entries. One vegetable marrow (green) : I, 11 Bell: 2, 0 Green ; 3, F Kippax. Two cabbage lettuce: 1, F Kippax: 2, 0 Green; 3, W Barry. Two lota lettuce : 1, H Bell ; 2, W Houlsdale. Six carrots : I. W Hawkins ; 2, F ffsmblett, Six turnips: 1, P Kippax ; 2, Hoolidale ; 3, H Bell. Six Beetroota F Kippax; 2, 0 Green; 3,1 Whalley. Six Parsnips: 1, W harry; 2, 0 Green ; 3, F Hamblett. Twelve sprays parsley : 1,0 Green • 2, T Whalley. Collection of regetablei (6 varied* : 1, F Kippax ; 2,0 Gress; 3, H Bell. Fier IT.-Oee dish cooking apples: 1,0 Green ; 2, W Moulad