      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>Mot:navr.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Aldermen Bolton, Pierpoint, Forshsw, Bennett, Jamos Evans, Messrs. If. Thornton, G. Pilling, S. Wright, W. Roberts, W. Furness and J. W. Heyward. LICENCE TRANSFERS. —The following applied for the transfer of licences : Thomas Hardman, Legh Arms, Mewy-street, from James Wootton ; Sarah Walker, Ship Inn, Liverpool-road, from her late husband ; Henry Hankiruton, King's Arms, Biattermarket-street from Joseph Potter ; and William Coldwell off-licence, Wakefield-street, from his deceased mother Satisfac- tory characters having been proved in each instance, all the applications were granted. NEGLECT OF FAIIILY.—Thomaa Wright, a stranger was charged with running away- and leaving his wife and two children chargeahle to the Union. Mr. Shearer stated that prisoner was before the court on July 10th, when the case against him SCIIR adjourned. Since that tin e, however, he hod not been near the Cairn or his wife and children, nor had he done a single thing towards their maintenance. He had been seen by the Inspector and by Mr. Ashland, relieving officer. Mr. Ashland said the prisoner went to see him a fortnight ago and he told him the cuss was entirely out of his hands and that he would report him to Mr. Sh . Prisoner stid that there had been a misunderstanding. It was his wife who left him. This was borne out by the wife who stated that he drove her out of the bones when in drink. Four previous convictiona having been proved against the prisoner, he was sentenced to a month's hvrd labour. DRUNKENNE,S.—Ten cases of drunkenness were also down for hearing at this Court. TCVIDAY.— Before Councillor Jackson-Holmes (chair- man) Colonel Edelsten and Mr. W. Richardson</p>
        <p>NEGLECT OF CHILDREN.—WiIIiam Thomas Sheliner- dine, 44. Fennel-street, was charged with using obrcene language on Sunday, and els, with neglecting hie three children in a manner likely to clime them unnecessary suffering and injury to their health, on Angust 14th and other detes. Mr. Browne, who prosecuted on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C., add prisoner only took home part of the money he eirned and spent the rest in drink. His wife mu a fustian cutter and earned about 12s. a week ; but for her work, and the kindness of the neighbours, the children would have suffered more than they had done. In consequence of a complaint Inspector Wainwright went to see the prisoner. Ile sew boti parents on the occasion of his visit and the prisoner was the worse for drink. The inspector warned hint if he did not attend to his duties and look after the children he would have to prosecute him. The rooms were decently furnished but were untidy in conervence of the wife working from morning until nigot. On Monday Inspector Wainwright visited the house and found no food in consequence of the man hiving been locked up. inspector Wainwright, and the wile cm.oborated Ur. Browne's atatemthL— The wife said her Unhand told her he was not going to work for 17s. 101. a week. -Prisoner was tent to gaol , for three months</p>
        <p>---- WSDNESDAY.—Before Mr. Charles White (in the chair), Alderman Sreethorat, Councillor Jackson Holmes • ni Mr. W. T. Williams. .</p>
        <p>—.— FAMILY NEL I.6CT.—John Kelly, no fixed abode, was charged with running away and leaving his two children chargeable to the Union. He p!eaded not guilty. Mr. Shearer stated that ,h 3 prisoner's wife was sent to gaol on July 25,13 and r that date the tw3 children were admitted to t. Workhouse. Every endesvour no made to find the driatmer and be was eventually appre- hended at Birk, .Ibead. The children when admitted to the Union wet 3 ion wry filthy and neglected condition. Prisoner had practically neglected his family for the put five year. IVnen arreeted prisoner sat,' that he had been lone rch of work. Three previous convictions having been proved the prisoner was sentenced to a month's hard labour. . _ . .</p>
        <p>Assat:l.T.—Andrew Clancy, 24, Dolman's-lane, wan charged with ay.:malting Bridget Hardy, daughter of Mr. hardy, 20, Dolman's-lane—The Cbief Constable said the prosecutrix was walking along Dolman's-lass when she ism the prisoner talking ton young woman she knew. She spoke to the woman, who asked the prosecutrix to wait a moment. Prisoner took exception at the woman leaving him, and attacked the girl most shamefully, knocking her to the ground and striking her. The mother of the girl W. also assaulted by the prisoner when taking her to the Infirmary. The girl was unable to be present that morning on account of her injuries and the case was rsraanded for a week. FRlDAV.—Before the Mayor (in the ch•ir,, Aidarrnea Bolton and Piersolot, Messrs. S. Wright, W. Roberta. W. Furness, J. W. Hayward, H. Thornton, G. Pilling and W. T. Williams. CLOSING OF Penic-1101:ses—Ifr, A. Browne applied for the assessment of the compensation payable is respect of the Higher Angel, Buttermarket-street end the Brewer's Arles, Wthwick-street. Ile said that it Was for the Bench th assess, if they so eared to assess, an amount the owners and tenants were entitled to as compensation for doing away with the licences for those houses.—ln the first instance £1.612 105 Ira, claimed, equal to an no. rage annual profit on 1,5 barrels of beer, 912 dozen bottles of beer and stout •nd 91 gallons of spirita and wig's. It had been agreed to pay the tenant £125, including everything, and he was latialled with that amount. The second case wan in regard to the Brewer's Arras, Winwiak-street, When Messrs Greenall consented to give up that place they were not aware that it was such a good house as it had turned out to be. ,C 2,3,7 10s 34 wee claimed in thin instance with an annuli aversga of 312 barrels of beer (over six barrels a week) sod 2.; dozen bottles. The premiers could be altered for. shop or other purpose and the rent would prob- ably he .hot £25 per annum at the outside. The tenant bad agreed to accept LlOO, and in addition to whatever was allowed Ira depreciation of the trade fixtures. The case wax actually based upon the figures allowed by the Cheshire Compensation Authority, i.e., 125, per barrel profit. The Bench retired and upon their return the Chairman announced that the decieion was to allow £1,2)0 in the case of the Higher Angel and LIAO in the case of the Brewer's Arms. Mr. Browne : We decline those. FOCNO ON ENCLOSED ['anuses:4.3hp Wright, 2, Ship-yard, was charged with being found on the enclosed premises, 159, Knntsford-road, with intent to commit • felony. The Chief Constable said the prisoner we. found about 4 o'clock the previous day et Lb. beck of the abseil premises which were uncocapted. It Wall uoertained that a quantity of lead was miming. Prisoner had been seen the previous day by the next- door tenant on the premises. When tweeted by the police he said that be bad been to get some old rags. The case was remanded for .even days.</p>
      </div>
