      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>LIVERPOOL RIOTS.</head>
        <p>THE LIVERPOOL RIOTS.</p>
        <p>The Sccnes in Liverpool on Sunday last will long be r. memb rd. For nearly an hour the plateau in front of St. George's Hall—the Trafalgar Square of Liverpool—was the scene of melee after melee, in which the police, in self defence, had to charge the furious mob: with drawn batons. After the Riot Act had been read, a section of the Warwickshire Regiment and Scots Greys were called out. The violence of the mob was Indescribable ; the air was filled wiih glass battles, bricks, stones, and pieces of iron. The place resembled a shamble, and for nearly an hour after the police had cleared the Square ambulances and cabs were carrying away the wounded. Our picture shows, on the left, the North-Western Hotel, where the fighting started.</p>
