      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>DROUGHT.</head>
        <p>THE DROUGHT.</p>
        <p>Not within living memory has the whole country suffered so long for want of rain as during this record summer. In many districts, particularly the East and South of England, there has been practically no rain for two months. Dairy farmers are having an extremely anxious time, and the milk yield is reduced in an extraordinary degree. Thousands of acres of grassland are as bare as sandhills. In many districts the shortage of water is little short of a calamity, and in some villages the supply is so scarce that It is being sold as high as twopence a pailful. Our picture shows children returning home with pails of water at High Easter, Essex.</p>
        <p>POPE PIUS X. The health of the rope has been causing grave anxiety for some time. His condition now, however, shows a tendency to gradual improvement. It is reported that the !eves' has disappeared, but the gouty symptoms are still present. His Holiness has been Pope since 1893, and is in his seventy-sixth year.</p>
        <p>Plumbers' Work, First Year. Pass: William Ball :roast Buckley Mellor, William Gibson and George F t_ Hughes. Ordinary Grade, PractiJal, Pass : George</p>
