      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>BUN IN A BOUDOIR.</head>
        <p>THE BUN IN A BOUDOIR.</p>
        <p>VALUABLE PICTURES DESTROYED BY FIRE Several thousand pounds worth of damage was done by fiie sod water at Dehimere Howe, Caddirgton, Northwich, the house of Major Wilbraham. Tbe curtains of a boudoir became lit by, it is said, the focussing of the sun's rays on windows and mirrors. The fire was not allowed to extend beyond the boudoir, but a picture by Luca Giordano, an Eastern study by Nieulant, French screecs, mirrors, and Japanese and Oriental china were completely destroyed, end pictures by Carre, Cuyp, Ruisdael, and others were so badly damaged as to maim restoration questionable. Other valuable pictures and old Chelsea sad Worcester china were saved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB</p>
        <p>A CLEVER VISH.</p>
        <p>Did you ever, when at the sea-side, see a cuttle fish? Sometimes he lies among the rocks at low water looking like a jelly fish with ten long waving arms. Each of these arms has a tiny round hand at the end and with these he can grip and hold any fish that may come within his reach. These hands are adapted as suckers--just like the leather suckers with which boys lift stones, though on a very small scale. A remarkable thing about the cuttle fish is his power to make ink. He does it to protect himself. Sometimes a great hungry dog fish spies him and rushes up to' catch him. Mr. Cuttle swims as hard as he can, but of course he is no match for the great dog-fish. Just as his enemy is upon him, however, he ejects an inky fluid which turns the water murky and dark, and then drops down to the bottom and hides as well as he can among the stones. The ink is not really black, it is a dark brown. Next time you open your paint box look at the paint called" sepia." This paint is made from the ink of the cuttle fish. TIIIS WEEK'S STORY.</p>
        <p>The bairel of rues t apples had been put in the attic until the bait was ripe enough to e it and motto, had warned her ill ee boys, whose ages range from five to eleven years, not to (ouch them. Then, one rainy day, she came full upon her sons, surrounded by applecores. At tier appronh two of the boys drew closer together ; but the third, a little distance off, who lay on his stomach contentedly munching an apple, apparently paid no attention to his mother's entrance. " Jack ! Henry ! Willie !" she exclaimed, reproachfully. " Whatever are you doing? And those Apples ! Didn't I tell you not to touch them 1" . .</p>
        <p>'Yea, mamm i," replied the eldest, " but we're not really rating them ; we're acting the Garden of Eden. Willie and I are Adam and Eve. Henry, over ihtre, in the serpent, trying to lead as to our downfall by shoeing us how good the apple, are."</p>
        <p>But, began the mother as sternly as she was able," you two must have beer, eating apples ; Henry hasn't done it all. I see as many as tea cores around you." " Oh, yrs," said Willie, the youngest, " we've all been taking turns being the serpent." LITTLE BOY BLUE. Be EUGENE FIELD.</p>
        <p>The little toy dog is covered with duet, But sturdy and staunch he stands; And the little toy soldier is red wit't rust, And his musket moulds in his hands. Time was when the little toy dog was new, And the soldier was passing fair; And that was the time when our Little B I Blue Kis.ed them and put them there. "Now, don't you go till I come," he said, "And don't you make any noise!" So, toddling off to his trundle bed, He dreamt of tbe pretty toys; And, as he was dreaming, an angel song Awakened our Little Boy Blue, Oh ! the years are tinny, the years are long, But the little toy friends are true! Aye, faithful to Little Boy Blue they stand, Each in the same old place, Awaiting the touch of a little hand, The smile of a little face. And they wonder, as waiting the long years through In the dust of that little chair, What has become of our Little Boy Blue, Since be kissed them and put them there.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>RUNCORN MAN'S GOOD FORTUNE.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN MAN'S GOOD FORTUNE.</p>
        <p>In the following paragraph a Runcorn man tells of his good for,une. We not only offer him our con- ' gratulations, but thank him for his outspoken state- ment. Many of us, too, will profit by his experience. I Mr. J. Vi hits, who resides at 3, Arthnr-street, Run- • corn says :—" It was owing to a cold I cght, which wi h affected my kidneys, that &amp;steamed my back- ache trouble" he says, " I can tell you that for some weeks I suffered agonies both at work and at home. The pains would come over me whenever I stooped or had to lift anything, and after I got warm in bed at night - there was a thick sandy sediment in the water too. " Just about that time I beard of Down's backache kidney pills, and I sent for a box to see what they would do for me. I am thankful I tried them, for they proved to be just the medicine I wanted. I got relief as soon mai commenced to use them, and after giving them a fair trial, I was as right as rain again, and I have kept well since that time. The kidneys are perfectly healthy, and my back does not pain me in the least. I shall always be glad to tecemmend Down's pills, and you are quite at liberty to publish this account of my expetience. (Signed) John White." 2s. DI a b x.l; boxes 13s. 9d. ;of all deale:s. , Ask for DOAN'S backache kidney pill", like Mr. White had.</p>
