      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD</p>
        <p>JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>NEW BAPTIST MINISTER I read that the Rev. H. Ogden, a B.ptist minister I who has been stationed in the neighbourhood of I Oldham' Las been called to a similar office in the War- rington district. I know t e rev, gentleman well; in fact, I have been r.ornewLat ihtimats with him since be was quite a boy. The following particulars may interest a section of' Examioer " readera. Mr. Ogden is a native of Todniorden. He is a young wihoser. A while aher bin aceerance of the pastorate wl ich he has jrist I esign. d for the local one now in question, he was married to a daughter of the present Town CM k of Todenorden (Mr. 0. Sorel ffe). The wedding took place at the W. Ilington-road Baptist Church in that town in the prenence of a multitude of friends sal ado irate I f the pai•-, both bride and bridegroom haring been actively associated witri the Sunday school and auxiliaries. Writing from m m.ry, I suppose that it would be within a coun'e of years or so of that happy occasion that Mrs. Ogden died ant xpectedly, and the sorely-stricken minister had overwhelming assurances of sympithy from all sides A few months ago, the cburch of which he then bad charge alarmed their appreciation of the deceased's merits ar.d their grief at her premature end by erect- ing a tablet as a permanent memorial. Mr. Ogden, who went to them from college was a great favourite, and the separation affected on his removal to Warrington will be keenly felt by many. ' RINGING THE CHANGES." The case cited in last week's " Notes and Notions" reminded me of an o'd circus ring " wheeze" that I often heard as a youngster. The clown would tell how he . ieited a" pub "and called for a tottle of beer, lot immediately asked the avendant to bring a cigar instead. He then apologieed, saying " Waiter, I've changed my mind again ; please take the cigar box away, and get me a glass of brandy." Hay.rg tossed rif the brandy, he was about to leave the house when it was pointed out that he bad not paid for the drink. " Ha, but," arid he, " I gave you the cigar for it ; it was a good and expensive cigar, you know." " All the same " said the waiter, " you didn't pay for the cigar." "Yes," urged the profitable customer, " I gave you the bottle of beer for it." " Well, well," impatiently pursued the other, " you didn't pay for the beer.' "Just so," blandly responded the ' change-ringer' " I didn't have it, did I ? Why, then, should I pay for it?' TREATS TO THE POOR. So the alleged "Treat to Slum Children " was enjoyed, tint by those for whom it was said to have been intended, but by another grade entirely. IJo not profess to be able to explain or understand it, but there is a god deal 'f that sort of thing up and down the creintry. I have in mind a Methodist Church in connection with which the under-fed and ill-clad youngsters of tla locality are given a substantial breakfast each Chti tmas morning, and are sent bon e rejoicing in the possession of dolls and other toys, icture cards, he. Some years ag,, 100 or so were thus entertained, but the numbers speedily rose, and last year had reached between 500 atid GOO. I looked in during the meals, and was amazed to find at the tables row after row of rosy, healthy-looking, well- dressed kiddies, very few indeed lof atom could fairly be said to be in need of chari y. The thing ie becoming a scandal. "ST. GROUSE."</p>
        <p>The recurrence of " the glc.rious twelfth ' con- 'trait s me to remark f v whatever th-1 reader may deem it worth that, whils I eas understand that eh, oting over dogs may be regarded as legitltnate wen, I cannot for the life of me perceive the lifer- -nee between the wholesale slaughter entailed in the " driving " tory generally practised and the common pigeon "shoot " which the gentry no,v in pose-salon of the Imo rent the north would probably be among the I int to den ounce. I may be told that in the case of the latter the birds are brought up by hand. Well, to are pheasants, and if one may trot say precisely the same of grouse, the preservation of moor game has now rein reduced to an coact science, and there id little essential difference. LIES MAJESTY AND RIPON.</p>
        <p>The King opened the season at Stu hey Royal on ,he guest of Lord Ripon. The newspaper referencen to the event breught upon me a Ito d of recollections As a youth, I lived at Fountains Hall with a relative, and, along with the tenantry and household, attended service at the lit.'s chapel conneced with Studley Royal. All to'd, choir included, we seldom mustered more than five-and-twenty, prominent among the con- gregation being the late Marquis, the present Lord Ripon (then Earl Grey), and Lady Mary Vyner, whose son was murdered by, Greek brigands. When next met the late Marquis, I attended as a reporter for the press the celebration of the anniversary of the Nutclough Co-ope ative Manufacturing Society, &amp;Mien Bridge—a wholesale el thing works success- fully established by a number of workingmen, in whose enterptise his Lordship took considerable personal interest—as did likewise the late Judge Hughes, author of 'Torn Brown's School Days," and some other celebrities. I note that the Loudon correspondent of the " Daily News " states that studley Royal" stands in the middle of a beautiful park "—which is true enough, for as a boy I had the run of it—aiding that it " extends as far as Ripon, three miles away "; but that is rather a stretch of imagioati-n. Moreover, he declares that " a church was erected in the park slew years ago," tec Well, it most be somewhere between 30 and 40 years since that church was built; the mosaics were being laid by Italian workmen while I was there. WHEN DOCTORS DIFFER. However, I was writing of grouse, and I have not yet quite done with the subject. I mean, of course, the red grouse—decidedly the most important of the I four species of its kind to be found in these islands. It flourishes in the North of England and in Scotland; but in the South it refuses to live. Why ? According to Mr. E. O. Mackenzie, the re mon in heather. In the " Estate Magazine " awhile ago be wrote : -" Efforts I to acclimatise the grouse further south than Derby- I shire in England have failed, even where a plentiful supply of heather as on Dartmoor and at Sar.dringham I promised that success might be pesible. But success I was not achieved, in so far that in a very few years in both places mentioned the grown, died off and have I long ago diaappeared entirely therefrom. Naturalists I account for this by the statement that the particular ' kind of heath the grouse are most partial to for food is not to be found on the moors in thee tuth of England and we believe thin to be the truth," Do they really? About the time that Mr. Mackenzie was repeating and confirming and enlarging upon the popular notion that grouse must have their heather, aid, inched cannot live without it, Mr. J. J. Stuble was Irani'', employed upon an article for " British Birds," con- troverting this idea that the red grouse cannot exist without heather, a term including true heather or ling (Calltma Vulgaris) and the two common heaths (Erica Tetralix and E. cinerea). He has made several surveys of moorlands lying (roughly speaking) between Manchester and Huddersfield, and in miles if walking has seen scores of grouse without meeting with a plant of the order Ericaceae. The plant called ling by all the keepers on those moors is tho crowberry (Enipet rum um), " One experienced keeper," he says, " refused to listen when I sugzested be had o t a plant of heather on he moor, and I rather think that this will be the mental attlude of many another meas." Mr. Stubbs gathers that real ni ouse disease is altogether unknown on these heatherleas moors; and he never yet found crowberry growing anywhere except on more or less pure peat. "On great tracts of the Pennine moors gr use are abundant and heather does not and cannot grow. Blackstone Edge ia easily examined by the observer who is not desirous of leaving the path. Here he will find the gra sue fin places) common, but he will search in vain for heather." All this is very interesting, and, to one like roysel who has spent many years in the very region just indicated, not a little puzzling. THE C LB's ENCAMPMENT. The experiences of the Warrington contingen: of the Church Lad's Brigade at Skegness rent me to an old chronicle which begins with these remarkable words : - "Skegness was at sumtyme a great haven louse. The old towne is now clear consumed an eten up with the se." A modern writer upon the subject .31:Hems that from Skegness to Grimsby are still visible along the entire coa-t the remain,' of a mighty forest. At Addlethorp and Mablethorp the %ranks can be seen at low tide. lienlembering that trees do not flourish close to the sea, the extent of land eaten up can be imagined. A WARRIOR BOLD. At laid week's meeting of the Widnes Town Council it was complained that certain (Acre's in the service of that body had refused to allow snbordin- aka to F. to camp as Territorials "although the Council nad passed a resolutiou giving every facility 110 young men who joined the forces." This ts</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>•-•,-''‘,-.1/40.'Ne-‘,l</head>
        <p>•-•,-''‘,-.1/40.'Ne-‘,l</p>
        <p>another story. The chief clerk of a big firm wad asked by a young fellow on the staff if, in the evett of his j ining the TerritoriAs, he would be allowed to attend the annual trait ing in camp. As this would involve a fortnight's absence from the office, du pint was referred to the head of the fi m, with a strong recommendation from the chief of the depart ment. The employer readily coneentrd, and praised the young man's loyalty amid self,acrilicing s mitt. This was communicated to him by his chief in person ' the latter remarking by the way that the question of of his summer holiday was at the some time settled. •• What dorm mesa ?" nixinualy inquired the yi ung clerk. " Will the airangenient interfere with my usual ten eays' leave !" " Vi by, to be sure," was e teply, " you cannot expect to be off over three weeks, eh ? ' But ilxit aas what he had expected, therefore, he has not joined tl e " Terriers," and den not intend ta do so.</p>
