      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>t PILGRIMAGES</head>
        <p>t PILGRIMAGES</p>
        <p>WARBURTON AND THE WARBURTONS.</p>
        <p>A PATCH WORK CHURCH</p>
        <p>An easy pilgrimage for a summer after- noon, with plenty of intsrest at the price of small exertion, may be enjoyed by as many as will walk or cycle to the village of Warburton. It is certainly an unpromising bit of country that stretches before us as we travel from Lymm, but this flat little village by the river side has a long stretch of history behind it, and is a connecting link with a famous heroine and a famous epoch. It was Ethelfleda, the "Saxon Amazon," widow of Ethelred, who founded Warburton. In the critical days when the danes held the North of England and when the Mersey marked the dividing line between Dane and Saxon, the energetic queen built a chain of fortresses along the river. Any solid bit of ground among the marshes that would hold a fortress or stockade was made into a fortress and garrisoned to hold back the Danes. Such was Thelwall, and such also was Warburton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>WERBURGH TOWN,</head>
        <p>WERBURGH TOWN,</p>
        <p>Queen Ethelfled3 seems to have held in special honour the saintly Werburgh,</p>
