      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>WARBURTON OLD CIIURCEI</head>
        <p>WARBURTON OLD CIIURCEI</p>
        <p>daughter of WuMicro King of Mcrcia. She and her husband had previously dedicated the great abbey of Chester to her honour and the same name was now conferred upon the fortress on the Mersey hank. It is thus that Warburton derives its name. The cluster of dwellings that sprung up became Werb-arge town (it is called Warburg° tone in the Domesday Survey) which later by usage softened into Warburton. After the Conquest the district fell to the Baron of Halton under whom it was held by the Duttons of Dutton.</p>
        <p>were many, but in spite of many efforts to alter the arrangement it lasted for just eight centuries. To tear Lyium from Warburton proved such a big job that the task burned itself into the native mind, and it became a proverb in Cheshire for any thing particularly difficult.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>JOHN BENNET AND WARBURTON,</head>
        <p>JOHN BENNET AND WARBURTON,</p>
        <p>Methodist memories are stirred by the remembrance that John Bennet and Grace his wife, spent several years at Warburton, and it seems probable that he died here. Grace Bennet was formerly Grace Murray, the sainty but fickle maid whom John Wesley would have married, and after the estrangement between these two good men, John Bennet spent his remaining days as minister of a congregation in Warburton. But John Bennet deserves a special chapter and must have it by and by. A. M.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>WARRINGTON BURIAL SOCIETY,</head>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON BURIAL SOCIETY,</p>
        <p>The mealbers of the shore Smiety met it the British Workman Public House, Horsemarket-street on Mon- d.), to celebrate the fortieth anniversary of the establishment of the Society. The atten lance of the members was very good, the meeting being presided I over by the President, Mr James Gillooly, wito was supported by the vice-president, Mr Willivm Taylor ; treasurer, Mr James Parr ; trostees, Messrs Same IGresty and Jcs.ph Adams ; auditors, Messrs David Harris, Frederick S Massey. ; Mr Lawrence Weir, General Secretary end others.</p>
        <p>The CIIA 1112.1 AX congratulated the members upon the steady progress the Society was making year by year. Each one almost seemed to doubla their gain as they watt along end he felt snored that the members would appreciate the financial statement when it was known to them, as it was lull of hope and encouragement. (Applause.) The GENERAL SKCIIKTARY in presenting his annual balance sheet showed that the Society's funds DOW amtunted to £4,458 11.. 64., compared with .61,178 IS.. 10A. for the year previous, a ((aloof £2Bl Ins. 84. The mimeo ioal strength was 1.569 no against 1.5.52 last year; the report further a oAol that the Society bad paid cony iu death claims LU 10s. as compared with £23 the year previous and they Lad invested close upon £5,000. In the elect ion of officers Mr William Taylor wen elected preaioent, Mr Joseph i.,&gt;rnfrey was elected vice-president for the year, Mr David Harris was again elected auditor.</p>
        <p>A very lengthy and interetting progra nm, wee gone throttah comprising the notite of mot ionssliecting the general welfare of 1.1., members on which • very heel, discussion took price ; the following members took part —Meyer.: S Massey, Jo•orta l'omfrey, William Hell, John Haley, Joeeph Adami, James Citesty sod others.</p>
        <p>A rote of thank• IVPS fie1.0,1..1 to the retiring president, Mr James G.HoIy, f.r the •',le and courteous inserter he had couducted the business of the 'Society during his term of office. Mr. JAMICI lJll,Lool.Y in response mid be felt he had I only fulfilled his duty sod etrr, ird out whet he promised when be took aloe.</p>
        <p>• Old ®abar CHICALA A DRY FEED FOP CF-IICKENS AGENTS</p>
        <p>GARTER &amp; SON Bride St. Warrinuton</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>WARBURTON FAMILY.</head>
        <p>THE WARBURTON FAMILY.</p>
        <p>A family that has weathered the changes and chances of eight centuries and held its possessions safely through them all deserves a little notice. But pedigrees are not for hot weather, and we shall only pick out from the family record a few scraps that have immediate interest. About the year 1248 one of the Duttons migrated to Warburton and following the custom of the age took the narre of the place. The Duttons became Warburtons and they survive yet in the great house of Arley. A chivalrous race they proved themselves and the survival of the family through so many centuries was certainly not due to any caution they showed in keeping out of danger. One of them served with the Black Prince coming home again another came through the dangers of Flodden Field. As the ballad has it. The Royall Ratcliffe that rude was never And trustye Trafforde hone to t.ve And WikilWy Warbertone out of Cl;esshire All came with the Earle (I Derbye. A CHILD MARRIAGE. _ _</p>
        <p>In the family history of the fourteenth century we find a story of child marriage similar to those described in our Wilmslow pilgrimage. This time it was the boys ward, Sir John Massey, who led him into marriage. John Warburton who died in 1384 left a son Peter, aged 13, aim, by his will, placed him under the care of Sir John Massey, of Tatton. Now Sir John had a daughter of four years old and seems to have decided that the join- ing of the two families and incidentally the estates would be an admirable thing. He therefore took the children to church and had them married. Theoretically the marriage was a good one, but when Peter grew up he decided that it wouldn't do and proceeded to agitate for a divorce. A record of the Court of Arches shows that the case came up in 1402 and that the request was granted. Ho was ordered however to pay the young lady 550 marks, doubtless as a salve for wounded feelings and • blighted affections. , The next happening suggests that there had been a special reason behind the divorce for Peter immediately married a young r widow. .</p>
        <p>This same Peter proved an especially attractive member of the family, and if he had written an autobiography it would have made good reading. He was one who took up arms on the losing side in the struggle between Richard 11. and Bolinbroke. His Cheshire companions lost their heads at Shrewsbury Cross, but Peter Warburton by some good fortune got safely back again. The male line of the family continued until nearly a century ago, but with the death of Sir Peter Warburton in 1813 the estates passed to a grand nephew, Rowland Egerton. As Egerton Warburton he maintained the traditions of the family and lived long and usefully to be succeeded by the present squire of Arley. ECCLESIASTICAL wattnuttiott. Ecclesiastical Warburton began with a priory of Premonstratensian Canons in the time of Henry It., afterwards absorbed (name</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <p>and all, we hope) in thelAbbey of Cockersand. Ecclesiastical Warburton exists to-day in a lovely old patchwork church kept for the delight of the curious,and a taqeful new one. This is as it should be. Let the old be kept, if only to show us how our fathers used to build, and let the new be placed upon another site. What an antiquarian hunting ground this England would be if Warburton wisdom had been universal?</p>
        <p>THE OLD CHURCH. It would be easy to miss the old church were it not that a friendly sign post points the way. Turning between some old cottages we find ourselves looking upon the ivy- covered tower of one of the quaintest churches in England. The tower is of brick, some of the walls of stone and the body of the church of timber. The stone walls are dated 1645 the brick tower is less ancient still; the real charm and glory of the building is its fourteenth century woodwork. Great rough- hewn timbers spring from the floor to support a heavy timbered roof, and right nobly have they filled their place for the storms of six centuries find them sturdy and unshaken and this despite the ravages of rot and worms. . .</p>
        <p>Nailed to the oak pillars are some quaint hat pegs made from buck's horn, a primitive arrangement to the modern visitor finds most attractive.</p>
        <p>The only relic of the Premonstratensian Canons lies in one of the pews. It is a stone coffin with lid complete, which was dug up from the graveyard. Save that the adjoining field is called the Abbey Croft, we do not know that there is anything at Warburton to recall its ancient monastical glories.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>TEARING IMAM FROM WARBURTON,</head>
        <p>TEARING IMAM FROM WARBURTON,</p>
        <p>A proverbial expression that has travelled far is that which says of difficult things that it is "like tearing Lymm from Warburton." Possibly the saying is as good as dead, but it flourished for centuries and is worth mention- ing if only as a bit of history. From the time of the Conquest the rector- ship of Lymm was held in two halves. The tithes were divided and the arrangement was (we copy this from Leycester, who lived here two centuries ago) that "one parson had one Sunday and the other parson the next Sunday and so by course." One of these half-parsons was appointed by the Warburtons, who always nominated the same man to be rector of Warburton also. The possibilities of friction and inconvenience</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>OUR SERIAL STORY.</head>
        <p>OUR SERIAL STORY.</p>
        <p>[Ail RMHTS RIPERVEI.I.</p>
        <p>Paddock Row, and did not mean to do with- out it.</p>
        <p>Deepe looked almost handaome and quite gentlemanly in his well-fitting clothes. The pile of dirty notes still lay on the table in the drawing-room. Mary took them up now and folded them.</p>
        <p>"Dinner is served, ma'am," said Agnes. Mary thrust the notes into the bosom of her dress. not going to keep them there?' said her husband.</p>
        <p>"Never mind," she replied. "Come and dine; I made that soup myself; it is the best oxtail."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>FOUNTAIN OF BEAUTY</head>
        <p>THE FOUNTAIN OF BEAUTY</p>
        <p>L. 1. MF.ADE, Author of " The Rebellion rf LI Carrington," "A Sweet Girl Graduate... &amp;c.</p>
        <p>CHAPI ERllit I I.</p>
        <p>"YOU EATZN'T LOST rrr.</p>
        <p>Mary Deeps took special pride in prepar- ing a nice dinner for her husband. Arnold was particular with regard to his food ; that is, he had become particular since the day Mary had damned upon his horizon with two hundred and twenty-five pounds in her poc- ket. He liked good wine and carefully-pre- pared dishes, soup with flavour in it, and fish that was reaily fresh. He hated sweets, but liked savouries.</p>
        <p>Mary, who was an excellent housekeeper, Was able to gratify all his tastes. She had got an inexpensive servant, but she could train her, and did so with excellent results. Now, as the hour approached for the meal, Mary Deepe, who had recovered much of her nerve, saw, first of all, that the table with its new damask—white as snow, and with a pro- per gloss upon it—was properly laid with shining silver and sparkling glass. She gave directions with regard to tho fire, and saw that the curtains were drawn. Then she went Into her bedroom. She had not wasted her two hundred and twenty-five pounds in buying much dress; hut she had a few old evening dresses which she used to wear when an inmate of Mr. Renato'll's house. She put one on now of very pale blue crepe-de-chine. It suited her well, clinging to her lissom young figure, adding to her height, and making her look her best. Mary felt excited. On the events of this day all her future hung. She and Arnold would be rich. They could easily let the fiat, and they themselves would go away. In a few years they might return under another name. or they might stay in New York or Sydney or Melbourne, or some other big town in a dis- tant part of the world; and, as Mary ex- pressed it, play the game to come advantage. She felt quite happy. Her fears were lulled. It never once occurred to her even for a single moment that Arnold Deepe could fail in nerve, could show lack of courage at a crucial moment She had got him the dia- mond. She had done the deed. Having first acted the disgraceful part of eavesdropper when poor Mr. lienshall lay dying, she had now added still further to her sin. She bad gone to see Rebecca and laid a trap for her, Into which the girl had quickly fallen. She had all but poisoned Rebecca, but—she had won her cause. A false diamond was subati- tuted for a real one, and Arnold Deepe had gone away with the real stone in his pocket to convert it into glittering gold. Of course, Rebecca had recovered by now, for Arnold had assured his wife that the effect of the poison was only to produce a sudden and immediate torpor which would pass in a short time. . . .</p>
        <p>Mary, therefore, found it possible to forget Rebecca. She returned to her little sitting' room and sat down. She wished Arnold would not keep her waiting so long. It was tiresome to stay for so many hours alone; for she had been accustomed to such $ bustling life. She poked the fire, and looked at her own pretty reflection in the glass, and wondered what their first steps would be after Arnold had secured the money. He would bring it back with him to-night—thotmands upon thousands of pounds! Mary resolved that, whatever the consequences, a part, at least, of this delicious SUM should be spent on her own wardrobe. She had been satisfied with- out a proper trousseau, but there was no earthly reason why she should not get it now that she had, 8o to speak, earned the money herself for its purchase. Her little maid-, Agnes by name, put her head in at the draw- ing-room door. —lt's past seven, ma'am. Do you want dinner served, or will you wait for Mr.</p>
        <p>Deepe "Oh," said Mary. rousing herself, "put the things back as much as you can, Agnes. Mr. Deepe ought to be here in a moment." Just then. Arnold's latchkey was heard in the lock, and Mary felt herself trombling. "There is your master," said Mary, turn- ing to the servant. "Go back to the kitchen at once, and bring in dinner when I ring." She felt impatient at the girl's presence— impatient of everything but her frantic tie- sire to learn the news. In her pretty blue dress, she ran into the little hall. Arnold was there, hanging up his coat. He removed his bat, and put it on the hat-stand. "Well, Arnold— well?" said Mary. He turned and looked at her. "Dinner is ready," she said. "Won't you get tidy—won't you change your dress?" "No!" he said. . _</p>
        <p>She knew then that some disaster had oc- curred, but could not make out what. Her heart smote her with terror. It suddenly oc- curred to her, and for the very first time, that she —she—w Oman as she was, had stronger nerves than the man she had mar- ried. If Arnold Deepe turned out to be weak as well as wicked, Mary would soon learn to loathe him. . .</p>
        <p>She marched into the pretty, brightly-lit drawing-room, holding her head very high. He looked after her with a scowl, and then, all of a sudden, his face softened. What- ever his sins, he loved this queer little vioman, after his fashion. He thought her beautiful. She was more to him than anyone else in all the world. He could not have stood a really good woman. Even a WOMMI like Jenny would have bored him to extinc- tion in a week. But Mary was without scruples. Her conscience was small in quantity and dulled by long disuse. Deepe. after a moment's hesitation, stepped gingerly into the drawing:room. He shut the door. • He came up to her. '4 I've made a fool of myself; you ought to hate me." . . .</p>
        <p>"That is very likely, Arnold," she said, calmly. "But tell me what you have done, and where—where is the diamond?" "Don't talk so loud, walls have ears," he answered savagely. "No one can hear. You're getting upset in your nerves," said his wife. "have you the diamond on your person?" "No,"</p>
        <p>"Good Heavens! You haven't lost it !" " I know where it can be found. I did what I am afraid is a mad thing. I pawned it to a man of the name of—oh ! I had best not tell you—a nan I know. He recognises its value and I can redeem it to-morrow. I will, of course. I'll have more nerve then. I got one thousand pounds for it—in notes, too. Here they are. '</p>
        <p>He thrust his hand into his pocket and took out a lot of dirty notes. "That's wh,y I am so late," said Deepe. "I had to go past the city into the East-end to find the mmi. Bah it was • dirty job, and he is a horrible creature. He'll give me back the gem to-morrow night for an additional hundred pounds. He has promised not to sell it to anybody else during flint tinie." Mary turned deadly pale while her hue-. band was speakinf, but after a moment she steadied herself. rhin was no hour to scold. The _man was broken down.</p>
        <p>"There's hot room," she said, evening things." "I—l can't dress."</p>
        <p>water in your dressing- " and I here laid out your</p>
        <p>You can—and shall. Be quick; Agnes will bring the dinner in exactly ten minutes. It's the sort you love—soup, turbot, a pair of grouse, and a chemei savoury, rye got that special Burgundy also that you like, and some of your own cigarettes. sow. be quick, go and dress." He gave her a wondering, half-stunned sort of glance, anti then went sway to his dressing-room. Mary bad insisted on his pro- viding himself niti, proper evening dress. ithe was accustomed to it, she said, at 0,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>CHAPTER XIII. TKICK ID.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XIII. TKICK ID.</p>
        <p>When dinner was over, and Deepe had eaten with appetite, for the food was so good and exactly to his taste, Mary motioned him to follow her to the drawing-room. You can smoke," she maid, curling her it-If up gracefully in • soft easy chair, "and you may prepare one of those cigarettes for me. We needn't talk for a quarter of an hour."</p>
        <p>He looked at her in some surprise and a renewal of the dread which had troubled him during all the afternoon. But Mary had closed her eyes. When Deepe provided her with a cigarette, she puffed gently little rings of smoke into the air. Deepe felt comforted, and smoked luxuriously himself. He liked a pipe best. The pretty, fresh drawing-room became clouded with • sort of aromatic cloud. The smoke lay against the new cur- Latex and perfumed Mary's davenport and the very chairs in which the couple sat. Sharp to the minute Mary opened her eyes. She flung the end of her cigarette into the fire.</p>
        <p>"Now it's time to talk," she said. "There is no one to listen. Agnes has gone to bed; I told her to go early. She is an innocent. country girl, and is not% accustomed to late hours. Tell me why you did what you did. Why did you lose your nerve?" "It was the feel of the thing against my breast," said Deepe. He had not meant to tell Mary any of his sensations, but her calm blue eyes and firm thin lips seemed to draw his confidence from him.</p>
        <p>"Yes," he continued, "I cannot explain to vou what I felt. You know what a marvel. loos thing it is—unlike anything we hare ever looked at before.— _</p>
        <p>"We know all that," said Mary. "Hurry on, Arnold, do." "I can't talk faster; I must tell my story my own nay," said Arnold, sulkily. ."All right, but please remember that I am in your secret, that I was your accomplice to- day in a matter which might vet easily have meant murder, that I brought you the 'Foun- tain a Beauty'--" "For God's sake, don't talk no loud," said the man.</p>
        <p>talk as I please," said Mary; "all thin low whimpering and muttering attractn attention. If you had half my spunk, not a living soul would suspect you." "I have—as a rule," said the man. "As a rule I have plenty of nerve, but—but—it was that living uncanny thing itself that upset me."</p>
        <p>"You had much better have left it with me. I could have gone to your Permian with it, and he'd have given me the money fast enough, and we'd have been a rich couple to- night. But there—l don't want to scold. Tell Inc what you did." "Don't be angry with me, Mary," said Deepc. "I am awfully broken down. I've got such a fit of shivering over me, I can scarcely hold myse:f together ; and you are my wife, Mary —my—my bride— and I love you dearly."</p>
        <p>• "Ires, that is all right," said Mary. "If I didn't suppose thsit you loved me, Arnold," she continued, with just a touch of softening in her voice, "do you suppose I could have linked my life with yours/ Well, I've done it, and can't undo it, worse luck."</p>
        <p>"Why do you say that? "Isn't what you have done worse luck?" she said passionately. "If I had given you up. I'd have received two thousand pounds, but after committing the most deadly crime, you come back with one thousand. Oh, no, Arnold; I keep the money. You took most of the money which I brought you that day to your lodgings, and I was satisfied, for I thought you just magnifirently wicked. I thought you so wicked that you knew no fear, and that you would not stoop to small things. Now, however, I am changing my mind. Once and for all—l will not stay with a man who is a coward. Go on; tell me what you did." "Where's the use? You say you will not stay with me. I was a coward—worse than you can dream of." Mary was silent. She shut her hard, straight lips. Her eyes grew flinty. Arnold looked at her. His face, compared to hers. ass passionate with suppressed fury. Sud- denly he put out his hand and touched her knee.</p>
        <p>"I swear solemnly to you, Mary, that I will never lose my nerve again. By to-morrow evening we'il have the thirty thousand pounds; we'll leave here—yes, at once." • All right," she said, recovering her former graciousness of manner. "I'm sorry I spoke so harshly to you, Arnold. But really. you drove me mad. Now—pull yourself to- gether. Be a man if you can, and tell me what about to-day." "I took the diamond to Peacock-build- ings." "</p>
        <p>"I told him that—that he could have the diamond if he gave me a cheque for thirty thousand pounds. He refused to give me a farthing of the money until he handled the "Well," said Mar',. "Well? That, surely, was natural enough. "Mary, 1 was overcome by the strangest feeling of mistrust. I simply could not show it to him. If lie had even drawn the cheque I might have borne it better, but he refused to do anything until I showed him the gem. lost my nerve completely. He is an mkt, and sinister, and masterful. I pretended I had not lot it about me." "boot!" said Mary.. "I know I was—l know I was. He didn't believe me; he accused me of being nervous, and said lie could take the diamond- from my person a ithout the slightest difficulty. Then I lost my head altogether. I turned and fled. I was lucky enough to reach the door before he did. I unlocked it, opened it, and went out on the landing. I called to him that I would be back in five minutes, with—it. I expected him to pursue me, but he didn't." "Oh—you—fool!" said Mary again. "Just so—just mo," said Deepe. "What did you do next?" "You remember my telling you of Ben Sherlock?”</p>
        <p>Mary nodded. "I didn't dare to return here with the dia- mond. I had nowhere to hide it. Ben has received stolen goods without question • of course, I knew that I couldn't get • tenth or a twentieth of its value from him. Neverthe- less, he could, if he liked, keep it safely for me until to-morrow. I went to his place in that awful back alley-1 don't know how I escaped with my lifo. Had anyone known what treasure I had about my person, you would be a widow now, Mary." Mary crossed one hand over the other on her knee and made no response. "You are a wife lust one week," con- tinued Deepe. "To-night you would have been • widow--the bride of a week, • widow," he continued, in a sentimental tone.</p>
        <p>"Oh! keep all that nonsenwe to yourself." said Mary. "What happened when you got to this thieves' den?"</p>
        <p>.„ "There is • girl there—Sherlock's daughter—who is as pure as you are. She is a good girl. and she has been a friend to me times and again. She told me that old Ben wee in the worst of tempers—sacage, in fact. I owed him, you see, for a former loan, and had paid him neither interest nor capital. Then I tempted the girl to look at the stone."</p>
        <p>"You showed at to that sort of girl?" "That sort!" said Deeps. "I tell you she is as good as you are." "Good or bud, it doesn't matiter. Go on, finish your tile." _ • . . . . .</p>
        <p>"She looked at it. Her word is her bond. She promincid not to betray me. But she got old Hen all to herself, and struck • bargain with -him. She brought me these back, and has promised on her life that if I return with the thousand pounds and one hundri.cl pounds interest any time before nix to-mor- row evening I shall have the 'Fountain of Beauty ' back. I will take it to Nadir Julaver to-marrow. I won't lose my funk. He will give me the thirty thousand he promised." Mary at very still. After a time, she put her hand into the bosom of her dress and drew out the dirty notes. They were mostly ten pound notes; a few were worth twenty- five pounds, it few fifty. They smelt of tobacco and dirt. Mary .oldei. up into • neat roll. She held the roll in her band.</p>
        <p>"Where do you intend to get the extra hundred pounds?" she said. "There s a hundred of your money still left," was the reply. "You must lend it to me. We'll have our thirty thousand to- morrow."</p>
        <p>"Arnold, I think you have taken leave of</p>
        <p>"Don't scold me!" he said, piteously; only help me." "Now listen." she said, in an author:ta. tive voice. 'You are afraid to ;wage Chit business. You must leave it altogether to</p>
        <p>"To you! But you cannot possibly go to that abominable back alley. It would be at much am your lir.", is worth." "I am not at all afraid ; I shall go early to. morrow morning." " . .'•</p>
        <p>"And with tgat money on your person?" "I tell you lam not afraid. Now get to your room, and to bed. You are no nervous. you cannot possibly recover unless you sleep." The man obeyed her. Already he was feeling her power. He did not for a single moment mean her to do Ws dirty work for him on the next day. He felt quite sure that sleep would restore his mental balance, that his accustomed, bold, impudent swagger would return to him, that he would be able to hold his own once more. _ _</p>
        <p>But when he opened his eyes on the follow- ing morning, it was to see Malty, neatly, but very plainly dressed. She was wearing the costume of a nurse.</p>
        <p>"How &lt;lid you get that?" he said, in aston- ishment.</p>
        <p>"I always keep it by me," she said. "I was a nurse for a year. Nobody ever at- tempts to molest a nurse. I can go anywhere in this dress."</p>
        <p>"'Pon my word, and it suits you," he said. "You look quite beautiful in it." lie stretched out his hand to take hers. He wanted to raise it to his lips. But you can't do this fur me, Mary. Oh! my God! I feel as shaky ac ever." She smiled.</p>
        <p>"I mean to do it," she said. "You are no mor, fit for thie work to-day then you were yesterday. Besides, it will not do. to have you going a second time to Ben Sherlock." "Oh—you will be murdered! Poor, poor M!'7.!" . .</p>
        <p>Not at this hour. Besides, my dress pro- tects me. Now, give me the hundred pounds which you still poemess of mine." He fidgeted. Eventually he gave her the money, taking it from an inner pocket of his waistcoat.</p>
        <p>"There." he said, "when this is spent we shall only have twenty-five over, and all this furniture has to be paid for. We have been ruinously extravagant." "As if that matters," said Mary, "when am coming back with thirty thousand. Oh ! why didn't I do this yesterday !"</p>
        <p>She went into the kitchen, gave a few directions to Agnes, told her husband to wait for her, and went out. She had already got explicit directions as to how to find the dreadful slum off the Commercial-road. and she well knew Peacock-buildings in the City. She reached the court which was the en- trance to the blind alley soon after nine. The nien who worked had gone to work. Several of the miserable children were at school, and many of the women were busy over their chartng or household matters. The court looked horrible, but it WAR com- paratively empty. Mary walked quickly and steadily through it, a train of small gutter children following her. "Come and see mm sick sister, nurse..." "Oh, nurse, come 'cid look in on father; be has broke his arm, and he's worry bad." Mary smiled at them and said she wan busy with another patient, and went straight to Sherlock's house. She asked there for Jenny Sherlock. A woman with a COIITS and bloated face opened the door. She said that Jenny was omit, and that neither she nor her man could see anyone. "I have come on very special business," said Mary, "and I must see Mr. Sherlock. Let me in, please."</p>
        <p>Her mariner was so firm and determined that the woman, soddened with drink, gave way before her. Mary entered the house. A minute later she found herself in the little parlour where Deepe had shown the diamond to Jenny. Hardly any light penetrated through the dirty window. The room was greasy and sickening in the extreme. It smelt of foul tobacco. It had that inde- scribable smell which is only known in the worst East-end habitations.</p>
        <p>Mary drew her breath several 'times sharply and quickly. All of a sudden she hoped that she would not faint. No wonder poor Arnold lost some of his nerve in a place like this. Just then, the door was opened, gmd a shaggy man, coarsely dressed, with a dirty shirt and a coat covered with grease, and a lopg beard of several weeks growth, entered the room.</p>
        <p>He was a powerful man, with a cast in one eye, a crooked mouth, and altogether a most repulsive appearance. " Now, what do you want t" he said roughly. "We ain't none of us ill that I know of."</p>
        <p>" 'Tisn't a case of illness," said Mary, speaking in her clearest, sharpest, firmest tone. "I put on this nurses's dress for safety, for a respectable woman hasn't much chance in the place where you live, Mr. Sherlock." "Keep your reflections to yourself,'. growled Sherlock. "I will, after I have explained my errand. I have come here for my husband, A:l'okt Deepe. I am his wife. This is his card. He has written some directions on it."</p>
        <p>The man stared furtively at Mary—then an odd expression came over his face. You want the bauble back?" he said.</p>
        <p>"That's about it," said Mary. "Well, you 'a come in good time; but I must have my I dred pounds on the loan. Eleven hundred pounds in my band, and you get the bauble again." Mary very calmly took the roll of money from inside her dress. She counted the notes deliberately, then she held her hand clenched firmly over them. "Put the diamond in here," she said, opening her other hand, "and you can have the notes."</p>
        <p>The man left the room muttering. When he came back he looked_ at her.</p>
        <p>" lou re a spit-fire, I guess, he said. "You'll do that weak chap good, you will. You're not of the sort my Jenny's made of. You're a spice o the wicked one in you; but you'll make Arnold Deepe—curse him !—sit up. Why, he ain't had • man. Here's your bauble."</p>
        <p>He laid the glittering stone in the girl's hand, which closed over it convulsively. She gave him the notes. "Good-bye," she said. "I'll tell the neigh- bours as I walk through the court that you're better." "Ay, do," he said, with a loud, triumphant sort of laugh; "and make yourself scarce, foe 'taint safe to carry a gem o' that sort about your person." Mary had made preparations for the recep- tion of the diamond. It was her turn now to wear a piece of ribbon round her neck. To the ribbon was fastened a little bag. She slipred the diamond into it and quickly passed through the court. A child cried piteously to her to come and attend to her sick mother. She promised to send another muse, and departed. Her feet shook so that she could scarcely walk; her knees trembled under her. Oh, to I what depths had she sunk when she had to 'visit such a hell on earth as the den of Sher- ' 100 i : How was abe ever to bold ttu her head</p>
        <p>again? Wa.4 the diamond worth it? She had that curious sense almost of panic coming over her which had visited Decpe on the pre- ViOUS day. When she reached the Bank sho did not hesitate, however, to call a han,om. She got into it and was driven to the en- trance to Peacock Buildings. She even de- sired the man to wait.</p>
        <p>"There's nothing like doing things boldly," she said to herself.</p>
        <p>The entrance to the court was too narrow for a carriage to pa.:s through, and Mary had to walk the few yards which lay between the entrance and the house where Nadir Julaver lived. Again she felt her knees trembling. She went up the shallow stepe, worn with age, worn with the tread of thousands of feet. She found herself in the entrance. She looked at the names painted in white on the black board. Nal• r Jularer this name alone stood for the possessor of the fourth floor. She went up the dirty worn stairs. She paused for breath on each landing. She wondered why her breath came so fast and why that grow- ing terror, greater and greater each minute, asiailed her. At last she reached the Persian's floor. The landing was somewhat wide, and there were several rooms. Mary's eyes, however, took in nothing but one fact. A sheet of paper was tacked lightly on the door of the main entrance—the door which bore the name of Nadir Julaver. In a handwriting which almost resembled print, so legible was it, Mary read a few words: "Nadir Julaver has left town for the present. Any letters which come for him will be kept at his office until further notice." Mary's head truly reeled. How she went down those stairs, how the diamond seemed to burn more and more against, her heart, she could ne‘er tell. Iler hansom was waiting for her. She knew that Deepe had missed his great, his golden chance—miised it, be- cause of his sudden pusillanimity! They must sell the dkamond now for what it was worth!</p>
        <p>She drove home. She felt tired, reckless. She knew well the difficulty which Deepe would have in dispo.sing of the gem; but at least she had got it; that was one consola- tion. There were surely people to be found in London who would give ten—perhaps twenty—thousand pounds for anything so unique, so glorious, so splendid. Her spirits rose a little. She paid her cabman and went upstairs. It was Deeps himself who opened the door. She came in at once and motioned him to fol- low her into their dining-room. "I have got it!" she said; gone." "What? what?" said Deeps. Nadir Julaver has left London. I went to his rooms. There was a notice on his door to say that he had gone. There was no ad- dress. His letters were to be kept for him." Deepe sank into a chair. His face was the colour of death</p>
        <p>" but he is</p>
        <p>"Gone!" he muttered—"gone! My God, my God! But Mary, you have got "Yes. You sent me to a loathesome place. I never saw any creature quite so bad as that man Ben Sherlock."</p>
        <p>"Mary—we must get rid of it; we mustn't keep it. Nadir Julaver knows that I have it. He will get it from inc—he will get it from me, somehow. He is clever—as clever as the devil. That move of him, going away, means unutterable things. Mary—give it co me!"</p>
        <p>"Sell it for the best price you can get," she said.</p>
        <p>--bee—pe—• took it from the little bag and held it in his hand. All of a sudden he snatched up" Get shat lunch—something good to eat—l am half fainting,' he said. 'lll be back in a minute."</p>
        <p>"No—no," said Mary, the gem with you like that.' "I must," he said. "I'll be back in a few minutes."</p>
        <p>"You can't take</p>
        <p>Before she could stop him he was out of the house and down the stairs. She clasped her hands tightly together as a woman will who is in despair. Then suddenly, pulling her- self together, she helped Agnes to Inc the table for lunch. The two had just done this when Deepe's voice was heard calling through the keyhole. His voice had almost risen to a shriek.</p>
        <p>"Let me in</p>
        <p>Mary remembered that he had not taken his latch key; but why didn't he knock? She went herself to open the door for him. Ile staggered in. "Mary," he said, "we're done! I suspected that stone; I took it to a jeweller. He ap- plied a test. It's—it's—a faked stone! Nadir Inlayer has the real one; and he's off, bark' to Persia. Mary—Mary—we're penniless!" He grovelled at her feet. Then he lay still. He had fainted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>VICTORY AT LAS</head>
        <p>VICTORY AT LAS</p>
