      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Prizes for the Best KI pt liorne.—Many endowments have been left by worthy people for the encourage. went of the domestic art,' oral virtues. and these old time bounties have taken various forms, such as prizes for he hest churchuer, for those of the pane, who could brat repeat the Ten C. mmandinerea, and there was the custom at Donmow of awarding a flitch of lam n to the couple who for a yeer and a day had not quarra lied or rer anted of tin ir marriage A new can- petition on similar lines ha. been starred by tbe North British Academy of Arts which is a Kering two prizes of -CIO each ennually—one for wives and the otter for daughtg ra of idowers—" for the best kept Lome, regard being had to arrays and metier, character, number of children, the ir ague. and other considerations, I, fluencing and attaching to the making of a happy home" this competition is for the present confiled to Newcastle, Gatesheel, rand Tyneside generally, but it is hoped later to increase the nileolane of awards and throw them open to the whole of the U eited Kingd am. liappiners In the domestic circle cannot he bought with money, like ready male clothing, but heart be c,tlti • vetted and if its eon aursgement can ho promoted by such competition-, the example of the Newcastle Academy of Arts might well be followed by other ' institvtions, and lay wealthy folks who hope to lo.ve the swirl better than they found it. hot Weather Philos phere.—Many parts of thecountry , have enjoyed a prolonged veil of fine weather ranch as none of ns living may over see ',vain, but it has con- ' vinced not a few that It in possialo to have even too much summer. Neither in clothing or in our manner of living are we fitted to enj ay very warm weaihnr, and although we bare pli nty of advice as to him to keep lanppy though hot," we gain little or nothing from it. SOlllO tell us that we should live and eleep ins tint, others with experience of the tr pica, advise us I. shut our doors end windows dining the hottest time of the day. Ttey forget that our hawses are not con- *trawled nub massive wells and roof to keep out heat, as are those in the tropics, and that to aunt our doors and windows undor a talegin: 8,,n, would be Score way of getting eloaly cooked. Others offer us advice as to how to Induce sleep in the hot weather, bat es a rule to follow it only helps to make our nights still more broken. Moreover one ganerolly notioes that Parr dress rerorn err, the food reformer, and the op 'sties of maol• nets generally, sac as mirth overcome by tropical host as MU.</p>
        <p>Unprotected Women.—The Countess Russell, in all interesting account of a earn an nape iltion she under- took with our other leviies, rays they were well pro- tected by a dog and a revolver. We hire been reminded, much too frequently of late, that such protection Is not unnecesa.ry in lonely country districts where there have keen many ruffianly attacks upon defenceless women. There is also a sew invention, which if it comes into the hands of the criminal class, is especially adaptel to make the highways and railway travelling more unsecore for women. This is a pistol, using chemical cartridges. It is noiseless, does not kill, but renders anyone against whom it is used ancoasoious for ten minutes or more. The danger of such a weapon coming into ths hands of evil-doers, is very obvious, especially to women left alone in the house during the day time who would be liable to he attacked by any stranger on their attending a ring at the door.</p>
        <p>The Ileliday Cot.—Ths app rat of Colonel L rckwo ol and Sir P. Banbury on behalf of the holiday cat comes a little late, because for very many people the holiday asason is not only" Impending " but bas actuilly com- menced. But none the loss their letter is svelcome,and it merits the consideration of all htimane people. It really is to be expected that people who are going to enjoy theme-Ives should have some consideration for their cats and make proper provision for them to receive food and drink whilst the house remains untenonted. No doubt the vast majority of honors- holders do make Some inch arrangements, but tsere I are still many sho are guilty of the cruelty of leaving the cat to fare so beat it can, or in other w 3rds, to starve. If they find it too much trouhle to provide for the animal's maintenance, they could at least adopt the suggestion offered in the appeal referred to, and send the eat to somo institotion where it would he mercifully destroyed.</p>
        <p>Fuller Skirts —Dime Fashi in, like the Pin of the world, is for the time being taking her holidays, but what few models have been shown for the early Autumn indicate a change to fuller skitta eel inn nue of the desigos the width of the garment is increased by • pleat down the centra of the back. Such • change is indeed inevitable, seeing that the tube costume, after descending through all the writ on grades of Society is now the almost universsl mode of" the maser," This state of things has ft-Aurally leesened the demand for theme meter!a's, and manufacturers will not be sorry to ate the disappearance of a mode, which, among other thing', is said to be responsible for the death of two ladles, who although exprtt swimmer.% were drowt ed in ahontingaccideat owing to their being hamperedwi hobble akiete. There have been complaints from various industrial centres of the adverse effects of skimpy modes upon local manufactures, and one can under- stand that the effect must be 000ridemble, seeing that dresses which formerly required twelve to sixteen yards of materials and lionag, are now compos dof much leas than half.</p>
        <p>Innovations ant Novelties.—The heat is responeltrie for many novelties and innovations is the world of do 83, and one of these is the long white linen coat trimmed with large gold buttons, and hiving black silk revers and cuffs, which are of costae removable Again, collars and coifs of white drill give • navy serge an appearance of coolness, especially if the whole is crowned by a white hat with white wings. It seems thia yearns thorigh it is impels ible to have too much white, and the most popular colour is navy. Cream has disappeared, except perhaps one particularly creamy shade of light liscurt. Black 11 greatly in evidence and touches of it appear most triesp,ctiAly n frocks, hats, cloaks, &amp;c., and where three are deftly i•trodoced the effect is eery good. Certainly black makes an admirable foil for brilliant colour games, on which it has a sobering effect. Fringe ireveryrchere, rind adorn. wide impartially our bags, slipper,. bate, frocks and parasols, in most instances it must be confessed, with singular auroras. New Neckwear.—Dainty neckwear plain a very ; important part in the toilettes of to-day, and never was there a greater varirey. Bat although the peke of the pretty seta one sees in every shop window is very moderate, a considerable sawing can be effected in • small allowance if these are male at home, and often the home-made set fits better than that purchased ready- made. Now is the time to dive into the treasures hoarded by cur grandmothenr,and to bring forth dainty trifles of lawn and lac, that may be utilised for wear on neck and wrists, and which accord no well with the style of dress in vogue. Fortunate indeed are the possessors of oil fashioned lace or embroidered sleeves which have not perhaps sewn the light for many a decade, hot are now a Joy, and prised accordingly. The Directoire or side frill is still popular, the latest of these taking the shape of a triangular piece of Irish crochet, bordered with a tiny muslin or lace frill. Trish crochet io revers shape is a nuck finish to the pretty little Q raker dresses i bat make snob ideal frocks for girls. it. li,uroftniuo embroidery used on limp cotton blouses in ochre and pale pink shad., is gay and holiday like. and with these should be worn a nacklet of brighl, bola us Bois•.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>'I he chief attraction at the flippadrt me (Ilia week is the performance of Marie Santoi and her company in " A night in Japan." Though only lasting some twenty minutes, this Niel:aeries! comedy is cramtet d full of tuneful music and pretty dancing. There is es-en a semblance of a love-romance - strongly eminiscent of the real "San Toy" and a lively wishy washy, a fit rce emperor, a charming princess and • g atilt English naval officer. One of the most effective numbels ia the import. d musical scena " Math the abadew of its Pyramids," in whit h the four pretty maidens who form the chorus make • de'ightful backgrt nod cf moving coLur, aid Miss Santoi rings most tot e'ully. Tbe mounting of the musical comedy is really excellent, end the the scenes would be a credit to many more ambitions productions. " The Three Thieves " is a sketch decidedly inside the o'clinaty run, and capably pro- duced by Messrs. Keno Johnston, Edward Gusto!), and Charles Sims. The comic burglar is a really amusing creation, and his method of righting a wrong extremely diverting. George Barnett and Irene West, described as the globe-trotters, put vt y lancing of the most skilful and eccentric character. Thnugh only on the stage a fe • minutt a they give a capital turn aal earn hearty applause. A woesl pianoforte absurdity entitled " A pantomime Hy " nffords Jimmy bans-, re and Eva Rosslyn ample scope for low comedy t dent, an I there is many a laugh at the trial of voice and figure for the ptntomime role Ilappy tttwo 0 (wit -se jokes are occasionally unhappily chosen) and Walter Stanley, appear a ith successes comedi ins, while Shepherd end Granville complete the bill with a 'moderate cross-tall and comedy boxing act. There are the usual musical and pictorial items during the teeing—nil high-class as usu I.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>4 ',MARVELLOUS CURES OF RCZEMA.</head>
