        <p>- ------- " Two of my danghters and tad of my sons tette suffel leg from very btd heads, the doctor 'orditi ing my daughters to have all their hair cut off so as t prevent it from spreading all over their heads, and to get to the sest of the tr 'WM in order to be able to rub some oiminent, wit'cli he g,vd them, well into the sores. These sores started in small blotches which got larger aail lager and thin buret and spread all over their heads, and in coins ca-es down their backs and b dies. The rbator said it was eczema, but they seemed to be daily getting some. The sores formed like little boils, then fiPed with rna4zr, and looked like absesmes. They becarns all infl tmed and i•ritate 1, causing them to be roost intly scratching themselves. This made the sores burst, and the matter 11 .wed all over their heads. We had to cut their finger nails and at times to put gloves on their hands, the irritation was so bad, to keep then from digging into the flesh, land at vight they would nearly lair thoir limbs off. ' The Wirers would be li•erally covered with blood. " The teachers would not let them attend school fur fear of infection spreading among the other children, so I res Iced to try the Cutictua Remedies, 'which I found to henfit my children gently. 1 c manned with the Cuticura Soap and Cuticura Ointment for one month, and the result is a marvellout cure." (Signed) George Arthur R Mason, 37, Fitzwilliara St., !bylined Common, nett. Barnsley, Yorks, Jan. 3, 1911. Post- free, from F. Newbery &amp; Sons, 27, Ch trterhouse Lood m, samples of Cuticura Soap and Ointment, with 32 p. bdok on skin treatment.</p>
