      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>DISTRICT nV3</head>
        <p>DISTRICT nV3</p>
        <p>Winwlck.</p>
        <p>CHOIR TRIP.—The members of the Parish Church choir bed their annual excursion on Saturday,Southport being the place visited. The party, numbering about thirty,went to the station in a 'bus. Arrived at South- port, they dispersed sod enjoyed themselves, each in his own way, meeting at Hayes' restaurant for dinner and tea. A very eejoyable day wan spent, thanks to the Rev. Canon Penrhyn, who supplied the funds, and also msee all arrangements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>Frodsham.</head>
        <p>Frodsham.</p>
        <p>DRtNKtN Lovnis DISSP IR.—At Frocleham, OD Wednesday, Harry Leader, a groom-coachman, of Worsley, Manchester, was charged with drunkenness and disorderly behaviour and also with having used threats towards Annie Fletcher, a domestic servant in the employ of Mr H P Mortimer, of Crofton Lodge, Kingsley.—Policemonstable Whalley said he found prisoner drunk in the yard of Crofton Lodge. Prisoner said he bad come to see Miss Fletcher. Witness told him it was very late, and he had better go home. He replied that the girl had given him up, sod hp would shoot himself.—Elizabeth Warburton, cook at Crofton Lodge, said he met prisoner on Sunday night. They spoke of Annie Fletcher, and prisoner remarked, "Annie is everything to me. ant my life is utterly ruined. I will shoot her and myself." He was drunk.—Prisoner expressed his penitence. and wept in the d oh. He said he did not mean what he had eaid.-03 promising to leave the district he was bound over.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>Helsby.</head>
        <p>Helsby.</p>
        <p>Fi,owc Sthsw.—The annual Hewer show and spnrts, held under the auspices of the Helsby Athletic Club, took place on Saturday in brilliant weather. The show was rather smaller than in previous years owing to the dry season, but the quality of the exhibits was most satisfactory. Messrs .roogood's silver challenge shield went to Mr H Gaskell, of Frodsham, and the Rev Clifton Smith's silverchallenge bowl for sweet peas NVILS won by Mr J Stanley Bower, of Frodsham, but the competition was a poor one. Mrs. Crosland Taylor's medal for the highest points in the cottagers' class went to Mr Joshua Atherton, of Helsby. Thu table decoration awards were :-1, el Brotherton ; 2, Alma Cross Taylor; 3, J M Taylor. Dr Briant of Helsby, was a frequent winner lathe open class, which included some unusually fine fruit dames. Mr hi A Taylor had a magnificent group of plants arranged for effect. The sports were watched by a large crowd, and some excellent racing took place. Details :—Boys 1, W Smith, Frodsham ; 2, H Parker, Helisby ; 3, W Pope, Helsby. ICO Yards Flat Hand icap.-1, .1 Halworth, Runcorn; 2, A Johnson, Hoole ; 3, J E Williams, Hawarden. 140 Yards Obstacle Race.-1, F Leach, Helsby ; 2. J Johnson, Chester ; 3. A W Lewis, Kingsley, One Mile Bicycle Handicap.-1, W J Pee., Whitby ; 2, P Foxley, Ellesmere Port ; 3, Joe Garner, Helsby. 220 Yards Flat Handicap.- 1, T, Johnson, Runcorn ; 2, J W Johnson, Halton ; 3, N H Wright, Christleton. Half-mile bicycle race.-1, W 0 Price, Whitby ; 2, P Foxley, Ellesmere Port; T Dodd, Whitby. 440 yards flat handicap.-1, J W Johnson, Halton; 2, U N Spencer, Frodsham ; 3, C H Wright, Bamton. 100 yards sack race.-1, R Barber, Helsby ; 2, T Connor. Latchford ; 3, S Hilditch, Hartford. Half-mile fl t handicap.-1, JII Newton (age 17) ;2, W Nelson, Weston ; 3, E D Gordon. Weaverham. Hill race.-1 and cap-winner : Albert Hall, Sandiway ; 2, F Bibby, Helsby ; 3, J Norcrass, Alvanley ; 4, A W Meredith, fieleby. Winner's time : 7mins. 31 1-sseoa. The prizes were distributed by Mrs G Crosland Taylor. The Heloby Silver Band was in attendance, and played for dancing in the evening.</p>
        <p>LYNN SESSIONS. Lymm Petty Sessions were held on Wednesday morn• ing at the Pollee Statien. Mr. A. V9atkin presided, and his brother magistrates were Admiral Jones-Parry, Major J. E. G. Groves and Mr. A. W. Calleudar. MANAGER ASSAULTS WOMAN WORKER. HEAVY PENALTY. -</p>
        <p>John Johnson, manager of Mr. Cowper's fustian cutting shop, Lymm, was summoned for vomiting on the 26th ult., Emma Wadsworth, wife of harry Wads- worth, farm labourer, Rush Green, Lymm.—Mr. T. S. Steel appeared for the complainant and Mr. A. Browne, who represented the defendant, put in a plea of not guilty. Mr. Steel said that the complainant had been in the employment of Mr. Cowper as a fustian cutter for 10 years. Johnson was the manager of the workshop and on the 26th July between 8 and 8.30 a.m. the com- plainant before stetting her work was desirous of open- ing her window. It was very hot and she wanted a little more fresh air. The defendant was standing near at the time and he shouted to her (as she was still trying to oven the window) " Shut that b— window" and ehe replied that if Dr. de Jong the medical officer of health) were there he would soon have it open. The defendent told her to take her book" and forthwith took her knife eel guide away. She asked him to pay her for the work she had done and she began to measure the amount of stuff she had completed. As she was pulling her work up the DEFEND INT RCSHED AT HER AND STRLTK HER IN</p>
        <p>Tlig NV•K. He then got hold of her by the neck and took her to the top of the stairs and tried to throw her down. However, the complairant managed to stick to the top stairs and eventually she got to the middle of the room when the defendant released her. As soon as she had measured up and was going across the room to go down stairs complainant took up a strainer—a piece of iron two feet long and nearly an inch In diameter—and threw it at defendant. However, he " ducked " and missed it, and then ran after her shouting " I will kill you." S3lllO awful language was employed by the defendant. Then complainant, along with nine other women, proceeded to Mr. Cowper's house to complain about the defendant's conduct, but as he was not in they could not do no. Since the assault Mrs. Wadsworth had been attended to by Dr. de Jong and she %cal euffering from a bruised neck, side and leg and a cut hand. Mr. Steel raid complainant was noting properly when she tried to open the window and it was a very serious offence for a man who was placed in charge of a number of women to so far forget himself subs did and he (Mr. Steel) hoped that defendant would be punished accordingly. It was really scandalous that Mrs. Wadsworth should have been treated as she had been. The plaintiff corroborated the statement of Mr. Steel end added that the struggle between her and the defendant lasted 10 minutes. Since the assault she had only been able to do a little work. Mr. Browne (cross-examinieg plaintiff) : How many times hsve you been discharged since you have been working there 10 years ?—No answer. How many times have you been convicted in this Police Court ?—That is nothing to do with this. Were you told that the next time you came to the Court you would be convicted without the option of a the ?—lbst is nothhig to do with the question. After hesitating she added that she did Lot remember it being so. How many times have you been convicted do not know whether it is three. Were you summoned two months ago? Yes, the day before the Coronation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>I SAID "A sw-sAa" TO NIT HUSBAND</head>
        <p>I SAID "A sw-sAa" TO NIT HUSBAND</p>
        <p>in my own house. Mr. Browne Fined 7s. 9d. Is it not a fact that Mr. Johnson (the defendant) only took you beck because he was prevailed upon to do no by Mrs. Johnson, just to give you one more trial ?—No answer. Was it not against the wink of Mr. Johnson that you went?—! cannot say that. Do you know that the window you were trying to open has never been opened for something like two years ?—No. Were there not plenty of windows open in the shop ? —I could not say. Didn't the defendant say : " Come away from that window it is nailed up."—No. The whole of the shop is subject to Mr. Johnson's orders?— It is through spite. When he told you to come away why didn't you obey ?-1 did and he struck me on the neck. The plaintiff did not reply to the question of Mr. Browne as to whether the frame she was working at was only three yards from the stairs and In reply to a further question she said that she was going to go down the stairs quietly, but the defendant would not give her • chance. Cf:he also said she got hold of the iron strainer when the struggle was over. Why should Mr. Johnson treat you as you say ?—He took his revenge out of me because of some bother on the bottom storey. Mrs. Worrell gave a similar story and said that when the complainant threw tha utraioer at Mr. Johnr.fn he WILL Nit"ltrirtt tort . had immediately ran c1619a the htilin iirtor her. In reply to Mr. Browee aritnene said that the defendant told the compltinsnt to go away from the window. Mr. Browne then opened his case for the defence. Me raid that the workshop was subject to the Inapec- lion of the factory Inspector and there was pleniy of ventilation. He considered It highly improbable that s man could have treated a women es had been described merely beceute elm opened a window. If a men wee defied in his o an workshop he was justified in tieing reasonable force to get her away. After the defendant bad asked the complainant to leave the window as it was nailed up he merely got hold of her and when she said she would go quietly he released hbr. He was merely upholding the conduct of the workshop by i acting n the may he did and he (Mr. Browne) asked the, Bench to dismiss the case. The de fendent In the box corroborated his solicitor's story and seid that the wnote ArrAIR oxtv 'LASTED I.'oOn If INUTIt9. Ile bad no cause whatever to act as had been alleged.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel (to defendant) : How many times have yon been here ?—About twice. Were yon not summoned for assaulting another woman ?—About eight or ten years ago and it was dismissed.</p>
        <p>The Clerk (Mr. H. A. Harris) read the police record of cases which showed that in 1895 the defendant was fined ss.</p>
        <p>The Defendant : I never remember it. Mr Steel How many people were in the workshop at the time of the affair ?—About ten or a dozen.</p>
        <p>And out of those you are only going to have one witness—a man ?—Yes..</p>
        <p>Defendant denied haying struck the complainant and affirmed that he got hold 0 f her and she pulled him about the shop. In reply to the Chairman the defendant admitted using bad language in the workshop. John Ratcliffe said that he did not see the " scene" commence. He heard a noise and saw Mr. Johnson end Mrs. Wadsworth pulling each other about, but be went on with his work. . . .</p>
        <p>_ The Chairman : Are these squabbles with the manager and the employees of a regular occurecce.— Not no bad as this. The Bench retired and returned after a few minutes absence, the Chairman said the evidence bad dis- closed a state of affairs in the fastian cutting shop in question which wee</p>
        <p>MOST_ USA VOURY AND UNPLEASANT..</p>
        <p>Language bad been used in the assault which appeared to be usually employed there. It ens moat disgraceful and they were determined to put it down if they could. In that case the Bench were unanimous that an assault hal been committed and they would fine the defendant I and costs. The fine, he concluded, would have been larger I were it not that the costs were great. They also I allowed the advocate's foe (.£3 3e. 61 in all). A DISPUCED WILL. Henry Thompson, of the Mariner's Home, Egtemont, was summoned for feloniously stealing four blankets, value 285., the property of Joseph Lord, of Lymm, on the 26th May.—Fhe defendant applied for an adjourn- ment on the ground that he had not had time to pro- cure witnesses.—The circumstances disclosed were that I the prosecutor was left by will the property of the sisters of the defendant amounting to £2B On. Bd., and the defendant objected to this. While staying at the how° of the prosecutor it is alleged that be took away the blankets, untying that they belonged to him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>Grappenhall.</head>
        <p>Grappenhall.</p>
        <p>THE LATE MR. MATTHEW LOMAS. DEATH OF MR, MATTHRW LomAs.—We regret to announce the death of Mr. Lomas, of Lush Villas. Grappenhall. After a rather short illness Mr. Loniss succumbed on Wednesday. His genial personality will be missed by a great many, for he was well known and highly respected. So Into as the Coronation he was active in public affairs, taking great interest In the festivities at Grappenhall, and holding the office of the Cnairman of the Entertainments Committee. To a great many people Mr. Lomas will he best remombe,ed as an old employe of Messrs. J. Crosfield and Sons. For upwards of 43 years he bad been attached to the clerical stall and was the oldest employ of the firm. Mr. Lomas has done a lot of useful work and was ever ready to take his share of private duty. For over 20 years he was secre- tarylof the " Mount Hermon " Lodge of the Ancient Order of Foresters. For over 40 years he assisted the Grappenball Church choir, of which he was a member. He was also one of the earliest members of the War- , rington Musical Society. The funeral will take place at Grappeuhall Church to-day at 3 o'clock.</p>
        <p>Llsool . . s • • • . 4" - , _ :..;... , ~,,or . ..__ ...</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNEN .</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNEN .</p>
        <p>CURIOUS HOUSE FIRE.</p>
        <p>A fie bappeaed at the houae of Mr. Ford, Dover. street, Runcorn' on Wodnesday afternoon. Oae of the bedrooms was boiag fumigated with sulphur, which forced its way through the bottom of the bu aket which contained it, and set the Hawing on fire. Toe brigade quickly arrived, and got the fi-nes under, though the fumes of the sulphur proved very trying to the men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>NOT "ALL RIGHT."</head>
        <p>NOT "ALL RIGHT."</p>
        <p>At Runcorn on Wednesday, William Keogh and John Hughes, of Cooper-street, were clorgad with stealing 1631ba. of lead from a shop in Church-street. The evidence showed that Keogh had been working as a bricksetter, altering the shop front. He was dis- charged on Wednesday of last week. Next day he arranged for a marine store dealer to call at the shop in the early morning with a donkey and cart. On this the lead was loaded, at six o'clock, and conveyed to a dealer, who purchased it on Keogh's assurance that it was all right." A further quantity was purchased by Mr. Colling's, plumber, the following Monday, and Hughes assisted to remove this. Keogh was sent to gaol for two months and Hughes for one month. The beach issuad a warning against the purchasing of lead in small qtontities and at unusual business bouts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians was held at the Dutton Workhouse on Tues- day afternoon. In the absence of Mr. J. Spruce, Mr. C. E. Linaker, jun. the VICI -chsirman, presided over a fair attendance. The officers present were :—Messrs G F Ashton !clerk), W E Hough (assistant clerk), J F Pearson, T. Cooke and W R DePrez (relieving officers), and .1 Parsons (master).</p>
        <p>MEDIf;AL !URN AS PUBLIC SERVANTS.</p>
        <p>The Merthyr Tydfil Union forwarded the following resolution : That this Board being of opinion that it is better to pay for prevention of disease and illness rather than for the cure of same believes that the time has now arrived when the medical practitioners of this country should become public servants." There was general laughter when the csocluding portion of the resolution was read and Mr. Davies immediately moved that the resolution lie on the table.</p>
        <p>Several members wanted to second the motion and amid further laughter it was agreed to.</p>
        <p>lUIGNATIO OF A NURSE.</p>
        <p>Nurse rent 115800 sent in her reeling—T.lo. n stating that the cause of leaving may a desire for a change. The resignation was accepted..</p>
        <p>TUE BARBER'S TESTIMONIAL.</p>
        <p>----- The Clerk Enid he bad received a letter from Mr. Brett, who was the barber at the institution, asking for a testimonial as he was making application for the post of porter in a large Union. The Chairman said it was not usual for the Board to give testimonials to contractors and the Board decided to refuse the request</p>
        <p>PA UPER LUNATICS.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported that the rate of maintenance of pauper lunatics in the Chester Asylum for the quarter ending September next was fised at Bs. 91. per head per week. RUNCORN RURAL COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>roe fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural Council was held immediately after the Board of Guardians. Mr Thomas Wright presided and the °facets present were Messrs 0 Flmon (clerk) W If &amp;mall (assistant clerk), .1 Swinton and W Diggle I (Survey.* J Farritiit4; I,lnspector), and Dr Adm.: (Medical Officer vt Health). SUPPOnT FOR 7115 1. INFIRMARY. In !submitting the M 1511103 of the Finance Committee Mr. Wooderard eaid that they recommended that the Council inbaoribe £5 ss. per annual to the Warrington Innetnary. He remarked that it was a deserving losototion and one which greatly benefitted the district.</p>
        <p>Mr. Talbot moved au amendment that the subscription be .ClO, as the Infirmary was doing a great de d of good Mr. Woods ard said he had gnat pleisure in second- ing the amendment, for he was quite sure that the Finance Committee would scree with it.</p>
        <p>M. Hallows and Mr. Pilling supported nn iit was unanimously agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>OUTBREAK OF DIPOTHEIII%.</head>
        <p>OUTBREAK OF DIPOTHEIII%.</p>
        <p>Dr. Adams submitted his report which stated that 15 eases or diphtheria had occurred in the district during the _lest month.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward said it was a very seriols matter. There were only four beds in the hospital for cases of</p>
