      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS TERRITORIALS. WELCOMED HOME FROM CAMP.</p>
        <p>St. Helens on Sunday welcomed home the members of the three Territorial battalions who had been in camp at Farleton. The sth South Lancashire Rifles were due back first, but in each case the men were over two hours late. Milnthrop Station provcd quite inadequate for the 10,000 men of the South Lancashire Brigade and their equipment. The 2nd Lancashire Royal Engineers arrived home about 3.45, and the R.A.NI.C. Field Ambulance at 5.30. The Rifles had an average number of 703 men and twenty-four officers in camp. Colonel lhomas, the commanding officer' vas obliged to leave camp early in the first week, and the battalion was under the command of Major Lionel Pilkington. The Engineers numbered 440, including the field companies and telegraph section, all under the command of Colonel J. P. Framr. The 2nd West Lan- cashire Field Ambulance, R.A.M.C., which included sections from Barrow and Randal, was under the com- mand of Colonel R. Jackson, and numbered about 400. All sections have had a busy and useful training, and the arrangements generally gave great satisfaction. TRADESMAN'S WIFE SENT FOR TRIAL The two charges of using certain instruments for an unlawful purpose were heard at St. Helens. on Tuesday against Annie Houghton, wife of a welLknown tradesman, of 25, Talbot-street. Mr. E. W. Swift defended. . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Turton, deputy town clerk, prosecuted, and said the proceedings were ia the native of a preliminary inquiry, and it was for the magistrates to decide whether, on the evidence that would be tendered to them, there was sufficient to put her on trial. If it won sufficient, or if there was a strong presumption raised es to the guilt of the accused, she ought to be committed for trial. He thought that the facts to be given in evidence would quite satisfy their worships. Inspector Dunn said that after he arrested Mrs. Houghton he found a number of instruments. When he charged Mrs. Houghton, she said, " I don't remember the name. Most likely I shall know the girl if I see</p>
        <p>Accused was committed for trial on the first charge bail beieg allowed.</p>
        <p>CUNNING THIEF SENTENCED. At St. Hahne, on Saturday, Patrick Blaney (25) of Sutton Heath-road, pleaded builty to two offences, under the Prevention of Crimes Act. It was stated that at ten o'clock on Friday night, the 4th inst. Mn. Hartley, of the Bridge Hotel. Liverpool-road, heard a noise of somebody moving about on the roof of a shed at the back of the premises. A policeman was informed At 11.50 Mrs Hartley said that she believed that she saw the shadow of a man crounhing near one of the bedroom windows. Constable Taylor obtained a ladder, and found prisoner on the shed fifteen and a half feet from the ground, with bin clogs and stockings off. —Prisoner said be got on to the shed in order to get into the next yard to sleep out. Ile had been out of work and could not go home. A list of ten convictions was proved against him—The Chief Constable said be was one of the most cunning thieves that he had dealt with.—Prisoner made a statement to the Bench that be had been trying to get work ; hut Inspector Dunn odd that be had found him work three times, and the Court Missionary had done the some, but he would not go straight.—The magistrates said his was a very bad record for a young man, and was a standing danger to the oommonity whenever ha was at liberty, and he would have to go to prison for the full term of twelve months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>MR. J. A. SEDDON AND THE LIVERPOOL RIOT. Mr. .1. A. Seddon, ex-M.P. for Newton, was a witness of the "Rod Sunday" riots in Liverpool. He described the scene in front of St. George's Hall as follows : I had been addressing the meeting at platform No. 2. Having bad many years' experience of public meetings, both in quiet and exciting times, I never addressed a more orderly crowd. Owing to the excessive beat, I divested myself of my coat. A few minutes after finishing I rose from the seat to resume my coat. In consequence of the noise of the Increasing uproar at the scene of the disturbance I turned my eyes towards Lord Nelson-street, and I saw one of the demonstrators on some sort of elevation addressing a crowd. His words seemed to be exercising a pacifying effect, sod while he was so engaged, the police on foot, came down and made a sudden baton charge, striking at men, women and children. This had the effect of clearing Lime-street, and then the police crossed over to the Plateau and began driving the crowd from Platform 3 towards St. John's- lane end, and in doing so they ur mercifully used their batons upon the audience, standing near to any plat- form. The police even weet the length of striking • woman who had olready bees injured. The peaceful intention of the audience was seen by the feet that not a single mhn lathe crowd at the front of Platform 2 had any weapon except here and there an ordinary walking-stick such as old ones carry. lam assured that the andience at the other platforms were also quite orderly. Having seen many labour troubles during the loot fifteen years, I have never seen a more unprovoked and brutal attack made upon a meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>Only a mill-girl."</p>
        <p>A Lancashire play of great human interest entitled "Only a mill-girl" has been presented with great success at the popular Rylands-street House this weak. As the title suggests, the drama has for its chief theme the life of cotton operatives in the north, but it is varied by some interesting scenes in the metropolis. Several of the performers make brave efforts to speak the old dialect of the county palatine, but they cannot help occasionally lapsing into conventional speech. The action of the piece is never allowed to flag and and those who like a novel stage spectacle find some- thing' to their lit.ing in the sensational race between the midnight mail and the motor car, ending, of course, in a victory for the car and the arrest of the fugitives from justice. Ihe scene which appeared to give most pleasure to the audience was that in which the looms were shown at work in the weaving shed. The play is from the pen of Miss Sheila Walsh, who herself takes the thankless part of the jealous and jut, igning Constance Daryl'. She is a clever and refined actress, and, without over-doing the part, invests it with splendid realism. Miss Elsie Shelton as Mary Raymond is a charming heroine and infuses a pathos into her characteliz dion which creates a deep impression. Mr. Harry Foxwell gives a remarkably vivid character study as John Raymond, the old overlooker, who has troubles enough to daunt most wen, but never loses his sense of honour and independence. Mr. Foxwell made the part live, his acting being so thoroughly natural and t ffecttve as to hold the audi- ence spellbound. Mr. Leonard Harrison impersona'es George Thornton, the villain of the peace, very cleverly, while Mr. Dent is Renton is a popular Jack Aynsleigh. Excellent comedy is provided by Mr. Cecil H. Raymond as Horace Edgar Shuttleworth, the would-be Sherlock Holmes, and Miss Maud Ryder as the irrepressible Martha Ellen Sampson. As usual, the band under Mr. Tom Bcothroyd's baton, plays some capital selections between the acts.</p>
        <p>The members of one of "The Chemists Soldier" touring companies found at Seacombe station that no one would move their scenery from the station, and were compelled to take off their coats and do the work themselves.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>HUSBAND AND WIFE.•</head>
        <p>HUSBAND AND WIFE.•</p>
        <p>THE WIFE'S FURNITURE.</p>
        <p>It happens not seldom that the wife has money and the husband has none, and in such a case the lady will furniah the house. Ibis is done very often, and there have been oases where the husband has run into debt, and a bailiff ha. walked into the house and seized all the wife's goods and chattels. Then the wife has made a claim for the goods, and a County-court judge hats tried the case to find out whether the furniture was hers or no, And he has more frequently decided against her than for her.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>WIFE'S MISTAKE.</head>
        <p>A WIFE'S MISTAKE.</p>
        <p>You naturally ask, Why is this The answer is short enonfrh. It is because, in the first place, when a man and wife are !iving together, the furniture in their house is supposed to belong to the husband. It is for the wife to prove that it is hers. In a case of this kind not very long ago, the wife claimed all the furniture, saying it was hero. She had /SOO when she was married, and the husband had nothing except about ..t5 at his bank. She handed him over the whole .2500, snd then they went to the furniture shops, bought -Ilerniture, and the husband paid for it by cheques on his banking account. The bills were made out to "Mr. Nihil." It need hardly be said, perhaps, that the husband's creditors took all the furniture, and the wife kept nothing. What she ought to have done was this: She ought to have kept the money in a bank in her own name. And, above all, she ought to have had the bills made out to "Mrs. Nihil." When the wife pays for the furniture, unless she intends- to make it liable for her husband's debts, she ought to pay for it herself personally, have the bills and receipts made out to her, and keep these bills and receipts. If she omits these pre- cautions, she runs a heavy risk of losing what she has bought.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>MARRYING THE WIFE'S DEBTS.</head>
        <p>MARRYING THE WIFE'S DEBTS.</p>
        <p>It sounds strange to talk of "marrying the wife's debts." Yet such a thing existed once. Before the Married Woman's Property Act, 18, a husband and wife were one, and the husband was that one. Consequently, if the bride owed anything to anybody before she was married, the unfortunate husband had to pay the piper. He had taken her "for better or for worse," and this was the "worse." Women in years gone by get rid of all their debts by marrying bankrupts im- prisoned in the Fleet Prison for debt. These men were in nowise particular. They knew they were in for life or thereabouts. They knew they already owed far more than they could pay; and it did not make one atom of difference to them that they owed a few hun- dreds or a few thousands more. The "wife" paid the "husband " a pound or two for the protection of his name, and thenceforth could snip her fingers in her creditors' faces, and gaily accumulate more debts. But now that imprisonment for debts has been abolished, except to a slight extent, this farce has ceased to be played. In these days the horror of marrying his fair one's debts need not weigh too heavily on the mind of coy bachelor who is contemplating matri- mony. He will only be liable to the extent of the property which the wife brings him: that is to say, if the lady, while yet single, owes £l,OOO, and she brings to her husband a little fortune of, say, £5OO, he will have to pay her creditors to the tune of £5OO, but no more.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>"KEEP OUT OF MY HOUSE!"</head>
        <p>"KEEP OUT OF MY HOUSE!"</p>
        <p>This is what an English lady said to her husband one fine day when he ventured to intrude into her home. The house was hers. She paid the rent, rates and taxes, and she had told her husband she ain't want him Any more. But he longed for her society, s.nd he went so far as to walk into her domicile without knocking. Then die ordered him out, and, as he "would not go, had him turned out by Use servants. Moreover. she brought an action against him for trespass, and lie had to pay damages and was for- bidden by the Court to do it again. Need- less to say, this occurred after the Married Women's Property Act. In Scotland this could not have happened, for in that happy country a husband, however downtrodden, has the right to go wherever his wife goes. If she Las a house of her own, he is entitled to live in it. though no doubt, if she has a will of her own (and there have been such ....sal ha will not be excessively comfortable).</p>
        <p>Many hazy notions exist both among mis- tresses and maids as to the notice that must be given to put an end to the service. The law on such an important topic is here explained, so that all readers may be quite clear about it in the future. When you, Mrs. B, hire a general servant, for how long do you hire her? And when you, Mary, con- tract to go to Mrs. B's, for how long do you let yourself to hire? Perhaps it never struck either of you. You never thought about it. You bad a hazy bort of notion, perhaps, that Mrs. B can get rid of Mary by giving her notice, and that Mary can put an end to the contract by giving Mrs. B notice. But that does not affect the question, which is : When the agreement was made, how long was it for? The answer will surprise many. The agreement, unless something appears to the contrary, is for one year—subject to the fact that either party can terminate it be- fore that year is up by giving a month's notice. When the year is up the service done, and the engagement at an end, so that the seitvant may leave, or the mistress may tell her to go without notice. It is just the same as if you hire a horse for a year, subject to returning it on giving a month's notice. By giving notice you can put an end to the hiring at any time, but at the end of the year it expires of itself.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>NOTICE AND WAGES.</head>
        <p>NOTICE AND WAGES.</p>
        <p>There is one superstition it is desirable to remove. An inquiry from twenty mistresses and an equal number of servants elicited the fact that all believed that the servant or mistress must give her notice on the day when the month's wages fall due. Thus, if you hire Jane as your housemaid and she comes on January 1, her first month's wages are due on the 28th of that month (four weeks). If in the first week of her service you find her to be unsuitable for the place, it is supposed that you must give her notice on the 28th, that Is, when you pay the wages. This notion is entirely erroneous. You can give the girl notice any day you like, and she has equal liberty to give you warning any day she likes. The date of pay- ment of wages has nothing at all to do with it. The same rule applies to all menial ser- vants, but not to others. It is difficult to say what Jeind of notice you ought to give your governess, for instance, or how much notice she is compelled to give you if she does not wish to remain. The governess is engaged by the year unless it is otherwise agreed at the time when she is hired. So if you have a private tutor for your son, he is engaged for a year unless otherwise agreed. lln these cases, as a rule, three months' notice is required; but this, again, may he varied by an agreement made beforehand. The best thing is to make an express stipu• lation as to notice at the time the engage- , ment is entered into.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <p>S • •</p>
        <p>THE SCOTCH LAW.</p>
        <p>In Scotland the law is different. Unless a servant is hired for a time specially agreed on, he or she is. understood to be en- gaged for six months. As in England, how- ever, the contract may be put an end to at any time by giving notice on either side. The notice required by the Scots law is forty days, and not, as in England, a month. It should be observed, however, that a farm- servant is not a menial or domestic servant, and be or she is hired for on ;:ear, with the option of giving forty (Jaye notice at any time. BrAh in 'hngland and Acotland there are plenty of good mistresses whom girls do not wish to leave. and plenty of honest, hardworking servants whom mistresses do not. want to part with. If the hiring is only for a limited time—a year in one country 'yid six months in the other—bow does the :ape stand when a ic,rvant does not wish tc</p>
        <p>-gave at the expirtGion or that rimer ' The answer is simple. By the mere fact of the /errant remaining after her twelve or six nonth3, and the fact of the mistress not lelling her to go, a fresh hiring is made for another period of the same length as the first, and on the came conditions. Thus, if you, living in London, -ennage a cook in January, 1905, and do not specify any par- ticular time of service—and everybody knows how unusual it is to say, "I engage you for a year;" one generally says, "I en- gage you at £2O a year "—she is understood to come into your service for a year. She being a good cook, January, 1506, find her stin in your kitchen. Neither of the parties say anything, but she stays. It is just as though you had gone to her and made.. a fresh contract, saying, "I am willing to en. gaga you again at the same wages you aro now receiving."</p>
        <p>TIIE SANITARY ASPECT OF THE DROUGHT. When the inevitable rain-storm brings the long- continued drought to an end, certain difficult problems of !imitation will have to be solved, or, if they are not an outbreak of fever is almost certain. During the dry weather that universal dr anaer—water—has to a great extent diaappeared, and dead animals, ripe fruit and other products have been allowed to rot in neglected corners. We have teen particularly struck with the conditions of many cattle-yards end pig-sties which closely adjoin farmhouses. They are at prevent as dry as tinder, but the materials for putrefaction have been steadily accumulating and rain is practically certain to make them sources of danger. Every medical authority knows the dangers that arise when drain- pipes that have accumulated filth during a long spell of dry weather are suddenly flushed. District and rural 'councils would be well ndvised to consider the situation in time, and with the advice of the medical officers of health take such steps no may be considered locally advisable towards reducing the possibilities of con- tagion. Water, it must be rernenberml, is a potent carrier of germs, and as are the flies.—" Country Life."</p>
        <p>BRUNNER, IMOND, AND COMPANY'S ACTION. VOLUNTARY INCREASE OF WAGES.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday night Messrs. Brunner, Mond and Company, chemical mannfacturers, Northwicb, Silverton &amp;c., who were pioneers in the eight hours system, holidays with double pay, &amp;c , issued a notice of an intention to voluntarily increase the wages of men on shift-work by 3d. per shift, week-end work beteg paid for as time end a quarter. This means an advance of shout 3s. per week. Efficisnt day labourers en heavy work are to be increased from £l. to 225. weekly. The firm gave notice that euring transport troubles and the difficulty of obtaining raw materials the works may be put on short time.</p>
