      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <p>...•</p>
        <p>Railway Strike Causes Chaos.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>Stations Deserted--Trade Paralysed.</head>
        <p>Stations Deserted--Trade Paralysed.</p>
        <p>Situation at Warrington Fullu Described.</p>
        <p>The strike war was declared by the railway workers on Thursday night, and to-day the country is in the throes of a general strike. A situation such as this country has not known within living memory is thus actually upon us. Not even a great war in its earlier stages could bring about a condition of affairs as is threatened by the fact of a general strike on the railways, backed up as it possibly may be by a renewed strike on the part of the transport workers. It means that the food supply of the great towns in which the vast majority of the nation is gathered together depends upon such few trains as the companies may be able to run under the protection of the forces of the Crown, and upon the possibilities of moving goods, also under protection, from the railway stations to the local centres of distribution. There is one ray of hope in the situation. On Thursday night at the House of Commons, after the men's leaders had definitely refused the offer made by Mr. Asquith of a Royal Commission to con- sider their grievances, and had issued thou- sands of telegrams calling on the men to cease work, Mr. Lloyd George met members Commission had no intention of suggesting a of the Labour Party and explained to them, on behalf of Mr. Asquith, that the Govern- ment in proposing the appointment of a Royal Royal Commission as generally understood, but a small ad hoc commission of three members, who would, without undue delay, inquire into the working of the Conciliation Board Act of Mr. Lloyd George. This explanation resulted in further negotiations taking place on behalf of the railwaymen's Executives, and late at night the leaders were in conference with Mr. Ramsay MacDonald, the chairman of the Labour Party, at Unity House, the London headquarters of the A.S.R.S. Mr. Buxton, the President of the Board of Trade, was in readiness to reopen negotiations with the men. The conference sat until 1 a.m., when it adjourned until nine o'clock yesterday with- out any decision having been arrived at.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>STRIKE SIGNAL.</head>
        <p>THE STRIKE SIGNAL.</p>
        <p>Men Leave Work on its Arrival.</p>
        <p>At Unity House, Euston Road, the head- quarters of the Amalgamated Society of Railway Servants, later in the evening, Mr. Thomas stated that subsequent to the meet- ing at the House of Commons, telegrams in the following terms. signed by the four general secretaries, had been sent to every part of the Kingdom: Your liberty at stake. All railwaymen must strike at once. Loyalty to each other means victory. (Signed) Williams, Fox, Louth, Chorlton.</p>
        <p>"Two thousand of these telegrams," con- cluded Mr. Thomas, "were sent off from 4.30 onwards." Almost everywhere the signal is acted upon at once, and railway men "came out" en bloc. By morning the railway service all over the country was suspended, and only a few mail trains were got through with great difficulty.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>LEADERS' STATEMENT OF MEN'S POSITION.</head>
        <p>LEADERS' STATEMENT OF MEN'S POSITION.</p>
        <p>"WORSE THAN SLAVES." Call tor a " United Blow tor Deliverance."</p>
        <p>The following manifesto was issued at the head- quarters of the Amalgamated Society of Railway Servants in the name of the four railway Onions just after midnight on Thursday : Fellow Workers,— Nearly four years ago an agreement was entered into between the Board of Trade, he representa- tives of the railway companies and your organiza- tions, which provided machinery for the purpose of dealing with disputes regarding rates of wages and</p>
        <p>hOurs of labour.' - Your organizations made every effort loyally to vary out both the spirit and the letter if the agree- ment they lad entered into on your behalf. From the commenct meat the comranies showed they were not prepared to carry out their share of the bargain by delaying in every way the formation of the Boards, so much so that in the case of one company Boards were not formed until April, IWO. After the Boards were formed every obstacle the ingenuity of the companies could suggest was placed in the say of the men's claims being considered sad reiressed. After considerable delay, when agreements had been arrived at, the companies applied flu ir own interpretation thereto, the signatories on behalf of the men being rt fused any voice in this all- important matter. Whether in revenge for having to meet the men or not we do not know, but coincident with the settlement a policy of harassing and driving the men was instituted, until to-day railwaymen are worse than slaves. .</p>
        <p>All these circumatances, which are well-known to railwaymen and can be amplified from their practical esperience, caused outbreaks at various centres. Even then had the companies met the representatives of the men the present position would have been saved.</p>
        <p>The compsoies arrogantly refused this, and by la". doing denied the railway workers of this country what has been etnceded to every other class of workers. . .</p>
        <p>As the men throughout the United Kinpdom hare shown their unchangeable determination not to tolerate this state of affairs any longer, we call upon every railwayman to join his fellows, and no strike a united blow for deliverance from petty tyranny, and also help to obtain higher wages, shorter hours, and a more human life for all.</p>
        <p>On behalf Of the Joint Executive Committees. J. E. Williams, Gen. Sec. A.S.R.S. A. Fox, Gen. Sec. A.B.L.E end F. T. Lowth, Cho. Sec. G.R.W.C. S. Chorltoo, Gen. Sec. C.P. and S.S. London, Aug. 17th, 1911.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>Warrington's Isolation.</head>
        <p>Warrington's Isolation.</p>
        <p>RAILWAYS &amp; WORKS CLOSE DOWN</p>
        <p>Warrington was one of the first towns to feel the paralysing effects of the national railway strike which was declared on Thursday night. All through the week the situation had been growing more acute, bodies of railway workers of different grades striking in batches, and it was realized that ' even if the trouble did not assume the gravest proportions, the trade of Warrington woul4 be seriously jeopardised. But the heaviest blow of all, which it had been fervently hoped would be staved off, fell yesterday. When Warringtonians ventured abroad in the early morning it was to find that railway communication with the out- side world had been almost entirely cut off. During the night practically the whole of the railwaymen who had not already left work, came out on strike in response to a message from their leaders. Porters (passenger and goods), engine drivers, guards, signalmen, shunters, ticket collectors. platelayers—all but a small remnant had obeyed the call to join the ranks of the strikers, who in Warrington must now number not less than 500. Businessmen whose work takes them to Manchester and Liverpool each day found that the train service from Central Station had been entirely suspended. Since eleven o'clock on Thursday night only two trains have passed through the station and the inconveni- ence to the public who have become accustomed to rely upon this prompt hourly service may be readily imagined. On Thursday it was announced that no more tickets to Manchester and Stockport districts would be issued until further notice, but the trains continued to run to Liverpool, though somewhat behind time, until the general strike on Thursday night brought the service to an abrupt close. Yesterday the station had a deserted appearance, work being entirely at a standstill, and the sound of an engine whistle would have been sweet music to many people who found themselves "held up" in the town. A number of trains, chiefly dealing with local traffic, had come to a stop at Warrington, and as it was impossible to work them further, the drivers were stranded here. The stationmaster (Mr Derbyshire) saw that they were provided with food, and their relatives were wired as to their whereabouts. One woman from another town who had made a vain effort to get to Stockport was obliged to spend the, night in the waiting-room, but how she was 1 to reach her destination at all yesterday was , a difficult problem. At Bank Quay station the position was little better. Practically all the men had ceased work and trains were few and far between. The ticket office was besieged by anxious inquirers to whom the invariable response was " I don't know " (which in the circum- stances, was quite true). Up to yesterday afternoon about a dozen passetger trains had come through—an infinitesimal number as ' compared with the normal volume of traffic. I There appeared to be no certainty as to the place from which a train bad , come or where it was bound until it actually arrived in the station. Then one of thefew remaining porters would convey the news to the booking office that "the train for so and so has come in "and tickets would be issued to those who wished</p>
        <p>to undertake the journey. Early in the morning all the windows of the station were boarded up, apparently to guard against any fear of attack, but up to the present there has been no suspicion of rioting or attempt at damaging the Company's property. The strikers appear to be content quietly to await developments. Goods traffic at both stations was, of course, entirely suspended and the</p>
        <p>inconvenience and loss to lacal traders must already be serious. Many waggon loads of local manufactures lie on the sidings waiting to be conveyed away and the congestion at some works, combined with a shortage of raw material, has become so serious as to necessitate closing down. The effect on the local labour world has reached a very grave point, and thousands of workmen will to-day be walking the streets unemployed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>WORKS CLOSING DOWN</head>
        <p>WORKS CLOSING DOWN</p>
        <p>The Dallam and Bewsey forges cf the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd., were closed down on Thursday night, owing to the difficulty in obtaining supplies and the impossibility of dispatching com- pleted orders. Two thousand men were thus thrown idle. Messrs. Rylands Bros. Ltd., wire manufacturers, followed suit last evening, and the stoppage of their works will affect from 1,500 to 1,800 men. Messrs. W. D. Houghton and Co., Sankey Wire Mills, were obliged to close on Wednesday evening, the number of their workmen who have thus temporarily joined the ranks of the unemployed being 350. Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Son, Ltd., and Messrs. Monks Hall and Co., Ltd., are still in a position to keep their works going. It is, however, obvious that if the strike continues long most of the workpeople of Warrington will be idle. The Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Co., Ltd., have practically had to close of their Latchford foundry and full time is not being worked at Academy-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>PUBLIC SERVICES.</head>
        <p>THE PUBLIC SERVICES.</p>
        <p>The plunging of part of Liverpool into darkness through the failure of the electricity supply on Thursday night gave rise to fears that Warrington might soon have to suffer similar inconvenience. We are, however, glad to be able to inform our readers that there is sufficient fuel in stock at the electricity works to maintain the supply for a fortnight. There is also no risk of the borough water supplies failing before the expiration of that period.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>MEETING AT WARRINGTON LAST NIGHT.</head>
        <p>MEETING AT WARRINGTON LAST NIGHT.</p>
        <p>RAILWAY MEN S FIRM ATTITUDE</p>
        <p>An enthusiastic meeting of the various Railway unions was held in Friars' Green schoolroom last evening, presided over by Mr. Walter Taylor, secretary of the A.S.L.E. and F. There were quite 400 men present besides a large number of rail way workers who were unable to get into the building. A resolution was passed that the men stand by the Executive Committee's , decision not to return to work until recogni- tion by the managers is assured. It was also decided that all men must be reinstated into their former positions or none of them would resume duty. A motion that all of the strikers must assist the authorities in maintaining order in the town, was agreed to with great enthusiasm.</p>
        <p>BARRICADING BANK QU</p>
        <p>I hoto, Tonge. .Y STATION YESTERDAY.</p>
        <p>~..,, 0.. .... , . i 1 .1. , '•,, CS , # • . • . I. .1 , •:., , t • "' -.!.':'•''' 'oi oil ~,,.. ,4 " .E.. • . 1. '... ' ' 1 i i 4.; ..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>WARRINGTON MEN'S DEMANDS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON MEN'S DEMANDS.</p>
        <p>STATEMENT FROM STRIKE COMMITTEE</p>
        <p>£1 Per Week for Porters.</p>
        <p>[TO THE EDITOR "EXAMINER."' From Strike Committee Railway Societies,</p>
        <p>Warrington,</p>
        <p>Sir, Please allow us a small space in your valuable paper to expound our appreciation to the general public. of Warrington for the peaceful and sympathetic manner in which they have assisted us during our extensive trials and troubles as a Strike Committee of the C.L.C., L. &amp; N.-W., and G.W. strikes in Warrington, especially mentioning the I.L.P. and Mr. Plinston for their generosity in providing us with excellent accommoda- tion for the purpose of holding meetings. There is no doubt many of the general public are not conversant or properly acquainted with the exact claims were put forward and which have resulted in a national railway strike. First of all we may mention that we have worked in conjunction with Manchester and I Liverpool and we asked our stationmaster to forward an application from us to head- quarters, Liverpool, asking for an advance in wages averaging at the rate of about 2s. per head per man; also a reduction in working hours from 60 hours per week to 54 hours; a guaranteed day; no man to book on more than once for one day's pay; no extra men to be stopped for slackness after 12 months continual service; no man to work more than 4 hours per day overtime: overtime to be paid at the rate of time and a quarter for week days and time and a half for Sunday; other superior ranks and occupations a rise in wages of similar proportion ; also for the abolition of the Conciliation Board. We ask for a minimum wage of El per week for porters reaching a maximum wage of 235., maximum to be reached by yearly advance of Is. per annum. We also desire to add that the Warrington Police have aided us splendidly with regard to our pickets. We have been harassed tremendously by a certain cowpany's agent out of pure bravado on his *IA and his actions have caused great indignation amongst our multifarious friends.</p>
        <p>I am, sir, Faithfully yours, WRIGHT MUSICETT. Chairman Strike Committee. Aug. 18t1i. 1911. HOW THE STRIKE BEGAN.</p>
        <p>Warrington first became really embroiled in the strike on Monday afterncrm. I. p to then the only tffect had been the curtailing of kcal supplies and the difficulty sending out go do, owing to the disloca- tion of traffic at various strike centres on the railway system. The railway employees of Warrington had, however, not given their ac.ive allegiance to the strike movemeat. On Sunday, however, many of the workers connected with the Cheshire Lines, Great Northern, Great Central and Midland systems deter- mined to strike. The first indication of revolt was the holding of a meeting of rsilway workers at Bridge Foot. where goods warehousemen, passenger porters, leaders, and shuniers, accompanied by carters assembled in large numbers ma Sunday afternoon. At this gathering it vas decided to demand an increase of 2s. weekly, with a fifty-four boars instead of a sixty hours week, time and a half for Sunday duty. and time and a quarter for ordinary overtime. It is stated that • deputation which was appointed to await upon the authorities at Liverpcol. faced to obtain an</p>
        <p>interview sod notice was accordingly given of the intention to leave work._</p>
        <p>When the prospect of a strike was noised abroad tmdeemen wisely took steps to secure the immediate delivery of heavy consignments of goods, and Mrries were passing in and cut of the Central Station goods yard throughout Monday morning and afternoon. Five o'clock was the hour fixed fcr the beginning of the strike. Immediately after the 455 express ttain to Manchester had departed, a signal was g.iven and the porters at once lat the platform. They marched to the goods 3ard where they were joined by the goods porter* at d labourers who had also resolved to join the strikers. All the men having handed in their checks they marched in a body dwell the slope to the street below where they were cheered by a large crowd. The number of men who came out on Monday was about 1(10. They acted, we are informed. entirely on their own responsibility and therefore did not become entitled to union per The strikers held a meeting at the head- quarters, the Bay horse Inn, and arrangements for picketirg the st,tion were promptly made. All the week men have been on duty at the goods yard gate and have endeavoured by peaceful persuasion, to induce drivees of carts not to remove goods to and from the station. We understand, however, that an exception has been made in the case of foodstuffs. An unusual quietness has pervaded Central Station since the strike began, the withdrawal of the staff having left the platform practically deserted. The Stationmaster (Mr. Derbyshire) and ore or two of the chief officials did their utmost to cope with the traffic which ran very disjointedly until Thursday night when the general strike pat attend to operations. Tuesday witnessed a development of the strike, the L. &amp; N.W. Railway being involved in the dispute. The strikers from the Central Station marched to Bank Quay goods lard shout noon to give encouragement to members of the goods staff whom it was anticipated would throw in their lot with the dissatisfied workers. A large crowd watched the proceedings with great inteiest but there was no disorder. When a numberl of warehousemen were seen coming up the slope. pickets raised cries of" Come on lads' and amid cheers the new recruits to the strike movement handed in their checks. By Tuesday evening about sixty goods porters, checkers, uarehousemen and others had struck and the gates of the goods yaAl were closed.</p>
        <p>Although the strike might have been expected to arum's feeling to fever heat the state of affairs has, on the whole, been remarkably peaceful. Pickets tave been pcs'ed at the gates of each goods yard, and driver', have been prevailed upon to turn back, but there has apparently teen nothing in the way of forcible interference. The police force, under the direction of Mr. M. Nicholls, was on Tuesday reinforced by the return of forty cor.stable, who had been drafted to Liveipool, aid t ey had no difficulty in preserving order.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>PROVISION PRICES RISING</head>
        <p>PROVISION PRICES RISING</p>
        <p>In conversation with a member of one of the leading local firms of grocers and provision dealers yesterday, a representative of the " Examiner " .was informed that the past week had been an exceptionally busy one. In anticipation of famine price., the public had been buying in to an extent almost equalling the rush of Christmas week, Sides of bacon, chests of tea, and quarter-loads of flour have been disposed of to householders who usually purchase in much smaller quantities. Practically all the groceries and provisions which have been allowed to enter Warrington this week has been brought in passenger trains. .</p>
        <p>The strike has already caused a consider- able advance in prices and should the trouble continue, it would be difficult to forecast what amount the housewife may be called upon to pay for necessaries. Best Danish butter has risen from 120 s to 150 s per cwt., making the retail price Is. 4d. per lb.—an advance of 2d. during the week. Bacon has risen by 3d. per lb., the present retail prices being is. for plain Wiltshire and is. 2d. for smoked. A fortnight ago sugar was Is. 9d. per dozen lbs., now it is 2s. 6d. Ham, the average price of which is 9d. per lb, is now being sold at is to is. 2d.</p>
        <p>The prices of meat have not yet been greatly affected.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>WARRINGTON AS A MILITARY CENTRE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AS A MILITARY CENTRE.</p>
        <p>A flutter of excitement was caused on Thursday morning by the arrival at War- rington of detachments of troops in prepara- tion for strike duty. The convenience of Warrington as a mobilising centre has been recognized by the authorities and strong contingents of men from several well-known regiments have been ordered to take up their quarters here in readiness to proceed to any town where their services may be required. On Thursday morning a section of the 18th Hussars,with full camping equip- ment, and 336 men and 15 officers of the 2nd Battalion Lincolnshire Regimert arrived at Bank Quay Station and marched to Orford, Barracks. The Hussars have since been encamped in a field near to the Barracks and the infantry are accommodated inside. Some of the Lincolnshires were drafted to Stockport later in the day. Yesterday morning the troops were supplemented by about 300 of the King's Own Rifles, but they were not in Warrington long, as they received orders to proceed to Burnley. During the past few days Warringtonians have witnessed the unusual sight of mounted soldiers, fully armed, conveying their baggage from the station to the camp.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>PETROL FAMINE.</head>
        <p>PETROL FAMINE.</p>
        <p>The dislocation of railway traffic has brought motor traction into abnormal requisition, and the consequence has been a rise in the price of petrol. In Warrington the advance during the week was from Is. 2d. to Is. per gallon. but in Li.verpool so highs price as ss. per gallon has been reached.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>CARTERS CONTINUE TO WORK.</head>
        <p>CARTERS CONTINUE TO WORK.</p>
        <p>During the week it was freely rumoured that the carters intended to join the strikers. On Wednesday evening, however, the members of the local Carters' Union held a 'meeting and decided to continue their work and formulate any grievances they might have in the usual way. Some of the men, hewever, have had little or no opportunity of following their employment, in conse- quence of the dislocation of trade. Last night, however, the feeling in favour of a strike was rapidly gaining gronud and a meeting will be held to-night.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>C.L.C. AND THE STRIKERS.</head>
        <p>THE C.L.C. AND THE STRIKERS.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday the following notice was posted at the Central Station : "The wages of the men who have, without notice, ceased work at this station and refused to return thereto will be paid on the usual pay-day but no money will be paid to any man on either Thursday or Friday this week until the committee's property—rule books, uniform, clothing, etc.—has been handed in." . _ .</p>
        <p>The men have refused to apply for the pay under the above conditions. " VICTORY ASSURED."</p>
        <p>The engineers who are on strike yesterday received the following telegram from head- quarters: "Excellent response. Victory assured."</p>
        <p>LABOUR COUNCIL AND THE STRIKE The Trades and Labour Council has passed the following resolution : "That this Council protests against the military and local police force, being used as strike breakers, and further points out that we cannot safely reduce the Force by about 40 men, or otherwise we are over staffed, which means over rated." ADVANCE TO P. &amp; K. LABOURERS.</p>
        <p>The Pearson and Knowles Engineering department, in conformity with an arrange- ment entered into some years ago with their men to follow changes in the rate of wages paid in the Manchester district,have conceded the advances of 2s. per week to engineers' labourers recently granted by the Man- chester firms. RAILWAY CLERKS' ATTITUDE.</p>
        <p>A crowded and enthusiastic meeting of members of the Railway Clerks' Association wu held at the Winmarleigh Cute on Monday evening when the follow- ing resolution was passed t—This meeting resolves that during the labour unrest amongst the outside grades in the Railway Service, to maintain a strictly neutral Position, performing our own clerical duties faithfully but on no account to perform the duties of the men who have withdrawn their labour.</p>
        <p>LIVERPOOL AND NORTH WALES STEAMERS. We have been asked to announce that this service has been tempersrily suspended, ()swim, to the dis- location of traffic caused by the strike.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>CRUISER AT DOUGLAS.</head>
        <p>CRUISER AT DOUGLAS.</p>
        <p>Cross-Channel Boats Running as Usual.</p>
        <p>The first-class cruiser Warrior anchored in Douglas Bay this morning at seven o'clock close inshore.</p>
        <p>The Viking left, as usual, at 8.39 for Fleet- wood, and other boats are ready to leave as usual with great crowds of holiday-makers on board.</p>
        <p>FRUIT TRADE LOSSES.</p>
        <p>THOUSANDS OF PACKAGES ROTTING The continued suspension of the announce- ment of sales at the rooms of the Liverpool Fruit Trade Association must have sufficiently indicated to all in the trade the serious losses which are being inflicted owing to the degree of transport deadlock. No trade is suffering more heavily and irretrievably. Many thousands of cases of fruit are deteriorating at the docks, and vessels bringing fruit are being diverted to other ports. So far, the fruit trade has found no means of improving the lamentable situation, or of transferring to the market the splendid stocks lying within reach. *** We regret that owing to disorganised mail service two of our pictures of the soldiers at Warrington have gone astray.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>WEEK'S HOBBY.</head>
        <p>THE WEEK'S HOBBY.</p>
        <p>FIREGUARDS AND CINDER</p>
        <p>CATCHERS.</p>
        <p>Fireguards are a necessity in every home where children are found. How to make them and also cinder catchers are explained below. An easily made fireguard is shown in Fig. 1. Some half-round strip iron about 1 in. wide is required for the top bar, fiat strip about in. thick and 1 in. wide for the bottom, and for the upright bars rod iron I in. thick, or it may be thicker or thinner as desired.</p>
        <p>The height, width, etc., are not given, as all guards should be made to fit the different fireplaces as required. But it will be found in practice that the ends of the guard should be long enough to keep the guard well away from the fire; 16 in. should be the minimum length of the ends. . . . . _</p>
        <p>fo make the guard (Fig. 1), cut off the re- quired lengths of iron for the top and the bottom, and to get the bends shown it WiLl be necessary to heat the iron. Having bent the top and bottom rims, they should be marked for drilling the holes for the rods. which should be about 3 in. apart. The beat</p>
        <p>way of ensuring the bars being parallel and true ia to first mark the top bar, drill, an? then mark and drill the bottom bar to it The holes in the bar must be countersunk on the outsides. co that when the uprights aro riveted in they can be filed off, and a smooth surface assured.</p>
