      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>FIXING TOGETHER.</head>
        <p>FIXING TOGETHER.</p>
        <p>The in. rod should next be out to the sixes required. They must be of the same Length, and the ends slightly tapered by filing to go through the bolts just far enough for riveting.</p>
        <p>To it the guard together, the following is an easy method: Rivet the two end bars Into the boitom, and then the centre bar. Tuns over and put on the top, and then put in the bars alternately. This will keep the guard aqua.re. . _ _</p>
        <p>'The guard may be polished (in which ea. the parts should he roughly polished before putting together). or painted any colour desired.</p>
        <p>The guard with a wire front ebown by Fig. 2 is very suitable for nurseries, etc. Not in mane uprights are required as for the flaw- guard sliown by Fig. I. the wire front being made on a frame, bent to shape, and tied on with lacing wire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>USEFUL CINDER CATCHER.</head>
        <p>A USEFUL CINDER CATCHER.</p>
        <p>Fig. 3 lIIAWS a wry useful and efficient cinder catenzr or guard. It is simple in con- struction.. and not only does it to a largo . .</p>
        <p>extent act as a preventive against fire, but also saves much fuel, as the narrow bars prevent the coal, etc., falling out. It consists of a wire par.ei, with two hooins at the top to hang on the bar, and it may be Lade out of straight wirework as shown in Fig. 4, with bars laced across', or corrugated work can be used: orefereoce. however. in</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>GLOBE</head>
        <p>GLOBE</p>
        <p>Furnishing Co.</p>
        <p>H 1, GRANT, PropTietor ) PEMBROKE Liverpcol PLAOE,</p>
        <p>generally gi:on to sir.lipt I‘oric. in, :Niro required should be No. 13 8.W.0. for the rim and No. 16 S.W.°. for the bars. For the mesh (that is, the distance between the bars) ;n nr 4 in. weitld be best where there are .I=4</p>
        <p>A GAME OF DARTS.</p>
        <p>For the target shown in our second illus- tration we must obtain a piece of wood half an inch in thickness and about a foot square. and it may be well sand-papered to make it smooth. Now draw a series of circles. If we possess a large pair of compasses the matter is quickly done; but we may not possess them, and then a good plan to draw the circles correctly will be to obtain, for the largest, a plate, perhaps, or some other circular article of a suitable size, and draw a line round it in pencil. A small basin may, answer the purpose of the second circle, at glass for the third, and a coin for the smallest. The rings thus formed must then be coloured and numbered. Two little brass rings, such as are used for hanging up small pictures, are then screwed into the upper edge of the board, and we have our target complete. Now to explain the manner in which the darts are made. A stick of wood,</p>
        <p>about the length of, but rather thicker than, an ordinary pencil, should be chosen. On, end should be split across and across shows this) for the distance of about an inch down, and into this paper wings are in- serted, made by taking a square of note- paper and folding it to a point in the centre and slipping it into the split stick, which will hold it quite tightly. For the point, the. stick must be split across once for the dis- tance of about an inch, and an ordinary stout pin inserted, until only about a quarter of an inch of it is left exposed. The cud of the stick must be then bound tightly round with strong cotton or fine string, and the dart is finished.. A number of darts should be made, and players should play in turn, those scoring the -highest numbers being the winners.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON SERVANT'S DISGRACE. PRISON FOR A THIEVING GIRL. At Douglas on Wednesday a servant girl, Emma. Barlow (19, slid to belong to Warrington, was, sentenced to three months' imprisonment for stealing £49 10s. from a boarding•honse where she was engaged. She was missed from the house last Tuesday altar- noon, and was arrested an hour later just as ste was boarding the Blrickpool boat. She had the mosey upon, her, less £2 for a new bat and mackintosh. The police were complimented on their smartness.</p>
        <p>DISC 11 A 11,, ED.-111 BM&amp;let Cole, no settled abode, was charged to remand with stealing a brooch, the property of some person unknown. The Chief Constable sail the cane had been remanded in order that the police might end the owner of the brooch. The prisoner stated that she had found the brooch on the rout to Runcorn. Sbe took it to a maid Its gentleman's house, in the vicinity, to whom she sold it for ed. The only information that the police could obtain hovr•ver, was that the brooch had been in a local pawnshop some eighteen months previously. Prisoner was discharged.</p>
        <p>THEiT or A JACKET.—John Price. • stranger. was charged on remand with stealing • youth's jacket value is., the property of Dol. Bros, 11,11orsemarket• street. He pleaded guilty. The Chief Constable said the jacket was hung outside the shop 111,17 in the morning and at i.:;‘) in the afternoon vs... to be safe by one of the assistants. Prisoner was found by P.C. Cooml v at the rear of Afrasra. Carter's. corn merchants, endeavouring to ieetroy the tap of the jacket which bore the Dame of the Arm. lie sun questioned and suited that he had bought tLe jacket in mencbeater. lie was taken into custody and admitted the theft. The Chief Constable said the prisoner seemed to hare come jct.° the town .1 straight away taken the coat. There were previous con. •ictions against tie prisoner at Liverpool, who was rentenced to or.. month's bard labour.</p>
        <p>The Ferret, the sew Brit irh twesty-osern-knot ,ctso- eoiog destroyer, attained a speed of thirty kro.4 an hour on an eight hours official spree' trial at Sheerness.</p>
